2 CONT ''' "[http://www.geni.com/people/Srul-Moishevich-Blank/6000000010019232673 Alexander Blank] had the theory that it was very beneficial for women to have many children''', but Anna Grosschopf gave up the ghost after her fifth. She 
2 CONC had one son and four daughters, her third daughter [http://www.geni.com/people/Maria-Alexandrovna-Blank/6000000010018910359 Mariya] being [http://www.geni.com/people/%D0%92%D0%BB%D0%B0%D0%B4%D0%B8%D0%BC%D0%B8%D1%80-%D0%9B%D0%B5%D0
2 CONC %BD%D0%B8%D0%BD-Vladimir-Lenin/6000000010018573170 Lenin]’s future mother. The son, [http://www.geni.com/people/Dmitry-Aleksandrovich-Blank/6000000010019176830 Dmitry], for a time studied in the faculty of law at Kazan University,
2 CONC  but committed suicide in 1850 at the age of nineteen. It fell to Anna Grosschopf ’s widowed sister [http://www.geni.com/people/Catherine-von-Essen/6000000010019347414 Ekaterina von Essen] to bring up the girls. She gave them an e
2 CONC ducation which went well beyond that which daughters of the nobility traditionally received. She taught them German, French and English besides giving them piano lessons. The seriousness with which these studies were undertaken is
2 CONC  indicated by the fact that in 1863 Mariya Blank was able to pass the examinations which qualified her as a teacher of Russian, French and German." (White 2001: 16)
2 CONT
