1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Бланк, Сруль Мошевич — Blank, Srul Moishevich'''
2 CONT
2 CONT *  * c. 1799/1801/1802/1804, various sources
2 CONT * ~ 1820-07-10. Бланк, Александр Дмитриевич — Blank, Aleksandr Dmitrievich
2 CONT * Ioo c. 1828
2 CONT * o-o 1842
2 CONT * † 1870-06-17, alt. 1870-07-29
2 CONT
2 CONT >''' '' ― ‘I'd like to arise from my grave in about a hundred years and have a look at how people will be living then.’ '' ''' Lenin's grandfather, Dr Alexander Blank (Service 2000, 11).
2 CONT
2 CONT '' Note: this person is somewhat contested. Alternative theories presented below.''
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Both Srul and his brother [http://www.geni.com/people/Abel-Moishevich-Blank/6000000010032179038 Abel] went to Saint Petersburg''' and were baptized into the Orthodox Christianity, hence '''Srul Moishevich Blank''' became '''Ale
2 CONC ksandr Dmitrievich Blank'''. The baptism took place in the Samson Cathedral, in Saint Petersburg's Vyborg district. The godfathers were senator Dmitry Baranov and the privy councillor, count Aleksandr Ivanovich Apraksin. From the 
2 CONC former they also got their new patronymic. This circumstance allowed them into the Saint Petersburg Medico-Surgical Academy in 1820, from which they graduated four years later. Aleksandr graduated 1824-07-19 and received final con
2 CONC firmation 1826-01-29.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Some alternatives to the mainstream theory exist.''' Apparently, there were at the time two different and unrelated "Aleksandr Dmitrievich Blank's" who both studied at the Saint Petersburg Academy of Medical Surgeons. According
2 CONC ly, both had remarkably similar intersts both professionaly and in social life etc., one of them, the converted Jew commoner, the other an ethnic German and nobility. According to this theory, Lenin's grandfather was fittingly eno
2 CONC ugh the latter Aleksandr Blank, so Lenin did not have "Jewish blood" after all. Whatever the contemporary reasons and arguments, this tends to be a "hotspot" that is often spotlighted and contested.
2 CONT
2 CONT >''' '' ―‘And in 1965, all archival documents relating to Lenin's Jewish grandfather were ordered ‘removed without leaving any copies.’ '' '''
2 CONT
2 CONT                       (Slezkine 2004, 338).
2 CONT
2 CONT '''With a diploma as surgeon-obstetrician''', he worked in the town of Porechye, Smolensk Governorate. Soon, he returned to Saint Petersburg and worked as a police medical doctor, then in the Naval Department; in 1837, he started 
2 CONC to work in Mariinsky Hospital. In 1842, he moved to Perm, then Zlatoust. In 1847, he retired from the practice of medicine and bought the estate of Kokushkino or Yañasala, now Lenino-Kokushkino, in Tatarstan—allegedly with 39 male
2 CONC  serfs—where he lived until his death in 1870. In 1887-1888, Vladimir Lenin was exiled to his grandfather's estate.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''At times he was''' a doctor for the Ukrainian poet Taras Hryhorovych Shevchenko. In 1837, in Saint Petersburg, he reportedly saved Shevchenko—then a young pupil of artist Shiryayev—from a dangerous illness. Later, in 1850s, dur
2 CONC ing his exile to Nizhny Novgorod, Shevchenko was afflicted with an "indecent illness out of his romance with actress Pekunova", most likely a sort of a sexually transmitted disease. Shevchenko sent for the retired doctor Aleksandr
2 CONC  Blank who was able to cure him.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Aleksandr married once, but tried twice'''. His first wife was [http://www.geni.com/people/Anna-Ivanovna-Grosschopff/6000000010019081631 Anna Ivanovna Grosschopff] (Анна Ивановна Гроссхопф). They had one son, [http://www.geni.c
2 CONC om/people/Dmitry-Aleksandrovich-Blank/6000000010019176830 Dmitry], who committed suicide at the age of 19 possibly because of a gambling debt and five daughters: [http://www.geni.com/people/Anna-Aleksandrovna-Blank/600000001001916
2 CONC 8010 Anna], [http://www.geni.com/people/Lyubov-Alexandrovna-Blank/6000000010019312734 Lyubov], [http://www.geni.com/people/Ekaterina-Alexandrovna-Blank/6000000010019480678 Yekaterina], [http://www.geni.com/people/Maria-Alexandrovn
2 CONC a-Blank/6000000010018910359 Maria] and [http://www.geni.com/people/Sophia-Alexandrovna-Blank/6000000010019448632 Sofia]. Each of the five daughters married a school teacher and left five to ten children. The fourth daughter, [http
2 CONC ://www.geni.com/people/Maria-Alexandrovna-Blank/6000000010018910359?through=6000000010019232673 Maria] married [http://www.geni.com/people/Ilya-Nikolayevich-Ulyanov/6000000010018994260 Ilya Ulyanov] and became mother of Vladimir L
2 CONC enin.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Anna Grosschopf's ancestors''' came from Northern Germany and that branch of the family produced many notable Germans that were discovered to be blood relatives of Vladimir Lenin. Among them are Nazi field marshal Otto Moritz W
2 CONC alter Model, German archeologist Ernst Curtius, President of Germany Richard von Weizsäcker and many others.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''In 1838''', Anna Grosschopff died and Aleksandr courted the widow of a government official of XII class, [http://www.geni.com/people/Catherine-von-Essen/6000000010019347414 Catherine von Essen] in 1842. This partnership was chi
2 CONC ldless.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''In May 1842''', Aleksandr, along with his children and his "wife", Catherine von Essen, moved to Perm. It was illegal to marry ones deceased wife's sister. Alexander applied for permisson to marry―without disclosing the sisters
2 CONC  birth names to the authorities―but was still denied. However, it did not stop the couple from staying under the same roof until Yekaterina's death in 1863.
2 CONT
2 CONT ''' "Alexander Blank took early retirement in 1847''' and, in respect of his standing in the medical hierarchy, was granted hereditary nobility some years later. Just before his retirement he bought the estate of Kokushkino near K
2 CONC azan. Blank’s social standing was enhanced considerably by this purchase, as he now entered the ranks of the landowning aristocracy and had become the owner of serfs, the traditional mark of wealth and substance in Russia. Unlike 
2 CONC many landowners, however, Blank treated the peasants on his estate with compassion and consideration, holding clinics for them in the manor house." (White 2001: 17)
2 CONT
2 CONT ------------------------------
2 CONT
