2 CONT ''' "Writing in 1927, Lenin’s sister''' [http://www.geni.com/people/Anna-Ilyinichna-Ulyanova/6000000010030450923 Anna] recalled that when she was four years old or thereabouts, in 1868 or 1869, her parents took her and her little 
2 CONC brother [http://www.geni.com/people/Aleksandr-Ilyich-Ulyanov/6000000010030785559 Alexander] on a steamer trip down the Volga from Nizhnii Novgorod to Astrakhan, the home of her father’s relations. She remembered the small two-stor
2 CONC ey house in which [http://www.geni.com/people/Anna-Alexeyevna-Smirnova/6000000010031154884 her grandmother] and her uncle [http://www.geni.com/people/Vasily-Ulyanov/6000000010031647977 Vasilii] lived, the warm welcome they receive
2 CONC d there, and the great fuss made over Alexander and herself. The trip stood out in Anna’s memory because it was the first and last visit ever paid by the Ulyanovs to the Astrakhan branch of the family. When Anna visited Astrakhan 
2 CONC her grandfather [http://www.geni.com/people/Nikolay-Vasilyevich-Ulyanov-%E2%86%90-Ulyanin/6000000010031431504 Nikolai Vasilevich Ulyanov] (1768–1836) had been dead for over thirty years. He had started life called not Ulyanov but 
2 CONC Ulyanin and as a serf in the village of Androsovo in the Nizhnii Novgorod province. It is possible that, while Nikolai belonged to the Orthodox faith, he might have been not a Russian but a [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mordvins M
2 CONC ordvinian] or a [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chuvash_people Chuvash]." (White 2001: 13)
2 CONT
2 CONT ------------------------------
2 CONT
