2 CONT ''' "Writing in 1927, Lenin’s sister''' Anna recalled that when she was four years old or thereabouts, in 1868 or 1869, her parents took her and her little brother [http://www.geni.com/people/Aleksandr-Ilyich-Ulyanov/6000000010030
2 CONC 785559 Alexander] on a steamer trip down the Volga from Nizhnii Novgorod to Astrakhan, the home of [http://www.geni.com/people/Ilya-Nikolayevich-Ulyanov/6000000010018994260 her father]’s relations. She remembered the small two-sto
2 CONC rey house in which [http://www.geni.com/people/Anna-Alexeyevna-Smirnova/6000000010031154884 her grandmother] and her uncle [http://www.geni.com/people/Vasily-Ulyanov/6000000010031647977 Vasilii] lived, the warm welcome they receiv
2 CONC ed there, and the great fuss made over Alexander and herself. The trip stood out in Anna’s memory because it was the first and last visit ever paid by the Ulyanovs to the Astrakhan branch of the family. When Anna visited Astrakhan
2 CONC  her grandfather [http://www.geni.com/people/Nikolay-Vasilyevich-Ulyanov-%E2%86%90-Ulyanin/6000000010031431504 Nikolai Vasilevich Ulyanov] (1768–1836) had been dead for over thirty years. He had started life called not Ulyanov but
2 CONC  Ulyanin and as a serf in the village of Androsovo in the Nizhnii Novgorod province. It is possible that, while Nikolai belonged to the Orthodox faith, he might have been not a Russian but a [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mordvins 
2 CONC Mordvinian] or a [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chuvash_people Chuvash]." (White 2001: 13)
