2 CONC ked Russian 'blood' on both sides of his family." (Service 2000:21-23)
2 CONT
2 CONT ''' "Writing in 1927, Lenin’s sister''' [http://www.geni.com/people/Anna-Ilyinichna-Ulyanova/6000000010030450923 Anna] recalled that when she was four years old or thereabouts, in 1868 or 1869, her parents took her and her little 
2 CONC brother [http://www.geni.com/people/Aleksandr-Ilyich-Ulyanov/6000000010030785559 Alexander] on a steamer trip down the Volga from Nizhnii Novgorod to Astrakhan, the home of [http://www.geni.com/people/Ilya-Nikolayevich-Ulyanov/600
2 CONC 0000010018994260 her father]’s relations. She remembered the small two-storey house in which [http://www.geni.com/people/Anna-Alexeyevna-Smirnova/6000000010031154884 her grandmother] and her uncle [http://www.geni.com/people/Vasil
2 CONC y-Ulyanov/6000000010031647977 Vasilii] lived, the warm welcome they received there, and the great fuss made over Alexander and herself. The trip stood out in Anna’s memory because it was the first and last visit ever paid by the U
2 CONC lyanovs to the Astrakhan branch of the family. When Anna visited Astrakhan her grandfather Nikolai Vasilevich Ulyanov (1768–1836) had been dead for over thirty years. He had started life called not Ulyanov but Ulyanin and as a ser
2 CONC f in the village of Androsovo in the Nizhnii Novgorod province. It is possible that, while Nikolai belonged to the Orthodox faith, he might have been not a Russian but a [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mordvins Mordvinian] or a [htt
2 CONC p://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chuvash_people Chuvash]." (White 2001: 13)
2 CONT
2 CONT ------------------------------
