1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ==Biography==
2 CONT '''James Edgar Stephens''' was born on July 23, 1888, in Castroville, Medina County, Texas, United States. His parents were [https://www.geni.com/profile-126333436 William Bell Stephens] and [https://www.geni.com/profile-126333416
2 CONC  Margaret Henrietta Stephens]. He was a Chief Dispatcher of Southern Pacific Railroad, Austin and Hearne, Texas.
2 CONT
2 CONT James married [https://www.geni.com/profile-34715857540 Julia Eugenia Stephens (Lancaster)]. Together they had the following children:
2 CONT *  [https://www.geni.com/profile-34715940019 Margery Edna Stephens];
2 CONT *  [https://www.geni.com/profile-34715940020 Mary Nixon Rogers (Stephens)];
2 CONT *  [https://www.geni.com/profile-34717874853 James William Stephens];
2 CONT *  [https://www.geni.com/profile-34709266998 Ruth Gene Martin]. * 
2 CONT
2 CONT He died on August 10, 1962, in Marble Falls, Burnet County, Texas, the United States from Agranulucytosis and was buried after August 10, 1962, in Austin, Travis County, Texas, United States. 
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT They lived in Lockhart, Austin, Hearne during his time with employment with the railroad. After retirement, they lived in Marble Falls, Texas until his death, Aug. 10, 1962.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Profession'''
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Chief Dispatcher''', for the railroad, his last position before retirement was in Hearne, Texas. Hearne was the busiest "rural" rail junction in Texas, a major reason the surrounding area historically hosted three interlocks. H
2 CONC earne's first railroad was the Houston and Texas Central (H&TC) Railroad, which arrived from the south in 1868 as it built north toward the Red River. In August 1870, the International Railroad was chartered, and four months later
2 CONC , it started building east from Hearne toward Palestine, with plans to connect northeast Texas with Laredo. In 1873, the International Railroad merged with the Houston & Great Northern Railroad to form the International & Great No
2 CONC rthern (IGN) Railroad. In 1881, the IGN completed the line from Palestine to Laredo via Hearne, Austin, and San Antonio. On July 16, 1903, Tower 15 was approved by the Railroad Commission to control the junction of the H&TC and th
2 CONC e IGN in Hearne.
2 CONT
2 CONT A third railroad, the Hearne & Brazos Valley (H&BV), was chartered in 1891 by local cotton farmers desiring to build a railroad from Moseley's Ferry to Hearne, where connections could be made with other railroads. Moseley's Ferry 
2 CONC was located where the Old San Antonio Road crossed the Brazos River (a site now occupied by the State Highway 21 bridge). In 1892, the 16-mile line was completed by H&BV, but instead of going into Hearne, it went north to a connec
2 CONC tion with the IGN four miles west of Hearne, a location that became known as Valley Junction. In 1899, the H&BV was almost destroyed by a Brazos River flood. It was rebuilt that same year, the routing was altered. Instead of going
2 CONC  to Valley Junction, the north end of the line was rerouted to go directly into Hearne where the H&BV could make connections with both the IGN and the H&TC
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1914, the H&BV was acquired by Southern Pacific (SP). SP bridged the Brazos River at Stone City (a community near Moseley's Ferry), and the line was extended under the H&TC charter south to Giddings, where a connection was made
2 CONC  to the San Antonio & Aransas Pass, another SP railroad. This extension became known as the Dalsa Cutoff because it permitted SP's Dallas-San Antonio traffic to bypass Houston via Hearne and Flatonia; the Houston bottleneck had im
2 CONC pacted SP's ability to compete for traffic between Memphis and Los Angeles. In 1929, a cabin interlocker designated Tower 140 was established at Tatsie to control the I-crossing of the Dalsa Cutoff. All of the lines in the Hearne 
2 CONC area remain in use by Union Pacific (UP), the successor to the MP and SP systems. This was one of the busiest hubs in Texas.*ref: [1]- [http://txrrhistory.com/towers/140/140.htm Texas History]] 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''References'''
2 CONT
2 CONT "Early Nixon's of Texas" by Pat Ireland Nixon, MD, Genealogies by Dr. and Mrs. Pat Ireland Nixon, Jr., Published by Carl Hertzog, El Paso, Texas 1956 [https://babel.hathitrust.org/cgi/pt?id=wu.89082553272&view=1up&seq=11]
2 CONT
2 CONT * [http://txrrhistory.com/towers/140/140.htm]*ref: [1]-Hearne, TX Railroad hub
2 CONT
2 CONT Lancaster, from Virginia to Texas, by Mary Nixon Rogers (Stephens), daughter of James Edgar Stephens and Julia Eugenia Stephens (Lancaster). Probated wills, land deeds, Census Records, Employment records, and Birth Records are ref
2 CONC erenced. all copies will be uploaded as time allows in the near future. Suzan Martin
