1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ==Biography==
2 CONT ''' William Bell Stephens''' was born on October 26, 1852, in Macon, Bibb County, Georgia, USA, Macon, Bibb County, Georgia, United States. His parents were [https://www.geni.com/profile-34717686826 Edward Stevens] and [https://ww
2 CONC w.geni.com/profile-34717686867 Edith Edy Stephens (Kilby)]. He was a Merchant and editor-publisher of Newspapers. 
2 CONT
2 CONT William married [https://www.geni.com/profile-126333416 Margaret Henrietta Stephens] on June 12, 1883, in Luling, Caldwell County, TX, United States. Together they had the following children:
2 CONT * [https://www.geni.com/profile-34712167592 James Edgar Stephens]; 
2 CONT * [https://www.geni.com/profile-34716574883 Irma Nixon Johnson]; 
2 CONT * [https://www.geni.com/profile-34715940013 Ora Mae Brewer]; 
2 CONT * [https://www.geni.com/profile-34715940011 Robert Nixon Stephens]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT He died on March 27, 1927, in Gotebo, Kiowa County, Oklahoma, USA, Gotebo, Kiowa County, Oklahoma, the United States, and was buried in Greenleaf Cemetery, Brownwood, Brown County, Texas, USA, Brownwood, Brown County, TX, United S
2 CONC tates. [https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/15164601/william-bell-stephens Find A Grave Memorial][https://www.geni.com/documents/view?doc_id=6000000214719795845 Obituary W B Stephens Hobart, Kiowa, OK ]
2 CONT ----
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT William Bell Stephens's parents perished during the Civil War; by age 14, he was sent to live in Texas with an uncle and aunt.  His younger brother, Edgar Alfred Stevens (Stephens), was separated and raised in a different state. L
2 CONC ittle is known about his young life until the 1880s when he met Margaret "Maggie" Nixon, but the Civil War era was a difficult time, and many records were burned. The family has a memoir from his deathbed, a sketch of his early li
2 CONC fe. 
2 CONT
2 CONT From the memory of William Bell Stephens. After the disappearance of his father and older brothers, his mother, Mary Edy Stephens (Kilby) took the younger children to Greenville, Butler County Alabama to stay with relatives. Gener
2 CONC al Sherman was burning the Courthouses and homes, and they were not safe in their home in Butler, Bibb County, Georgia. Sadly, soon after arriving in Greenville, Eady and their daughter Mary were killed in an attack by the Norther
2 CONC n troops. Mary Edith Stephens (Kilby) protected her young children but was shot and killed in front of the young children, they also witnessed their sister, Mary being shot as well. This left the children orphans, and the youngest
2 CONC  ones, William Bell, Benjamin Franklin, and Edgar Alfred Stephens, split up and divided between relatives. They never heard from Benjamin Franklin Stephens again in the future, so he didn't know exactly where he settled in the fut
2 CONC ure. William Bell Stephens and Edgar Alfred Stephens, aka Stevens, did remain in contact. Both settled in Texas by the time they were adults. William Bell Stephens came to Texas with William and Nancy Broadus at age 14 to live wit
2 CONC h an uncle and aunt of his mother's.
2 CONT
2 CONT Biography
2 CONT
2 CONT In Guadalupe County, he married [https://www.geni.com/people/Margaret-Stephens/6000000012839840153]  on June 12, 1883. She was born on Oct. 29, 1860, the daughter of Captain Robert Thomas Nixon and Laura Ann Nixon (Woods) of  Old 
2 CONC Nixon, Guadalupe County, Texas. He was a business owner and journalist. ---- 
2 CONT ----
2 CONT [https://www.geni.com/documents/view?doc_id=6000000105895790948&] William was a successful merchant in Old Nixon until he sold his business and moved with Margaret to open a Newspaper Business in Hondo, Texas. The Hondo Anvil Hera
2 CONC ld, a weekly newspaper serving Medina County since 1886, owed its origins to a nineteenth-century county seat dispute that divided the Southwest Texas towns of Castroville and Hondo City and to a man who later bought the principal
2 CONC  papers from each town and put them together. The Castroville Anvil was established in July 1886 after Castroville defeated a move to make Hondo the county seat. Castroville supporters staged a large celebration of their hard-won 
2 CONC victory. One of the features of the event was the firing of anvils, a process by which gunpowder blows anvils into the air. The loud, cannon-like reports set the nearby hills ringing with echoes. The newspaper was named Anvil to s
2 CONC uggest a parallel. Two previous papers had operated in Castroville, the Era (1876–79), and the Quill (1879–82). With a total capital of $2,500, the Castroville Printing and Publishing Company was formed on May 24, 1886. Circulatio
2 CONC n was more than 500 within a year and 750 by 1888. W. B.(William Bell)  Stephens, the first Anvil editor and printer, was succeeded after two years by P. J. Stephenson. In 1889, the paper was sold to the state Farmers' Alliance, w
2 CONC hich sought $5,000 in stock from members. John G. Hall served as editor. In 1892, Castroville lost to Hondo City in another county seat election.
2 CONT ----
2 CONT He owned various mercantile businesses but enjoyed writing and editing. He also owned various newspaper businesses until retirement in Gotebo, Kiowa County, Oklahoma. 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Education''' The University of Texas BS, MA in Eng-Lit
2 CONT ----
2 CONT ----
