1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ==Biography==
2 CONT '''Laura Ann Nixon''' was born on February 18, 1832, in Randolph County, North Carolina, USA. Her parents were [https://www.geni.com/profile-126333342 William Wood] and [https://www.geni.com/profile-126333366 Henrietta Denman]. La
2 CONC ura was a Pioneer homemaker who died giving birth to her twelfth child. She was a wonderful mother,  teacher, and counselor to her children.  At a time when infant mortality was appalling, all of their children survived to adultho
2 CONC od, except two infants. Laura was an exceptional pioneer wife and mother; it was evident in the future success of her children. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Laura married [https://www.geni.com/profile-34715961279 Captain (CSA), Robert Thomas Nixon] on August 31, 1852, in Randolph County, North Carolina, United States. Together, they had the following children:
2 CONT * [https://www.geni.com/profile-34715961281 William Thomas Nixon];
2 CONT * [https://www.geni.com/profile-34715961280 James Wesley Nixon, MD];
2 CONT * [https://www.geni.com/profile-34715961283 John Phillip Nixon];
2 CONT * [https://www.geni.com/profile-126333416 Margaret Henrietta Stephens];
2 CONT * [https://www.geni.com/profile-34740807684 Steven Harrison Nixon];
2 CONT * [https://www.geni.com/profile-34721942469 Elmira Isabelle Champion];
2 CONT * [https://www.geni.com/profile-34740807689 Robert Lee Nixon];
2 CONT * [https://www.geni.com/profile-34740807691 Alexander Stephens Nixon];
2 CONT * [https://www.geni.com/profile-34740807688 Viola Mae Wilson (Nixon)];
2 CONT * [https://www.geni.com/profile-34740807692 Henley Nixon];
2 CONT * [https://www.geni.com/profile-34740807690 Sam Houston Nixon];
2 CONT * [https://www.geni.com/profile-34715961282 Stephen Harrison Nixon]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT She died on July 4, 1872, in Guadalupe, Texas, USA (Childbirth) with the12th child, Guadalupe County, Texas, the United States and was buried, at The Nixon Family Cemetery, Old Nixon, Guadalupe County, Texas/
2 CONT ----
2 CONT Laura's Legacy
2 CONT
2 CONT The Nixon Women of Texas
2 CONT
2 CONT When one views the photo of the spacious plantation home set on 14, 000 acres of the Texas Hill Country, one most likely thinks, that all the Nixon women were the typical southern debutantes.
2 CONT
2 CONT Attending social events, and wearing the latest Paris or New York fashions the Nixon daughters did not disappoint the crowds. They were members of the Junior League, Eastern Star, and many charitable organizations along the way.
2 CONT
2 CONT Capt. Robert Thomas Nixon made sure if they desired, they attended the best prep schools, dance, music, foreign language, finishing schools, and colleges, as well. This is where the typical debutante story ends.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Nixon girls were on horseback herding livestock and mending fences. Many became accomplished equestrians as did their grandchildren. They also learned alongside their brothers and husbands how to manage a large Texas ranch.
2 CONT
2 CONT They were able to choose what profession and college degree they wanted, with one exception, their father refused to pay for a teaching degree at a teacher’s college, he said the salary was too low, so they had to choose a profess
2 CONC ion and a degree they would support them and a family. This was the first generation that started our family tradition of the East Coast Prep School, Philip Exeter.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Nixon daughters were treated exactly equal to the Nixon sons. This was unusual for this time in history, but normal for the Nixon lineag over generations. They were originally from the Friend’s Religion, known as Quaker for se
2 CONC veral generations until their father arrived in Texas.
2 CONT
2 CONT After working on the ranch, they would come in in the evening to dress for a formal social affair, their choice if they wanted to attend, look like a debutante, and be presented to society. If only others at the events had seen th
2 CONC em hours earlier roping calves, branding cattle, or cooking, cleaning beside the maids, only the Nixon women could do both successfully and gracefully.
2 CONT ----
