2 CONT ==Biography==
2 CONT '''Captain Robert Thomas Nixon''' was born on April 13, 1827, in Randolph, North Carolina, USA, Randolph County, North Carolina, United States. His parents were [https://www.geni.com/profile-79564215 Zachariah Nixon] and [https://
2 CONC www.geni.com/profile-34717925391 Sarah Nixon (Thomas)]. He was a Merchant, Farmer and Rancher.
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert married [https://www.geni.com/profile-126333375 Laura Ann Nixon (Wood)] Randolph County, North Carolina, United States.  He married 1) Laura Ann Nixon (Wood) on, August 31, 1852. In the Autumn of 1852, they left for Texas, 
2 CONC settling in Guadalupe County. For the first 400 acres they paid $800 for the land as they arrived after the free land grants.  Laura died July 4, 1874, He married 2) Frances Amanda Andrews on December 19, 1872. She was a wonderful
2 CONC  mother to Robert Thomas' children from Laura. 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Twelve Children with Laura Ann Nixon (Wood)'''
2 CONT # [https://www.geni.com/profile-34715961281 William Thomas Nixon];
2 CONT # [https://www.geni.com/profile-34715961280 James Wesley Nixon, MD];
2 CONT # [https://www.geni.com/profile-34715961283 John Phillip Nixon];
2 CONT # [https://www.geni.com/profile-126333416 Margaret Henrietta Stephens (Nixon)];
2 CONT # [https://www.geni.com/profile-34740807684 Steven Harrison Nixon];
2 CONT # [https://www.geni.com/profile-34721942469 Elmira Isabelle Champion (Nixon)];
2 CONT # [https://www.geni.com/profile-34740807689 Robert Lee Nixon];
2 CONT # [https://www.geni.com/profile-34740807691 Alexander Stephens Nixon];
2 CONT # [https://www.geni.com/profile-34740807688 Viola Mae Nixon];
2 CONT # [https://www.geni.com/profile-34740807692 Henley Nixon];
2 CONT # [https://www.geni.com/profile-34740807690 Sam Houston Nixon];
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert married [https://www.geni.com/profile-34717925392 Frances Amanda Nixon] on December 8, 1872, in Guadalupe County, Texas, USA. '''Seven Children born with Fannie''''''
2 CONT # [https://www.geni.com/profile-34740807674 Corinne Pearl Hyman];
2 CONT # [https://www.geni.com/profile-34740807673 Mollie Myrtle Allen (Nixon)];
2 CONT # [https://www.geni.com/profile-34687119548 Pat Ireland Nixon, MD];
2 CONT # [https://www.geni.com/profile-34740807672 Beula Viene Wood];
2 CONT # [https://www.geni.com/profile-34740807675 Alta Estelle Fisher];
2 CONT # [https://www.geni.com/profile-34740807677 Thomas Hendrix Nixon];
2 CONT # [https://www.geni.com/profile-34740807680 Zebulon Vance Nixon]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT He died on March 27, 1897, at the Nixon Plantation, Nixon, Guadalupe, Texas, USA, Texas, the United States from natural causes and was buried after March 27, 1897, in Kingsbury, Guadalupe County, Texas, USA, Nixon, Gonzales County
2 CONC , Texas, United States. 
2 CONT ---
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT '''References'''* [https://www.wikiwand.com/en/Nixon,_Texas] - At the end of the century, he owned and fenced 14,000 acres of land, grazed 1,000 head of well-bred Durham Beef cattle, a herd of Jerseys, 300 horses, and mules. Rober
2 CONC t Thomas, as early as 1870 was experimenting with crossbreeding of Spanish Ponies and Bay, Spanish Ponies, and Quarter Horse Stallions. He was the owner of a good Billy-stock stallion and he continued to have good horses for the r
2 CONC est of his life. The Quarter Horse for July 1947 "The Story of The Old Joe Bailey" by Mrs. H. H. Wentworth gives interesting facts about Robert "Bob" Nixon's Billy stallion and his numerous offspring. The Nixon's used for a saddle
2 CONC  horse, but just south of their Ranch lived Tom King, who regularly went with his horses to tracts all over the county. "pg. 12 "Early Nixon's of Texas"
2 CONT ----
2 CONT When General Gordan Granger landed at Galveston, Texas on June 19, 1865, and proclaimed" The Slaves of Texas were Free", Robert Thomas Nixon was not affected for he owned no slaves. pg. 10 "Early Nixon's of Texas"
2 CONT ----
2 CONT Military August 1861 Lamar County Capt. G.W. Patterson Commanding Officer 9th Brigade Rank 2nd Lt. Promoted to Capt.
2 CONT ----
2 CONT Why The Quaker lineage Nixon's may have chosen The Confederate [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Texas_in_the_American_Civil_War] By the 1860s this branch of the Nixon's were not in The Friends Society at this point, they were in the
2 CONC  Methodist Church in Guadalupe County, Texas. Dr. Pat Ireland Nixon, his son, made a note in the genealogy book that there were no Quaker Meeting Houses in Guadalupe County Texas, so this may have been why his father joined the Me
2 CONC thodist church. They were bearing arms and protecting their family, which was against strict Friends Society beliefs. 
2 CONT ----
2 CONT American Civil War- Texas Confederate 9th Brigade - Mounted Texas Mounted - Went in as 2nd Lt. - Capt. at end of Civil War reform time. Capt. G.W. Patterson  9th Brigade  Aug 1861-1865 The Nixon Cemetery - National '''Landmark-htt
2 CONC ps://images.findagrave.com/photos/2008/9/CEM46874433_119998382459.jpg'''
2 CONT ----
2 CONT ''' Nixon and Huff Freight Company'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Capt RT Nixon was in a business partnership with his friend [https://www.geni.com/people/Leonard-Huff-Sr/6000000031200261905 L C Huff] until Leonard died in 1873.. 
2 CONT Indianola, Galveston and Matamoros, Mexico. They went to Matamoras because they could bring in goods from Spain and France without duties. They delivered corn, cotton, and other goods for sale and shipping. They brought back flour
2 CONC , wine, whiskey, vinegar, fine goods, supplies, and fabrics for Nixon's, Huff, Josey, and Johnston's stores. 
2 CONT ----
