1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ==Biography==
2 CONT Obit-Gonzales Inquirer March 6, 1939
2 CONT
2 CONT Died at the Age of 83
2 CONT
2 CONT Last of Old School of Family Doctors of Horse And Bugg Era
2 CONT
2 CONT Doctor James Wesley Nixon,  last of the old pioneer physicians and surgeons of this section, died Sunday afternoons at 4:15 in the Holmes hospital here.  He had been seriously ill since last January. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Funeral services were held at 4 o'clock  this afternoon (Monday) from the family residence with Rev. K. C. Minter, pastor of the First Methodist Church, officiating. Interment was in the Masonic cemetery and funeral arrangements w
2 CONC ere in charge of Robertson and Seydler.
2 CONT
2 CONT Active pall bearers were Pink Cobb, O. W. Thompson, C. T. Rather, Jim Scheske, J. J. Matthews and Nixon Lynch.
2 CONT
2 CONT Surviving are three children: R. L. Nixon, Gonzales, Dr. J. W. (Jamie) Nixon, San Antonio, and Mrs. Luella N. Kelley of Austin; nine grandchildren, Luella Nixon, Laura Nixon, Jim Nixon, Mildred Nixon, Polly Nixon, Patty Nixon and 
2 CONC brothers; Mrs. Maggie Stephens, Oklahoma; Mrs. Ella Champion, Luling; Sam Nixon, Yancey; Mrs. Walter Hyman, Luling; Mrs. Ed Wood, Luling; Mrs. Alta Fisher, Luling; Z. V. Nixon, Luling; Dr., P. I. Nixon, SAN Antonio; Mrs. Frank C. 
2 CONC Allen, Corpus Christi.
2 CONT
2 CONT Dr. Nixon was 83 years, 10 months, and 15 days of age at the time of his death. He was born at Old Nixon, in Guadalupe county near Luling, the eldest son of R. T.  and Laure Nixon, pioneer settlers who came to this section from No
2 CONC rth Carolina. He attended the University of Virginia and Tulane University of New Orleans to study his chosen profession and did post-graduate work at the medical branch of the University at Galveston.
2 CONT
2 CONT Dr. Nixon was first married Dec. 29, 1886, to Miss Annie King, and to this union one child, Laura Nixon, who died as a baby was born, Mrs. Annie King Nixon also passed away a few years after her marriage and on November 26, 1890, 
2 CONC Dr. Nixon and Miss Mary King were united in marriage and to them were born three children who now survived. The late Mrs. Nixon preceded her husband in death on January 28, 1937.
2 CONT
2 CONT Three brothers and sisters also preceded Dr. Nixon in death, Mrs. Garrett Wilson and J. P. Nixon of New Mexico.
2 CONT
2 CONT Dr. Nixon first began the practice of medicine at Old Nixon in 1882. He then went to Wightsboro where he practiced until 1893 when he came to Gonzales and continued his professional career until 1917, when he retired. Even after a
2 CONC nnouncing his retirement in 1917, the demand for his services from those he had ministered unto for so many years was so heavy that he could not attain to freedom he desired from his profession, which is given as one of the reason
2 CONC s why he purchased a ranch in Medina county and moved there during the same year of his retirement.
2 CONT
2 CONT After eleven years of ranch life, Dr. Nixon moved to Austin in 1928 and then back to his old home in Gonzales in 1931, where he resided until death.
2 CONT
2 CONT There are many living in this section today, who remembered Dr. Nixon for his characteristic kindness, his wit, and a keen sense of humor. Numbers of those who are now middle-aged can recall a vivid picture of Dr. Nixon riding dow
2 CONC n the street on a big gray horse, and how they would scrapper when he playfully chased them or attempted to rope them.
2 CONT
2 CONT The two big bays and blacks that pulled the doctor at a fast clip in a buggy about the countryside, are also remembered by many. Dr. Nixon retired from practice about the time the automobile came into its own as a practical means 
2 CONC of conveyance. 
2 CONT
2 CONT "They won't stop when I say whoa," is the reason the doctor gave for using his two horses to a buggy, rather than an early day automobile.
2 CONT
2 CONT Dr. Nixon's long professional career was deadly that of the " old country family doctor, " with all the traditions of service and romance, which endeared him to hundreds of overall this section. The weather was never too dark for 
2 CONC Dr. Nixon to streak out down country lane and by-ways to answer a call of distress, despite the blackness of the night, the cold and rain.
2 CONT
2 CONT Coupled with his friendly and sympathetic manner, he had the acumen of a most accurate memory, even into his declining days, He was keenly observant, read constantly, and was thoroughly conversant with affairs of the day.
2 CONT
2 CONT As an example of his keen memory a few years ago a man walked up to his shook his hand and said: "You don't remember me?"
2 CONT
2 CONT "You haven't cut your foot again lately, have you?" Dr. Nixon asked after a moment of reflection. He had treated the man for a gashed foot 32 years previously.
2 CONT
2 CONT Another idiosyncrasy of this doctor of the old school, who made friends remembered their names and faces, was to change their names if he thought they were not fitting. He had his own names for everybody and so booked them on his 
2 CONC own accords.
2 CONT
2 CONT Despite his activity in administering to the sick, Dr. Nixon found time for study and research in medicine. He developed the use of Chapparo Amargosa in the treatment of chronic dysentery, which due to his research work, testing a
2 CONC nd study is now available in prepared form. Chapparo Amargosa is a small thorny bush that was used as a Mexican home remedy for the treatment of bowel trouble, which grows in this county and Southwest Texas. He subjected himself t
2 CONC o the risk of death in testing the plant to prove that it was not toxic, taking heavy dosages of medicine derived from the plant. After proving to his satisfaction that the plant was useful as the medicine he prepped and used in 1
2 CONC 883. He sent a package of Chapparo Amargosa to laboratories in London, England to be compounded into medication. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Dr. Nixon and the late Dr. W.  Hildebrand, another of the well-known and beloved old physicians were partners for a time. He lived longer than several of the younger doctors of the community some who started practicing about the f
2 CONC irst time of Dr. Nixons retirement ___* Reference: [https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/171503052/james-w_-nixon Find A Grave Memorial] - [https://www.geni.com/projects/SmartCopy/18783 SmartCopy]: ''Jun 27 2020, 23:00:45 UTC''
2 CONT
2 CONT '''About'''
2 CONT
2 CONT James Wesley Nixon, MD''' was born on April 20, 1855, in Guadalupe County, Texas, United States. His parents were [https://www.geni.com/profile-34715961279 Captain Robert Thomas Nixon] and [https://www.geni.com/profile-126333375 L
2 CONC aura Ann Nixon]. He was a Horse Rancher, Physician, and Surgeon. 
2 CONT
2 CONT James married [https://www.geni.com/profile-34742579245 Anne C. Nixon (King)] on November 29, 1886, in Gonzales, Texas, USA. Together they had the following children:
2 CONT # [https://www.geni.com/profile-34742579247 Laura Ann Nixon]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT James married [https://www.geni.com/profile-34722417449 Mary Nixon (King)] on November 26, 1890, in Wrightsboro, Gonzales, Texas, USA. Together they had the following children:
2 CONT # [https://www.geni.com/profile-34741435055 James William Nixon, MD];
2 CONT # [https://www.geni.com/profile-34741435054 Luella Kenley];
2 CONT # [https://www.geni.com/profile-34741435053 Robert Leroy Nixon]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT He died on March 5, 1939, in Gonzales, Gonzales County, Texas, United States, and was buried after March 5, 1939, there. 
2 CONT ----
2 CONT ---- '''Education''' Philips Exeter Academy, Prep School, Capt. Robert Thomas Nixon was concerned, although his children were intelligent the Texas Schools were inferior to Virginia schools, he decided his children should attend P
2 CONC hillips Exeter Academy as prep for college and medical school. Future generations of our Nixon lineage followed this family tradition through the present day.[https://www.exeter.edu/]
2 CONT
2 CONT * University of Virginia
2 CONT * Tulane University
2 CONT * The University of Texas at Galveston Medical Branch
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT '''References'''*
2 CONT
2 CONT Medical Doctor and Rancher.  He attended the University of Virginia and Tulane University of New Orleans to study his chosen profession and did post-graduate work at the medical branch of the University at Galveston, later became 
2 CONC the University of Texas Medical Branch of Texas at Galveston in the late 1890s. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Registration information with American Quarter Horse, Assoc.,  Owned by Janes Wesley Nixon, MD  NIXONS JOE BAILEY dk ch. H, QUARTER HORSE, 1902 Color: dk ch Height: 15.1 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''U0074800''' number
2 CONT
2 CONT Foaled in Missouri 1902 or 1903  Purchased and brought to Texas by Tom Stephenson and H Kimble Woods  Sold to Dr. J. W. James Wesley Nixon, Medina County, TX  Popular stallion bred to mostly Billy mares
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT "Early Nixon's of Texas" by Pat Ireland Nixon, MD, Genealogies by Dr. and Mrs. Pat Ireland Nixon, Jr., Published by Carl Hertzog, El Paso, Texas 1956 Graduate degree, University of Kentucky. Medical Degree, Tulane University Medic
2 CONC al School, New Orleans, LA. Also, residency in Galveston, Texas. [https://newbooksinpolitics.com/political/the-early-nixons-of-texas/]
2 CONT
2 CONT The University of Virginia - 
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT Find-A-Grave[https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/171503052/james-w_-nixon] [https://books.google.com/books?id=C6VGAQAAMAAJ&pg=RA2-PA12&lpg=RA2-PA12&dq=james+wesley+Nixon,+md+Texas&source=bl&ots=zuWzmvWpeP&sig=ACfU3U2Cm2CJB2QEG2Gy4
2 CONC Bdpc7_oW9uPwQ&hl=en&sa=X&ved=2ahUKEwiJh5D4v7PkAhUHTawKHQxtCXYQ6AEwEnoECAkQAQ#v=onepage&q&f=false]
2 CONT
2 CONT Please note, some information in this book Certain errors always do seem to creep in, so we must be careful. "A History of Texas" by Frances White Johnson " Please Read with caution, don't take as FACT" '''his father, Robert Thoma
2 CONC s Nixon, didn't come to Texas with his Four Orphan Brother's in 1852 because his father Zachariah Nixon died in route to Texas in a Storm by Sea in 1855.''' I don't know who, Frances White Johnson was referring, nor is this fact a
2 CONC nd a few other ones she added, not mentioned in "The Early Nixon's of Texas" by Pat Ireland Nixon, MD, son of Robert Thomas Nixon. In fact, they were all grown men by this date, so that statement was wild conjecture and not true. 
2 CONC  Caution... see above notes https://books.google.com/books?id=LZNJAAAAYAAJ&pg=PA1500&dq=Robert+Thomas+Nixon&hl=en&sa=X&ved=0ahUKEwigyr-olqzjAhVRV80KHYHqB6gQ6AEIKDAA#v=onepage&q=Robert%20Thomas%20Nixon&f=false
