1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Elmira Isabelle Champion (Nixon)''' husband was a prominent physician and surgeon that owned a Drug and Mercantile business in Luling, Texas. When her husband died from Typhoid Fever, she managed the profitable 
2 CONC Mercantile business and made their stately family home into a large boarding house assisting the payment of college tuition for her children's college degrees and medical school. This work ethic and independence learned from her f
2 CONC ather, Captain Robert Thomas Nixon's Quaker lineage. "Ella's" father brought his daughters up and educated them as he did his sons. Her degree from The University of Texas was Business Administration, History. Her father refused h
2 CONC is children to attend college to become schoolteachers, he knew during this time a schoolteacher's salary wouldn't support a family. She was loved and admired by her children, sisters, brothers, grandchildren, and friends. "Ella" 
2 CONC never remarried.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''The Nixon Women of Texas'''
2 CONT
2 CONT When one views the photo of the spacious plantation home Ella Nixon was raised, set on 14, 000 acres of the Texas Hill Country, they most likely think, all the Nixon women were the typical southern debutantes.
2 CONT
2 CONT Attending social events, wearing the latest Paris or New York fashions the Nixon daughters did not disappoint the crowds. They were members of the Junior League, Eastern Star, and many organizations along the way.
2 CONT
2 CONT Capt. Robert Thomas Nixon made sure if they desired, they attended the best prep schools, dance, music, foreign language, finishing schools, and colleges as well. This is where the typical debutante story ends.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Nixon girls were on horseback herding livestock and mending fences. Many became accomplished equestrians as did their grandchildren. They also learned along with their brothers how to manage a large ranch business.
2 CONT
2 CONT They were able to choose what profession and college degree they wanted, with one exception, their father refused to pay for a teaching degree at a teacher’s college, he said the salary was too low, so they must choose a professio
2 CONC n and a degree they would support them and a family. This was the generation that started our family tradition of the East Coast Prep School, Philip Exeter.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Nixon daughters and were treated equally to the Nixon sons. This was unusual for this time in history, but normal for the Nixon linage over generations. They were originally from the Friend’s Religion, known as Quaker for seve
2 CONC ral generations until their father arrived in Texas and joined the Methodist Church.
2 CONT
2 CONT After working on the ranch, they would come in in the evening to dress for a formal social affair, their choice if they wanted to attend, looked like a debutante, and being presented to society. If only others at the events would 
2 CONC have seen them hours earlier roping calves and branding cattle, or cooking, cleaning beside the maids, only the Nixon women could do both successfully and with grace.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT ----
2 CONT ----
2 CONT '''Elmira Isabelle Champion''' was born on April 1, 1862, in Guadalupe County, Texas, United States. Her parents were [https://www.geni.com/profile-34715961279 Captain Robert Thomas Nixon] and [https://www.geni.com/profile-1263333
2 CONC 75 Laura Ann Nixon]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Elmira married [https://www.geni.com/profile-34748670116 Nicholas Champion, MD] on December 28, 1882 in Texas. 
2 CONT  Together they had the following children:
2 CONT * [https://www.geni.com/profile-34721943326 Nina Katherine Champion]; 
2 CONT * [https://www.geni.com/profile-34748670124 James Nixon Champion]; 
2 CONT * [https://www.geni.com/profile-34748670126 Marie Teresa Champion]; 
2 CONT * [https://www.geni.com/profile-34748670125 Albert Nicholas Champion Md, Jr]; 
2 CONT * [https://www.geni.com/profile-34748670127 Rosamond Champion]; 
2 CONT * [https://www.geni.com/profile-34748670128 John Nixon Champion, Sr]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT She died on January 13, 1945, and was buried at Luling City Cemetery in Luling, Caldwell County, Texas, United States[https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/57013777/elmira-chamption]
2 CONT ----
2 CONT ---- '''The Nixon Women of Texas'''
2 CONT
2 CONT When one views the photo of the spacious plantation home set on 14, 000 acres of the Texas Hill Country, they most likely think, all the Nixon women were the typical southern debutantes.
2 CONT
2 CONT Attending social events, wearing the latest Paris or New York fashions the Nixon daughters did not disappoint the crowds. They were members of the Junior League, Eastern Star, and many organizations along the way.
2 CONT
2 CONT Capt. Robert Thomas Nixon made sure if they desired, they attended the best prep schools, dance, music, foreign language, finishing schools, and colleges as well. This is where the typical debutante story ends.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Nixon girls were on horseback herding livestock and mending fences. Many became accomplished equestrians as did their grandchildren. They also learned beside their brothers how to manage a large plantation.
2 CONT
2 CONT They were able to choose what profession and college degree they wanted, with one exception, their father refused to pay for a teaching degree at a teacher’s college, he said the salary was too low, so they must choose a professio
2 CONC n and a degree they would support them and a family. This was the generation that started our family tradition of the East Coast Prep School, Philip Exeter.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Nixon daughters and were treated exactly equal to the Nixon sons. This was unusual for this time in history, but normal for the Nixon linage over generations. They were originally from the Friend’s Religion, known as Quaker fo
2 CONC r several generations until their father arrived in Texas.
2 CONT
2 CONT After working on the ranch, they would come in in the evening to dress for a formal social affair, their choice if they wanted to attend, looked like a debutante, and being presented to society. If only others at the events would 
2 CONC have seen them hours earlier roping calves and branding cattle, or cooking, cleaning beside the maids, only the Nixon women could do both successfully and with grace.
2 CONT ----
