1 OCCU Farmer
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ==Biography==
2 CONT '''Edward Stevens, 2nd Corp, CSA''' was born in 1805 in Macon, Bibb, Georgia, USA or Kentucky, Macon, Bibb, GA, United States. His parents were [https://www.geni.com/profile-34746801287 William J Stevens] and [https://www.geni.com
2 CONC /profile-34746801300 Mary Stevens (Clark)]. He was a Farmer. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Edward married [https://www.geni.com/profile-34717686867 Edith Edy Stephens (Kilby)] on August 29, 1826, in Jasper, Pickens County, Georgia, United States. Together they had the following children:
2 CONT * [https://www.geni.com/profile-34717928847 Sarah Ann Stephens]; 
2 CONT * [https://www.geni.com/profile-34717928846 Caroline M Lang (Stephens)]; 
2 CONT * [https://www.geni.com/profile-34717928848 James Edward Stephens]; 
2 CONT * [https://www.geni.com/profile-34717928850 Edgar Alfred Stevens]; 
2 CONT * [https://www.geni.com/profile-34717928851 Benjamin Franklin Stephens]; 
2 CONT * [https://www.geni.com/profile-34717928849 Mary Elizabeth Stephens]; 
2 CONT * [https://www.geni.com/profile-126333436 William Bell Stephens]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT On December 17, 1864, while in Franklin, TN fighting with the 33rd, Edward, was taken POW. He was transferred from Louisville, KY on January 4, 1865, to Camp Chase in Columbus, OH. FOLD3 Military records state he was released from
2 CONC  Camp Chase on June 4, 1865. A train ticket was provided for him to Nashville, TN. Some family members assumed he died during the Civil War, no record of his death during the time of the war, he was alive June 4, 1865, and when re
2 CONC leased, he did survive the travel back to Nashville, TN near where he was after being a prisoner at Camp Chase. He also made it back to Greenville, Butler, Alabama, and reunited with his wife, Eady. Administrator Bond to Probated 
2 CONC Will with Eady Stevens signature dated July 28, 1868, filed with the court, Greenville, Butler County, Alabama. [https://www.geni.com/documents/view?doc_id=6000000200435575855]
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT ----
2 CONT '''Military'''
2 CONT Company "C" 17th Alabama Infantry Regiment, organized in Butler Co., AL, was 
2 CONT also called the "Blue Rifles".
2 CONT Alabama, U.S., Civil War Muster Rolls, 1861-1865
2 CONT 17th Alabama Infantry Regiment
2 CONT The following is a roster of Company "C" as organized and marched to the 
2 CONT front, the copy bearing date of September 25, 1861.
2 CONT Confederate
2 CONT Walter D. Perryman, Captain 
2 CONT John Bolling, 1st Lieutenant (1) 
2 CONT John Payne, 2nd Lieutenant (2) 
2 CONT L. W. Trawick, 3rd Lieutenant (3) 
2 CONT C. C. Lloyd, 1st Sergeant (4) 
2 CONT Robert Perry, 2nd Sergeant 
2 CONT Peter Goodson, 3rd Sergeant
2 CONT J. W. Wallace, 4th Sergeant (5)
2 CONT W. R. Peterson, 1st Corporal (6)
2 CONT '''*Edward Stevens, 2nd Corporal'''
2 CONT J. W. Skipper, 3rd Corporal (7)
2 CONT Henry O. Seale, 4th Corporal (8)
2 CONT
2 CONT Name	Edward Stevens
2 CONT Military Unit	17th Alabama Infantry Regiment
2 CONT Military Unit - Company	C
2 CONT Muster Roll Date	1861 14 Sep
2 CONT Rank	Corporal
2 CONT Place of Enlistment - State	Alabama
2 CONT Roll #	186
2 CONT Archive Collection #	SG025026-4
2 CONT
2 CONT July 4, 1862, discharged from 17th Alabama Infantry Regiment - Company C
2 CONT to sign with the 33rd Regiment Co H
2 CONT "The Greenville Guards," or "The Davis Rangers."
2 CONT
2 CONT The last battles he fought before he was taken POW  December 17, 1864.
2 CONT Battle 1
2 CONT Spring Hill
2 CONT Spring Hill, TN  |  Nov 29, 1864
2 CONT Battle 2
2 CONT Civil War  |  Battle
2 CONT Franklin Battle  [https://www.battlefields.org/learn/civil-war/battles/franklin]
2 CONT Franklin, TN  |  Nov 30, 1864
2 CONT Battle 3
2 CONT Civil War  |  Battle
2 CONT Battle of Nashville  [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_Nashville]
2 CONT Davidson County, TN  |  Dec 15 - 16, 1864 
2 CONT
2 CONT ''' Captured as a prisoner'''  on December 17, 1864, when he returned to Franklin, TN, transferred to Camp Chase on January 4, 1865, and then released on June 4, 1865. Records show he was provided a train ticket to Nashville, TN. 
2 CONT
