1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ==Biography==
2 CONT '''John Nathaniel Lancaster''' was born on May 5, 1827, in Bellefonte, Madison County, Alabama, United States. His parents were [https://www.geni.com/profile-34715964807 Josiah Lancaster] and [https://www.geni.com/profile-34715964
2 CONC 803 Lucy Ann Lancaster (Hudson)]. When John Nathaniel Lancaster was born  his father, Josiah, was 34 and his mother, Lucy, was 23. 
2 CONT
2 CONT John Lancaster was a school teacher and rancher in Bellefonte, AL. They moved to Lockhart, Caldwell County by 1883 and he remained a Texas resident until his death at age 92. His wife Mary Ann Lancaster (Jones) was also born in Be
2 CONC llefonte, AL. During the Civil War, he lived in Alabama, but he signed up and served as a  Confederate  Soldier in Texas Duff's Brigade, guarding the boarding of Mexico. [3] He was a rancher and farmer in Texas until retirement. H
2 CONC e and his wife Mary Ann Lancaster (Jones) were wonderful parents, all of their surviving children received college degrees and many went on to receive graduate, doctorate, and medical degrees, but he filed for Texas Confederate Pe
2 CONC nsion Benefits December 1, 1913, and was approved March 1, 1914, so it must have taken everything they had to educate the children. 
2 CONT
2 CONT His father, grandfather Hudson left an inheritance for him and his sister 
2 CONT
2 CONT John married [https://www.geni.com/profile-34715964663 Mary Ann Lancaster (Jones)] on June 21, 1848, in Jackson, Al. Together they had the following children:
2 CONT # [https://www.geni.com/profile-34715964662 William Horace Lancaster, MD];
2 CONT # [https://www.geni.com/profile-34715939849 Henry Edgar Lancaster];
2 CONT # [https://www.geni.com/profile-34715964814 Thompson Moses Lancaster];
2 CONT # [https://www.geni.com/profile-34715964817 Mason Edward Lancaster];
2 CONT # [https://www.geni.com/profile-34715964816 Landonia Madeline Heppenstall];
2 CONT # [https://www.geni.com/profile-34715964815 Josiah Lee Lancaster]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT  [https://www.geni.com/profile-34715964819 Lucy Ann Ellison]; (daughter of Emily) https://www.geni.com/people/Emily-Lancaster/6000000223035298834?through=6000000075596248853 
2 CONT
2 CONT He died on March 31, 1919, in Kingsville, Kleberg County, Texas, United States and was buried after March 31, 1918, in Lockhart, Caldwell County, Texas, United States. 
2 CONT ----
2 CONT ----
2 CONT '''Documented Slave Owner'''- on June 8, 1840, when his grandfather willed him a precious 4yr old enslaved girl named Emily Hudson Lancaster born in Jackson County, Alabama. She was brought to Lockhart, Texas and would've became f
2 CONC ree after the Civil War.[https://www.geni.com/documents/view?doc_id=6000000223034857902&graph_node_id=profile-34688527390&mode=tagged Grandfather Capt. John Hudson named enslaved 4yr old Emily to John Nathaniel Lancaster ]
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Military''''
2 CONT
2 CONT Texas Confederate- When the war broke out he chose to fight and protect the border of Texas from Mexico. 
2 CONT * '''Duff's 33rd Calvary'''
2 CONT * Enlisted in Capt. Robinson's Company -
2 CONT * Company C Duff's Regiment 
2 CONT * Served -  Company at Captain Duff's Regiment on the Border of the Rio Grande River - 
2 CONT * Discharged in Austin, TX in 1865 after surrender.  [https://www.tshaonline.org/handbook/entries/thirty-third-texas-cavalry]
2 CONT
2 CONT '''THIRTY-THIRD TEXAS CAVALRY'''. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The origins of the Thirty-third Texas Cavalry began when James Duff organized the Fourteenth Texas Cavalry Battalion. Duff, an immigrant from Scotland, established a merchant business in San Antonio in 1856. In the summer of 1862,
2 CONC  the Texas government ordered his Fourteenth Texas Cavalry Battalion to the Hill Country to break up the Union Loyal League, organized by German immigrants, and enforce the oath of allegiance. Fearing punishment, many Germans fled
2 CONC  Texas for safety in Mexico and the North. Their flight out of the state led to one of the deadliest events in German Texan history. A number of members of the Union Loyal League, led by Maj. Fritz Tegener, https://www.geni.com/pe
2 CONC ople/Frederick-Tegener/6000000000729514133 were among the many Germans attempting to escape to Mexico. Confederate Texans, led by Duff, received news of their departure and attempted to stop their flight. The Texans intercepted th
2 CONC e Germans on the west bank of the Nueces River twenty miles from Fort Clark, on August 10, 1862, at what was to become known as the battle of the Nueces. In the skirmish, the Fourteenth Texas Cavalry Battalion attacked and killed 
2 CONC the majority of the fleeing immigrants.
2 CONT
2 CONT After the battle of the Nueces, the Fourteenth Texas Cavalry Battalion expanded to the Thirty-third Texas Cavalry Regiment with the introduction of several Tejano companies raised by https://www.geni.com/people/Colonel-Santos-Bena
2 CONC vides/6000000066162431946, a successful merchant, rancher, and former mayor of Laredo. The newly-organized regiment patrolled the Rio Grande and defended against raiding bandits and Unionists from Mexico. By November 1863 Benevida
2 CONC s received permission to organize a new contingent, Benavides's Regiment, and he was promoted from major to colonel. After the departure of Benavides, the Thirty-third was spread across South and Central Texas with Duff and five c
2 CONC ompanies stationed at Corpus Christi, one company camped at San Antonio, and the balance patrolling the lower Rio Grande. In April 1864 the regiment was removed to Bonham in North Texas because of supply shortages. By summer, the 
2 CONC Thirty-third joined Sam Bell Maxey's command. As Gano's Brigade returned to Texas after their raid into the Indian Territory that resulted in the Second Battle of Cabin Creek, General Maxey attached Colonel Duff's Thirty-third Tex
2 CONC as Cavalry to the brigade. These men, being fresh and rested, escorted Federal prisoners captured during the raid to Camp Ford in Tyler, Texas. As the war ended, the Thirty-third received a transfer to William P. Hardeman's Brigad
2 CONC e and finally disbanded in May 1865. [http://lifeofthecivilwar.blogspot.com/2012/08/union-germans-in-confederate-state.html]
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT ----
2 CONT ----
2 CONT '''Children''' 
2 CONT
