2 CONT Lancaster DNA SNP rs1006737 in the gene CACNA1C may cause Bi-Polar A few of the Lancaster cousins committed suicide, just a note further studies continue.  You might check for this snap in your DNA, not everyone inherits all the s
2 CONC ame. 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Lavaca County, Texas Biographies'''[http://www.lavacacountyhistory.org/biographies4.htm]
2 CONT
2 CONT '''DR. WILLIAM H. LANCASTER''' is an exceptionally popular and successful physician. He is scholarly and well informed in every branch of his profession, is intelligent and well posted on all matters of public interest, and stands
2 CONC  well in the community, both as a citizen and as a professional man. Although he has been a resident of this town only since 1887, he has already given abundant evidence of the ability which qualifies him for a high place in the m
2 CONC edical profession. He is a native of Bellefonte, Jackson County, Alabama, born in May 1849, and was the eldest of seven children born to John Nathaniel and Mary A. (Jones) Lancaster, natives respectively of Tennessee and Alabama. 
2 CONC The maternal grandfather, Moses Jones, was an early settler of Alabama, and an active man in the various occupations. He settled in Bellefonte, that State, purchased a large tract of land, and in connection, carried on merchandisi
2 CONC ng. For some time, he was Probate Judge, and he was an early editor, conducting a paper during the war. The Federal soldiers then came and destroyed all his property. One of his sons, Henry, was in the Confederate army and was Maj
2 CONC or of the Eleventh Alabama Regiment during the siege of Atlanta. There he lost his life. Mr. Jones died about 1873 or 1874. He was a highly active and prominent man in his community. The paternal grandfather of our subject married
2 CONC  in Tennessee and moved to North Alabama, where he purchased a large estate, but died soon after. His wife was a Miss Hudson, of a wealthy and influential family. After her husband's death, she married Judge T. M. Rector, a promin
2 CONC ent lawyer of North Alabama. She and her husband came to Texas in 1853, located east of Austin, and he farmed for the rest of his life, dying in 1893. Many of his relatives reside in this section. The grandmother died in February 
2 CONC 1867. The father of Dr. Lancaster was reared in Alabama, and he married Miss Jones. He followed farming there until 1856 when he came to Texas and settled in Travis County. He served four years in the Confederate Army. About 1884 
2 CONC he moved to Lockhart, and now resides in that town. Dr. W. H. Lancaster was seven years of age when he came with his parents to Texas, and he was educated at Manor, Texas. In 1873 he began the study of medicine in New Orleans, Tul
2 CONC ane University, and during the years 1875 and '76, attended Louisville Medical College, where he took his degree in the latter year. He then began practicing at Lockhart, remained there about ten years and one year at Red Rock, an
2 CONC d in 1887, came to Moulton, where he was among the first to locate. He assisted in surveying the town and bought the first lot sold in the place. Since then he has been in constant practice here, and he also opened the first drugs
2 CONC tore at this place. Dr. Lancaster is quite active in politics and is a frequent contributor to the paper in the vicinity. He took a post-graduate course at Tulane University during the winter of 1881 and '82 and is now a member of
2 CONC  the State Medical Association of which he is Third Vice-president. The doctor is a pleasant, genial gentleman and his large practice is constantly increasing. He is interested in the development and growth of this part of Texas, 
2 CONC and besides his pleasant place in Moulton, owns property in Jackson County. He is a Mason and Master of Moulton Lodge No. 298. Dr. Lancaster was married June 10, 1880, to Miss Linda Ella Williamson, a native of Alabama and daughte
2 CONC r of John H. Williamson of Lockhart. Four sons and one daughter are the fruits of this union: Moore, Lewis, Lifford Williamson, Bessie Edna, and Frank Houston. Dr. Lancaster and his wife a member of the Methodist Church. —pp.385-3
2 CONC 86 SW Texas History [http://www.lavacacountyhistory.org/biographies5.htm] [1] ref 
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Biography==
2 CONT '''William Horace Lancaster, MD''' was born on May 2, 1849, in Bellefonte, Jackson County, Alabama, United States. His parents were [https://www.geni.com/profile-34715964660 John Nathaniel Lancaster] and [https://www.geni.com/prof
2 CONC ile-34715964663 Mary Ann Lancaster (Jones)]. He was a Physician. 
2 CONT
2 CONT William married [https://www.geni.com/profile-34725329056 Linda Ella Lancaster] on June 10, 1880 in Lockhart, Caldwell County, Texas, United States. Together they had the following children:
2 CONT * [https://www.geni.com/profile-34716575060 Moore Lancaster]; 
2 CONT * [https://www.geni.com/profile-34725355185 William Moore Lancaster]; 
2 CONT * [https://www.geni.com/profile-34725355479 Bessie Edna Lancaster]; 
2 CONT * [https://www.geni.com/profile-34725329061 Lewis Kenneth Lancaster]; 
2 CONT * [https://www.geni.com/profile-34725329062 Lifford Williamson Lancaster, MD]; 
2 CONT * [https://www.geni.com/profile-34725329063 Frank Huston Lancaster, MD]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT He died on April 30, 1931, in Ganado, Jackson County, Texas, United States. 
2 CONT ----
2 CONT ----
2 CONT ----
2 CONT '''Profession'''
2 CONT
2 CONT * Physician and Surgeon, Moulton, Jackson County, Texas
2 CONT * Owned a Pharmacy and Mercantile Store in Moulton.  '''Memberships'''
2 CONT * Texas Medical Association, Vice President
2 CONT * Master Mason, Moulton Lodge #298, Jackson County, Texas '''Education'''
2 CONT * Tulane University, Master's degree 
2 CONT * University of Louisville Medical School, Louisville, KY. Medical Degree
