1 OCCU Marketing Analyst
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} September 2021
2 CONT
2 CONT In Memory of Emily 
2 CONT
2 CONT It's time for our family to start training for our 4th St. Jude Memphis Marathon! We will all do the half this year - in person!  Being in Memphis will bring back so many memories of times spent with Emily - hard times yet fun tim
2 CONC es as well.  We are determined to run for the kids and families of St. Jude and to help bring HOPE to them just as St. Jude gave hope to Emily and our family. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Please consider supporting us this year through a donation to St. Jude for Team Emily Strong. The link for Brenda's donation site is below.  We appreciate anything you can do! Now ... it's time for us to strap on our running shoes
2 CONC !
2 CONT
2 CONT https://fundraising.stjude.org/site/TR?px=5012924&pg=personal&fr_id=131089&copy_link_share
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Biography==
2 CONT '''Emily Ann Stephens''' was born on March 14, 1994, in Waco, McLennan County, Texas, United States of America. Her parents were [https://www.geni.com/profile-34747517172 John Andrew Stephens] and [https://www.geni.com/profile-347
2 CONC 53089698 Brenda Kay Pike]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT She died on January 23, 2018, in Memphis, Shelby County, Tennessee, United States of America. 
2 CONT ----
2 CONT ----
2 CONT ----
2 CONT NEWSCAST of her death. KWTX [[https://www.kwtx.com/content/news/Waco--Former-China-Spring-valedictorian-loses-long-battle-with-cancer-471698534.html]]
2 CONT
2 CONT WACO, Texas (KWTX) 
2 CONT
2 CONT Emily Stephens, 23, passed away Jan. 23 at St. Jude Children’s Research Hospital in Memphis, where the recent college graduate and 2012 Valedictorian of China Spring High School had been for the last few months of her life.
2 CONT
2 CONT “I remember a few weeks ago she was so tired and I said ‘don’t you want to stay in and rest’ and she said ‘nope got to keep living. Got to keep going.’ That was her nature. She just kept fighting and fighting and kept living,” her
2 CONC  parents, Brenda and John Stephens told KWTX.
2 CONT
2 CONT “She was really determined to not let this brain tumor dictate her life.”
2 CONT
2 CONT Cancer may have followed Stephens throughout her life, but it didn’t stop her from being involved in numerous community activities and even graduating from college with the highest of honors.
2 CONT
2 CONT Stephens was not only the valedictorian at China Spring High School in 2012 but also was captain of the dance team, editor of the yearbook and president of the Waco chapter of the National Charity League.
2 CONT
2 CONT “Even when she felt bad, she continued to live,” her mother said. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Emily was diagnosed with a brain stem tumor at the age of 4.
2 CONT
2 CONT She underwent surgery but doctors were not able to remove all of it.
2 CONT
2 CONT Her parents say she remained healthy until about the age of 12 when in seventh grade she underwent surgery again with the same result.
2 CONT
2 CONT When she was a high school sophomore, Stephens went to MD Anderson in Houston to undergo radiation and remained healthy until her sophomore year of college at the University of Texas.
2 CONT
2 CONT She then missed a semester of college to travel to St. Jude’s for treatment, but even that couldn’t knock her off track.
2 CONT
2 CONT “She went back to school that next January and took summer school to catch up and graduate with all her friends. And she graduated with high honors,” her mom proudly said.
2 CONT
2 CONT Following graduation, Emily took a job in Dallas as a marketing analyst for Frito Lay, which is where she was when her tumor unexpectedly hemorrhaged in early October.
2 CONT
2 CONT By November, she was back in St. Jude’s and planning to come home to Texas in mid-January when an MRI scan two days before discharge turned up bad news; the tumor had spread down the young woman’s spine.
2 CONT
2 CONT “She was so close to coming home and was so excited,” Brenda said.
2 CONT
2 CONT Emily’s parents say they would like to thank the amazing staff at St. Jude’s, particularly Dr. Amar Gajjar, who helped care for their daughter.
2 CONT
2 CONT The couple is now planning to help the hospital that helped them with so much by helping with a fundraiser this March in Waco for St. Jude’s, all in an effort to honor the memory of their beloved daughter who never gave up.
2 CONT
2 CONT “She’ll be remembered most for her wit and her humor and she loved her friends and her family,” her parents said through tears. “She loved everything about life. She had fun. She was kind to everybody and she lived life.”
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Education'''
2 CONT
2 CONT * Valedictorian at China Spring High School in 2012- captain of the dance team, editor of the yearbook and president of the Waco chapter of the National Charity League.
2 CONT * University of Texas, Austin 
2 CONT * McCombs School of Business in 2016 with honors
2 CONT
2 CONT  She was a member of Delta Delta Delta Sorority and worked in the Undergraduate Office of the business school.  She loved her UT experience and had friends from the many diverse areas of her campus life. She was then employed with
2 CONC  Frito- Lay in Plano as a marketing analyst until her illness made it impossible to work. 
2 CONT ----
