1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ==Biography==
2 CONT '''Sarah Elizabeth Nixon (Thomas)''' was born on July 2, 1808, in Caswell County, Davidson County, North Carolina, USA, Caswell County, North Carolina, United States. Her parents were [https://www.geni.com/profile-34717926073 Robe
2 CONC rt Thomas, I] and [https://www.geni.com/profile-34717926074 Margaret Thomas (Warrick)]. Her family were Methodist, she met and married Zachariah with out The Friends Approval of their union. A love match, he was willing to take th
2 CONC e chance of disassociation by the Quaker's, but he was a respected member, so they only reprimanded him, approved the marriage after the fact, Phineas Nixon and others were appointed to visit their home and report back at next mee
2 CONC ting. ref- [1]
2 CONT
2 CONT Sarah married [https://www.geni.com/profile-79564215 Zachariah Nixon] circa September 28, 1825, in Randolph, North Carolina, Randolph County, North Carolina, United States. Together they had the following children:
2 CONT * [https://www.geni.com/profile-34715961279 Captain (CSA), Robert Thomas Nixon];
2 CONT * [https://www.geni.com/profile-34717928405 Margaret Gaziell Bruton];
2 CONT * [https://www.geni.com/profile-34717928407 Stephen Decatur Nixon];
2 CONT * [https://www.geni.com/profile-34717928406 John Tinsley Nixon]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT She died in 1847 in Malboro, Randolph County, NC, United States, and was buried in Randolph County, North Carolina, United States. 
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT Her husband was a wealthy planter in Randolph County North Carolina who didn't believe in slavery, as his many of his Friends Society relatives raised money to send his slaves, his brother's slaves, his uncle's slaves, and many of
2 CONC  The Friend's Society Slaves were sent back to Haiti by ship verified by a Newspaper article when his brother, Phineas Nixon, III. Zach opposed slavery, returned slaves to Haiti in 1826, many years before the Civil War, [https://w
2 CONC ww.newspapers.com/clip/36333900/the_arkansas_gazette/] Phineas Nixon, III carried them back to their homeland. The Nixon's, although had slaves listed on Census Reports for generations, didn't believe in slavery, they were protect
2 CONC ing the slaves, paying them when they worked, and were not popular among other landowners around them. ---
2 CONT ----
2 CONT '''References'''*
2 CONT
2 CONT * Marriage -ref- [1]-[https://www.geni.com/documents/view?doc_id=6000000108487806821] They were married by a local minister, not approved by Friends Meeting before wedding, but accepted after, Phineas Nixon and other appointed to 
2 CONC visit home and report next monthly meeting. 
2 CONT
2 CONT *"Early Nixon's of Texas" by Pat Ireland Nixon, MD, Genealogies by Dr. and Mrs. Pat Ireland Nixon, Jr., Published by Carl Hertzog, El Paso, Texas 1956 [https://babel.hathitrust.org/cgi/pt?id=wu.89082553272&view=1up&seq=11] Note: a
2 CONC ll genealogy books have their mistakes please do your research, research and add documents. 
2 CONT
