1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ==Biography==
2 CONT '''Hallie Laura Nixon''' was born on August 16, 1893, in Medina County, Texas, USA. Her parents were [https://www.geni.com/profile-34715961283 John Phillip Nixon] and [https://www.geni.com/profile-34745353008 Dora Ann Wilson]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Hallie married [https://www.geni.com/profile-34745354717 Gladden Clyde McAnelly, Sr] on January 12, 1913, in Yancey, Medina, Texas, USA. Together they had the following children:
2 CONT * [https://www.geni.com/profile-34745354720 Dora Mae McAnelly];
2 CONT * [https://www.geni.com/profile-34745354723 Hallie Lorine McAnelly];
2 CONT * [https://www.geni.com/profile-34745354722 John Ernest McAnelly, Sr];
2 CONT * [https://www.geni.com/profile-34745354724 Gladden Clyde McAnelly, Junior];
2 CONT * [https://www.geni.com/profile-34745354721 James William McAnelly];
2 CONT * [https://www.geni.com/profile-34745354725 Charles Wayman McAnelly]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT She died on May 8, 1986, in Hondo, TX, and was buried in Yancey Cemetery, Yancey, Medina, Texas, USA. [https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/18003746]
2 CONT ----
2 CONT The McAnelly children were very well educated, most attending college, including a very young Texas A&M.  Gladden, was Valedictorian of his class in 1909 from Devine. Gladden was an excellent baseball pitcher – left-handed with a 
2 CONC tremendous curve and great fastball. When he graduated, he was drafted by some team in the then-fledgling Major League Baseball Association. His father, P.E., told him, “A man should not be playing a boy's game,” and would not let
2 CONC  him go. Unhappy with the decision, Gladden refused to go to college, married his childhood sweetheart, and started ranching.
2 CONT
2 CONT Gladden and Hallie lived in a ranch home that had no inside utilities. Hallie gave birth and raised seven children. She came from one of the wealthiest families in Texas and was used to all of the conveniences, but Hallie and Glad
2 CONC den were proud homeowners and wanted to build their fortune themselves. They were taught to work hard as children.
2 CONT
2 CONT Hallie was raised, on a large Texas ranch, the Texas Hill Country, people most likely thought, all the Nixon women were the typical southern debutantes.
2 CONT
2 CONT Attending social events, and wearing the latest Paris or New York fashions the Nixon daughters did not disappoint the crowds. They were members of the Junior League, Eastern Star, and many organizations.
2 CONT
2 CONT John Phillip Nixon decided if his children wanted to, they would attend the best prep schools, dance, music, foreign language, finishing schools, and colleges. This is where the typical debutante story ends.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Nixon girls were on horseback herding livestock and mending fences. Many became accomplished equestrians as did their grandchildren. They also learned alongside their brothers how to manage a large ranch business.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Nixon women could choose what profession and college degree they wanted, some decided to be homemakers and ranchers. This was the 2nd  generation of our family tradition of the East Coast Prep School, Philips Exeter.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Nixon daughters were treated equally to the Nixon sons. This was unusual for this time in history, but normal for the family for generations. They were originally from the Friend’s Religion, known as Quaker for several generat
2 CONC ions until their grandfather arrived in Texas and joined the Methodist Church.
2 CONT
2 CONT After working on the ranch, they would come home in in the evening to dress for a formal social affair, their choice if they wanted to attend, look like a debutante. If only others at the events had seen them hours earlier roping 
2 CONC calves, branding cattle, or cooking, cleaning beside the maids, only the Nixon women could do both successfully, and with grace.
2 CONT
2 CONT Hallie dearly loved Gladden since she was a young girl, grew up, and married him, THis was all she wanted in life, 
2 CONT ----
2 CONT * Reference:
2 CONT * [https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/18003746]
2 CONT * *"Early Nixon's of Texas" by Pat Ireland Nixon, MD, Genealogies by Dr. and Mrs. Pat Ireland Nixon, Jr., Published by Carl Hertzog, El Paso, Texas 1956 [https://babel.hathitrust.org/cgi/pt?id=wu.89082553272&view=1up&seq=11 Hathit
2 CONC rust]
2 CONT
2 CONT * [https://devinenews.com/the-mcanelly-family/]
2 CONT
