1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ==Biography==
2 CONT '''Mary Russell Field (Colvin)''' was born on February 8, 1818, in Culpeper, Culpeper, United States. Her parents were [https://www.geni.com/profile-55670342 James C. Colvin] and [https://www.geni.com/profile-126964283 Martha Poll
2 CONC y Colvin]. She was a Merchant. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Mary married [https://www.geni.com/profile-126964477 Henry Hill Field] on October 7, 1843, in Culpeper, Culpeper, Virginia, USA. Together they had the following children:
2 CONT * [https://www.geni.com/profile-34678278438 Martha Rosa Steele]; 
2 CONT * [https://www.geni.com/profile-34678278443 Frances Russell Trigg]; 
2 CONT * [https://www.geni.com/profile-34678278435 William Edward Field]; 
2 CONT * [https://www.geni.com/profile-34678278434 Mary Virginia V McNeal]; 
2 CONT * [https://www.geni.com/profile-34678278440 Albert Francis Field]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT She died on January 24, 1885, in Lockhart, Caldwell County, TX, United States, and was buried in Lockhart Municipal Burial Park, Lockhart, Caldwell, Texas. [https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/31423568]
2 CONT ----
2 CONT ----
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Mary Russell Field (Colvin)'''  husband, Henry Hill Field was a Merchant and large Plantation owner in Texas. He moved with his first wife, Diana Field (Slaughter) to Eufaula, Alabama, until her death, February 19, 1838. He the
2 CONC n moved back to Culpeper County, Virginia for several years. He sold his home in Eufaula he shared with his first wife in 1842, but in the deed, Mr. Field Reserved Ten Square Feet of the lot, which contained the graves of his firs
2 CONC t wife and child. He moved back with Mary Russell Field (Colvin) and family by 1848, where they lived until 1855 when they moved to Lockhart, Caldwell County, Texas for the remainder of their lives. After her husband's death, Mary
2 CONC  took over his Merchant business and successfully running of the Plantation until her sons were old enough to step into the business.  She didn't remarry, she was very independent and wanted to keep her property in her name and he
2 CONC r children's control if she would've remarried during this era all of her property would've been in her new husband's control. None of our maternal grandmother's remarried, I was told for this reason. She was an astute businesswom
2 CONC an passing down this knowledge to her daughters and sons. The Field family sold the land over time and much of the land that was part of the plantation is the town of Lockhart, Texas today.  
2 CONT
2 CONT The stained glass windows at the Emmanuel Episcopal Church has a plaque in the memory of  Field Family name dedicated and donated by the Field family members. [(ref [https://www.americantowns.com/place/emmanuel-episcopal-church-lo
2 CONC ckhart-tx.html [http://www.eeclockhart.org/history.html] F]
2 CONT
2 CONT '''References'''
2 CONT
2 CONT [https://www.familysearch.org/library/books/records/?navigation=&perpage=&page=1&sort=_score&search=Lancaster+From+Virginia+to+Texas&fulltext=1&bookmarks=0 Lancasters From Virginia to Texas - Family Search Library]
2 CONT
2 CONT * [https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/31423568] All graves of the Field Family are well-maintained at the Lockhart Municipal Cemetery in Lockhart, Texas. 
2 CONT * Field Genealogy - ”Lancaster From Virginia to Texas” Mary Nixon Rogers
2 CONT * Eufaula Alabama Historical Homes Registry. 
2 CONT * Culpeper County Records 
2 CONT * ‘’’https://en.m.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lockhart,_Texas’’’
2 CONT * [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eufaula,_Alabama]
2 CONT * Field Genealogy referenced he owned 50 slaves, but didn't name them, need probated Will Caldwell,  Texas, around the time of his death, Jan. 10, 1858. A copy may be in ”Lancaster From Virginia to Texas” genealogy book referenced
2 CONC  above. 
2 CONT * '''Residence''': 1860 - [Blank], Caldwell, Texas, USA
