1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ==Biography==
2 CONT '''Rebecca Wyatt Trigg''' was born in 1821 in Augusta County, Virginia, United States. Her parents were [https://www.geni.com/profile-34746309017 Wyatt Bingham] and [https://www.geni.com/profile-34746309016 Rebecca Bingham]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Rebecca married [https://www.geni.com/profile-34715937635 John Anthony Trigg] on Jan 1, 1838, in Saline, Missouri, USA, Saline County, Missouri, United States. Together they had the following children: 
2 CONT *[https://www.geni.com/profile-34717967761 Pvt. (CSA), William T. Trigg];
2 CONT * [https://www.geni.com/profile-34717967761 William Trigg]; 
2 CONT * [https://www.geni.com/profile-34678278759 Colonel Bingham Trigg];
2 CONT * [https://www.geni.com/profile-34724112029 Colonel John C. Trigg];
2 CONT * [https://www.geni.com/profile-34717967760 Mary Rebecca Trigg];
2 CONT * [https://www.geni.com/profile-34717967763 Abraham Trigg]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT She died in October 1851 and was buried in Smith Memorial Methodist Church Cemetery, Marshall, Saline County while her husband was away during the California Gold Rush, so the public stories have stated, but perhaps it was his bru
2 CONC ised ego, that her father made a point to leave him out of his probated will.
2 CONT ----
2 CONT According to Saline County records Judge John Anthony Trigg left for California in 1849, appointing William A. Wilson as his Deputy in the District Clerk position of Marshal County, Missouri. By 1850, he was still in California, n
2 CONC ot planning to return that year, so he resigned from office and didn't serve between 1850-1853.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was away from his family from the spring of 1849 until just before Feb 19, 1852, when we know he is in the local news again giving a speech.
2 CONT
2 CONT The three-year absence when John was in California prospecting for gold, not writing home to his family. When he left to prospect, Rebecca W. Trigg, was due to give birth to their fifth child, Abraham Trigg, born May 12, 1849. Reb
2 CONC ecca was left with five children ages newborn, 3, 5, 7, and 11 years old. She was ill when he left, he never wrote the family, and for three years they didn't know where he was or how to contact him When he returned, she had been 
2 CONC deceased for over a year, she passed away in October 1851. It was said he was planning to move his family to California after he returned, but after Rebecca's death, it was said he was heartbroken and changed his plans. The younge
2 CONC r children didn't know or remember him, and the two older sons were unhappy he left their mother and never wrote them. The rift between all of the children and their father lasted a lifetime.
2 CONT
2 CONT Rebecca's father's Probated Will in May of 1849 was unusual for the time, he left his entire estate and money to his wife, and after her death, it would descend to his daughter, Rebecca Wyatt Trigg, when she was deceased the estat
2 CONC e would go to her natural born children. Also, no provision for the son-in-law, or that he would manage the estate or the money of the estate. This insulted her husband, in his last will he made a point of stating the fact her chi
2 CONC ldren inherited quite a sum and, therefore needed no more. Her dowry was quite a gift of land to John Anthony Trigg along with money, her sons did contest their father's will and received their share of the land in the end. [https
2 CONC ://www.geni.com/documents/view?doc_id=6000000113734512854 Judge Wyatt Bingham left out his son-in-law ] Judge John Anthony Bingham was in charge of probating all wills in Saline County at the time. John Trigg left for California a
2 CONC  short time after Wyatt Bingham's death and didn't return until after Rebecca's death. 
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT '''References''' 
2 CONT * Probated Will of Wyatt Bingham [https://www.geni.com/documents/view?doc_id=6000000113602884892 Wyatt Bingham 1849]
2 CONT * Lancaster From Virginia To Texas by Mary Nixon Rogers, Trigg Bingham Genealogy.  (note: Ancestry.com and a few online biographies have her parents wrong. The Probated Will of Wyatt Bingham proves he and Rebecca Bingham (Bingham)
2 CONC  were the parents of Rebecca Wyatt Trigg (Bingham) without a doubt.  
2 CONT * https://familysearch.org/ark:/61903/4:1:MB3T-ZT7
2 CONT * '''Residence''': 1850 - Saline County, Saline, Missouri, United States
