1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ''' Not to be confused with John David Trigg''' b  10 JULY 1815 • Pittsylvania, Virginia, USA died 2 JUNE 1891 • La Cygne, Linn County, Kansas, United States, married Elizabeth Jordan, Ancestry.com Thru-Lines match
2 CONC ed DNA and confused the link to John Anthony Trigg. John David Trigg's parents were  William Fleming Trigg and Suzanne Ditty.
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Biography==
2 CONT '''Judge John Anthony Trigg''' was born on April 5, 1813, in Campbell, Virginia, USA, Campbell, Albemarle County, Virginia, United States. His parents were [https://www.geni.com/profile-34660284339 William Trigg] and [https://www.
2 CONC geni.com/profile-34660284362 Sarah Doshia Anthony]. He was a District Judge, District Clerk, and Farmer. 
2 CONT
2 CONT According to Saline County records, he left for California in 1849, appointing William A. Wilson as his Deputy in the District Clerk position of Marshal County, Missouri. By 1850, he was still in California,  not planning to retur
2 CONC n that year he resigned from office and didn't serve between 1850-1853.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was away from his family from the spring of 1849 until just before Feb 19, 1852, when we know he is in the local news again, giving a speech. It was said he went to California to join a group in gold mining. We do find one ment
2 CONC ion of him in a shipwreck in Vera Cruz, Mexico, en route to New Orleans, LA, on March 22, 1850, with a $30,000. shipment, [https://www.geni.com/documents/view?doc_id=6000000225094289961 Shipwreck of Brig Titi - Veracruz, MX ]. He 
2 CONC survived the wreck. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The three-year absence when he was in California prospecting for gold, not writing home to his family. When he left to prospect, his first wife [https://www.geni.com/profile-34715937591 Rebecca W. Trigg], was due to give birth to 
2 CONC their fifth child,  [https://www.geni.com/profile-34717967763 Abraham Trigg], born May 12, 1849. Rebecca was left with five children ages newborn, 3, 5, 7, and 11 years old. She was ill when he left for California, he never wrote 
2 CONC the family, and for three years they didn't know where he was or how to contact him  When he returned, his beloved wife had been deceased for over a year. It was said he was planning to move his family to California after he retur
2 CONC ned, but after Rebecca's death, he was heartbroken and changed his plans. The younger children didn't know or remember him, and the two older sons were unhappy he left their mother and never wrote them. 
2 CONT
2 CONT John married [https://www.geni.com/profile-34715937591 Rebecca W. Trigg] on December 27, 1835, in Saline, Missouri, USA, Saline County, Missouri, United States. While he was away during the California Gold Rush, she passed away in
2 CONC  October 1851.  Together they had the following children:
2 CONT
2 CONT * [https://www.geni.com/profile-34717967761 William Trigg]; 
2 CONT * [https://www.geni.com/profile-34678278759 Colonel Bingham Trigg]; 
2 CONT * [https://www.geni.com/profile-34724112029 Colonel John C. Trigg]; 
2 CONT * [https://www.geni.com/profile-34717967760 Mary Rebecca Trigg]; 
2 CONT * [https://www.geni.com/profile-34717967763 Abraham Trigg]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT John married [https://www.geni.com/profile-34717967747 Amanda H. Trigg] on  Dec 27, 1852. Together they had the following children:
2 CONT * [https://www.geni.com/profile-34717967765 Theodosia Cooney]; 
2 CONT * [https://www.geni.com/profile-34717967764 Abner J. Trigg]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT He died on March 4, 1872, in Marshall, Saline, Missouri, USA, Marshall Township, Saline County, Missouri, United States, and, was buried, in Marshall, Saline County, Missouri, United States of America, Marshall, Saline County, Mis
2 CONC souri, United States. 
2 CONT ---'
2 CONT ---- 
2 CONT '''Addional Information'''
2 CONT
2 CONT [https://www.geni.com/documents/view?doc_id=6000000104230741821& News Article Trigg vs Trigg] John Anthony Trigg's sons tied the Estate up for years after his death, they disputed the Will in Trigg vs Trigg. [https://www.geni.com/
2 CONC profile-34678278759 Colonel Bingham Trigg]; was a District Attorney in Texas and another brother; [https://www.geni.com/profile-34724112029 Colonel John C. Trigg]; was a Judge in the State of Missouri. Finally, their stepmother se
2 CONC ttled out of court on August 15, 1880, with an agreement all of the children would receive an equal share of the land in Saline County, Missouri. The famous transcribed Will is below, but the sons had the last word.
2 CONT ----
2 CONT ----
2 CONT He worked as a Judge until the Civil War got into full force, then he joined the Union side as did most of the family and his wife's Bingham Family.  Shocking to him was his oldest son by his beloved first wife; [https://www.geni.
2 CONC com/profile-34715937591 Rebecca W. Trigg] chose to support the "Confederate" side. He re-wrote his will and left each son $1 and Bingham only 10 cents. It was a family legend the young men chose the Confederate Army because they t
2 CONC hought their father was a hypocrite to join the Union side. After all, he owned slaves. The young sons didn't support owning slaves, they saw many men in Missouri joining the Union that owned slaves, and they considered them all h
2 CONC ypocrites. Bingham Trigg in later years wrote and presented a petition to provide equal education as written by The Constitution for all children. He was upset they didn't have the opportunities his children had, so they could bec
2 CONC ome doctors, attorneys, etc. Sadly, their father died and they never spoke to him before his death.
2 CONT ----
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT His sons always maintained the problems with their father started well before the war and their father's slave ownership. It was their father's treatment of their beloved mother, Mary Rebecca Trigg Bingham. He left her with four c
2 CONC hildren when she was within two months of giving birth to the 5th child. she was not well, and he didn't send a letter to check on their mother. When she passed away they had to bury their mother without him and they didn't know w
2 CONC here to send a letter about her death. He finally returned home long after her death, he was so distraught over her death that he changed his plans about moving the family to California to continue the gold venture. He remarried t
2 CONC he same year he returned and found out about his wife's death most likely knowing he had five young children without a mother. 
2 CONT ----
