1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ==Biography==
2 CONT '''Colonel Bingham Trigg Kleine''' was born on November 10, 1911, in Gonzales, Gonzales County, Texas, USA, Gonzales County, Texas, United States. His parents were [https://www.geni.com/profile-34678301349 William C. Kleine] and [
2 CONC https://www.geni.com/profile-34678278764 Julia Bingham Kleine Cauthorn].  He was named in honor of his maternal grandfather, Colonel Bingham Trigg, and appeared to follow his grandfather's service legacy. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Bingham married [https://www.geni.com/profile-34764814761 Jane Hendrick O'Neal] on January 14, 1937, in Shreveport, Caddo, Louisiana, USA. They had two children. 
2 CONT
2 CONT He died on February 18, 1984, in Shreveport, Caddo Parish, Louisiana, USA, Shreveport, Caddo Parish, Louisiana, United States, and was buried in Shreveport, Caddo Parish, Louisiana, United States of America.  --
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Colonel Bing Kleine was theColonel Bing Kleine, Commanding Officer of the 371st Fighter Group in World War II.- Europe in World War II.'''
2 CONT
2 CONT ''' The 371st Fighter Group on D-Day, June 6, 1944''' '''Happy Birthday, 371st Fighter Group!''' Posted on July 14, 2013, by 371fg It was seventy years ago now, on July 15th, 1943, that the 371st Fighter Group was activated at Ric
2 CONC hmond Army Air Base in Virginia, “…pursuant to the authority contained in General Order #52, Headquarters I Fighter Command, dated 12 June 1943.”  The huge wartime buildup of American airpower for combat in a global conflict was w
2 CONC ell underway at that time.  As such, the 371st Fighter Group’s existence began modestly. 
2 CONT
2 CONT On this date of activation, the unit was temporarily commanded by First Lieutenant Leon Bilstein.  As the acting commander, his newly established unit numbered nine officers and 165 enlisted men, and no assigned aircraft.  Of thos
2 CONC e assigned at activation on July 15, seven officers and 49 enlisted men were off station on detached service at the Fighter Key Personnel Cadre Course in Orlando, Florida.  The group’s full complement would eventually number some 
2 CONC 149 officers, 728 enlisted men, and over seventy P-47 Thunderbolt aircraft, according to the pertinent table of organization and equipment for a single-engine fighter group.
2 CONT
2 CONT The new group set up its first headquarters in a corner of the 327th Fighter Group’s headquarters building.  Scant days later, the group moved for the first time, on July 19, to building T-2743.  A week later, on July 26, Captain 
2 CONC Edmund A. Goolsbee replaced Lt. Bilstein as acting commander. 
2 CONT
2 CONT But the” Big Wheel” arrived the next day, July 27, when Lt. Col. Bingham Trigg “Bing” Kleine became the 371st Fighter Group’s first and only wartime commander, assumed command.  He was from Gonzales, Texas; married, father of one.
2 CONC   Col. Kleine was a Regular Army officer, and an experienced senior pilot, having earned his wings at Randolph Field in October 1935.  He was fresh from a brief command of the Boston Air Defense Wing and quickly brought his vast e
2 CONC xperience to bear in building up the group.  In less than eight months the 371st Fighter Group would be trained, deployed, and in place in England, ready to engage in aerial combat with the enemy over Northwestern Europe.
2 CONT
2 CONT Colonel Bingham T. "Bing" Kleine, U.S. Army Air Corps, Commanding Officer of the 371st Fighter Group during World War II. ''''''References'''''' []https://371stfightergroup.wordpress.com/2013/07/14/happy-birthday-371st-fighter-gro
2 CONC up/[]
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT '''The French Farm Girl of the Flying Field: Yvette Hamel and the 371st Fighter Group'''
2 CONT
2 CONT By Lt. Col. Terrence Popravak, Jr. (ret.), 142nd Fighter Wing Public Affairs History / Published August 28, 2013
2 CONT
2 CONT PORTLAND AIR NATIONAL BASE, Ore -- It was the summer of 1944 in Normandy, France. In June, Allied forces had successfully landed on the European continent and began the great campaign across northwestern Europe to defeat Nazi Germ
2 CONC any. Within days of the landing, aviation engineers carved out advanced landing grounds (ALGs) to be used by forward-deploying tactical air forces. One of the first, if not the very first, fighter outfits to be based in France was
2 CONC  the 371st Fighter Group (371FG).
2 CONT
2 CONT For those who may wonder what the 371FG has to do with the Oregon Air National Guard's 142d Fighter Wing, the 371FG was redesignated after the war as the 142d Fighter Group and allotted to the state of Oregon. The 142d inherited t
2 CONC he lineage and honors of the 371FG and is currently designated as the 142d Fighter Wing.
2 CONT
2 CONT But in the summer of 1944, the unit was a fighter group equipped with the Republic P-47 Thunderbolt. Based at ALG-6 (A-6), Beuzeville Airfield, the 371FG employed this aircraft effectively; its pilots and ground crews helped batte
2 CONC r German forces attempting to contain the Allied foothold on the continent. Of note, A-6, also known as La Londe, was adjacent to the famous village of Sainte-Mère-Église.
2 CONT
2 CONT On July 15, 1944, after nearly a month in action at A-6, the group commander, Colonel Bingham T. "Bing" Kleine, took advantage of some less than optimum flying weather and had a first birthday party for the group on the green of t
2 CONC he chateau that served as group HQ. It was at this birthday party that Col Kleine announced another "first" for the 371st; that the group would become a "father" to a young French farm girl named Yvette Hamel.
2 CONT
2 CONT How the 371FG came to "adopt" 16-year-old Mademoiselle Yvette Hamel is a tale of overcoming adversity. In early July 1944, Yvette was milking cows with her sister on the family farm in Normandy, which was being used by an American
2 CONC  artillery unit. But on that fateful day, she was grievously wounded by German artillery and nearly died - her sister was killed. US Army medics provided first aid, saved her life, and transported her to the Army's 67th Evacuation
2 CONC  Hospital, a field hospital at La Fière, about four miles west of Sainte-Mère-Église. Surgeons there were forced by the extent of her wounds to amputate both of her legs; they also worked to save her shattered right arm. Yvette be
2 CONC gan her recuperation in this field hospital amidst wounded soldiers, as no French hospital was available or equipped to take care of her serious and still life-threatening wounds.
2 CONT
2 CONT But the 67th Evacuation Hospital soon had to move on and could not bring Yvette. However, a fortuitous meeting of Col Kleine and the hospital's staff resulted in the 371st at nearby A-6 taking over responsibility for helping in Yv
2 CONC ette's recovery. There are probably few combat units in the annals of warfare that assumed such a humanitarian responsibility, albeit one that gave many group members a greater motivation to do their utmost to fly, fight, and win.
2 CONT
2 CONT Yvette recuperated with the group for several months, being treated royally by unit members, and learning "GI English" in the process. The men pitched a tent for her exclusive use, lined with parachute silk to brighten the interio
2 CONC r. The group medical staff took care of her medical needs and monitored her condition. The group dentist fashioned a special chair for her to recline during the day. Pilots, ground crews, and even the cook made her feel welcome, v
2 CONC isiting her and doting upon her creatively as best they could in field conditions. The French Red Cross came to help too: a pair of French sisters separated from their families volunteered to assist. While Yvette rested and regain
2 CONC ed her strength, the war continued, as the group hammered the enemy in Normandy, engaged in the breakout from the Cotentin Peninsula by Patton's Third Army (Operation Cobra), and then supported that army's epic and swift movement 
2 CONC across France.
2 CONT
2 CONT In addition to direct care, group members took up a couple of collections to help with Yvette's future medical needs. The first collection of $2,000 was placed with Col Kleine pending the time Yvette needed it to help with her rec
2 CONC overy and procuring artificial legs. A second collection taken near the end of the war provided $3,000 more in assistance for her needs.
2 CONT
2 CONT When the group moved eastward 300 miles to Perthes Airfield (ALG A-65, also called St. Dizier) in late September 1944, to keep up with General Patton, Yvette was packed aboard a C-47 transport plane. "She traveled with us as we mo
2 CONC ved around France as 'miscellaneous parts,'" remembers 404th Fighter Squadron P-47 pilot Willis R. "Wally" Walling, of Pawleys Island, South Carolina. Yvette's condition continued to improve, and her presence was a morale booster 
2 CONC and motivator for the group; Yvette was "really a great mascot," said Walling, who recalled that she stayed with the group "...up till (after) we moved to St. Dizier."
2 CONT
2 CONT The group was not long at A-65, however. The western front slowed near the German frontier as enemy resistance increased. By the end of September, the 371st was ordered south to support the US Seventh Army, which was approaching t
2 CONC he western front, coming up the Rhone River valley from southern France. Yvette did not accompany the group on this move to the next flying field at Dole-Tavaux (ALG Y-7), which was initially planned as a short deployment of ten d
2 CONC ays. Many group personnel remained at A-65 into mid-November, by which time all ended up at Dole as that deployment was extended. This all occurred as the time neared for Yvette to undergo further surgeries in preparation for rece
2 CONC iving prosthetic devices, which group members had collected money to buy for her. Group staff, however, particularly the medical section, were still involved with her follow-on care at other military facilities as the group prepar
2 CONC ed for the move to Y-7.
2 CONT
2 CONT By early December, General Eisenhower himself reportedly became aware of Yvette and decided her days of moving around as miscellaneous cargo for the benefit of the 371st were over; but he offered her an option of going to the U.S.
2 CONC , or remaining in France, for further medical care. Away from her family in Normandy and faced with having to make a quick decision, she decided to remain in France; the group continued with the war, while Yvette went to Paris for
2 CONC  reconstructive surgery and the next phase in her rehabilitation battle.
2 CONT
2 CONT After the war, Yvette gradually overcame her disability, married, and had a family. Group veterans kept in touch with her, and in 1980, Yvette came to the United States to join some 371FG pilots at a P-47 Thunderbolt Pilots Associ
2 CONC ation reunion held in Lake Charles, Louisiana. In the 1990s, several group members returned to France and met with her again.
2 CONT
2 CONT Yvette Hamel passed away in 2007, but not before her story inspired a nicely-written novel based on her life called "Sunward I've Climbed" by author Annie Laurie Morgan. It was published in 1994, in time for the 50th anniversary o
2 CONC f D-Day. It conveys a better appreciation of her struggle, and the positive impact members of the 371FG had on her, and that she had on the 371st Fighter Group.
2 CONT
2 CONT The story also reportedly found its way into the well-known Airborne Museum at Sainte-Mère-Église, which is said to feature a display sharing this remarkable story.
2 CONT
2 CONT Yvette Hamel lived through a traumatic period of history and was dreadfully affected by it. But with determination, she overcame her grievous wounds and endeared herself to a generation of GIs as she did. Although she is gone now,
2 CONC  she remains in the hearts of 371FG veterans and is part of the rich history of the 142d Fighter Wing. Her example of grit and grace from World War II is worthy of reflection today.
2 CONT
2 CONT [https://www.142fw.ang.af.mil/News/Features/Display/Article/864399/the-french-farm-girl-of-the-flying-field-yvette-hamel-and-the-371st-fighter-gro/] Photos of Yvette Hamel and the 371st 
2 CONT
