1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ''''''Moses Jones'''''' was born circa 1801 in Tennessee, United States. He was a Probate Judge, Plantation owner, Paper Mill Owner, Newspaper owner. 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Judge Moses Jones''' was an early settler of Alabama, was an active man in various professions. When he settled in Bellefonte, Jackson County, he purchased a large tract of land and built a large plantation home and paper mill.
2 CONC  He was also a successful merchant and owned a Newspaper named "The Bellefonte Era" until  Federal Troops under Major General Ormsby MacKnight Mitchel burned the Paper Mill and newsprint during the Civil War, along with the planta
2 CONC tion home during the month of April 1862 The General came into Alabama was able to seize Huntsville and the towns around without firing one a shot, this Federal Troop leader was a genius.[1] ref. 
2 CONT
2 CONT At the end of Moses Jones life after the Civil War, as many of the citizens of Jackson County, Alabama he lost his fortune, he was in financial ruin after a lifetime of success, his pride kept him from following his children to Te
2 CONC xas, he died trying to re-build his life in Alabama. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Bellefonte, Alabama is now a Ghost town was once a growing and beautiful city before the Civil War, but it was severely impacted by the war and was just about completely wiped out by the end of the war. The relocation of the count
2 CONC y seat in 1868 drew off business from town. This town rapidly lost population, dropping off the census rolls by 1880. The town was abandoned by the 1920s. All that remains of Bellefonte today are the cemetery, the chimney of the l
2 CONC ocal inn, and piles of scattered bricks. (2) A direct descendant of Moses Jones, Miss Ethel Acklin, in a letter in 1959, stated" I was told as a child that Moses Jones published articles in favor of the South and criticized the No
2 CONC rth to such an extent that he was a "Hunted Man" when the North invaded his part of Alabama, he and his family had to flee to his mountain home, at Jonesville, Alabama. His Plantation was taken over as headquarters for the Union s
2 CONC oldiers and it was burned later when the soldiers moved South. An old servant of Moses Jones who stayed behind to look over the property, hid the silver, Confederate Money, and other valuables under the kitchen," (Miss Acklin, pas
2 CONC sed away in January 1970 in Austin, Travis, County, Texas.) 
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Biography==
2 CONT '''Judge Moses Jones''' was born in 1803 in Tennessee, United States. His parents were [https://www.geni.com/profile-34639952352 Moses Jones, I] and [https://www.geni.com/profile-34746821007 Delilah Jones (Derrick)]. He was a Plan
2 CONC tation owner, Paper Mill Owner, Newspaper owner. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Moses married [https://www.geni.com/profile-34722847490 Delilah Jones (Armbrister)] in 1828 Bellefonte, Jackson County, Alabama. US. Together they had the following children:
2 CONT * [https://www.geni.com/profile-34715964663 Mary Ann Lancaster (Jones)];
2 CONT * [https://www.geni.com/profile-34746821012 John W Jones];
2 CONT * [https://www.geni.com/profile-34746821014 Hettie Melissa Jones];
2 CONT * [https://www.geni.com/profile-34746821011 Joseph H Jones];
2 CONT * [https://www.geni.com/profile-34746821013 Horace Jones];
2 CONT * [https://www.geni.com/profile-34746821015 Laura F Jones];
2 CONT * [https://www.geni.com/profile-34746821017 Maria Eleanor Jones]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT He died circa 1874 in Alabama, USA, Bellefonte, Jackson County, AL, United States. 
2 CONT ---
2 CONT
