1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ==Biography==
2 CONT '''Lucy Ann Lancaster (Hudson)''' was born on June 3, 1803, in Prince Edward County, Virginia, United States. Her parents were [https://www.geni.com/profile-34688527390 Captain John Hudson] and [https://www.geni.com/profile-347159
2 CONC 64891 Lucy Hudson]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Lucy married [https://www.geni.com/profile-34715964807 Josiah Lancaster] in 1823 in Tennessee, United States. Together they had the following children:
2 CONT * [https://www.geni.com/profile-34715964811 Ann Drucilla Wildbahn]; 
2 CONT * [https://www.geni.com/profile-34715964660 John Nathaniel Lancaster]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1853, Thompson Mason and Laura Rector were among the last members of the family to leave Alabama. He sold his wife's land and his land and merged all assets. Then they moved to Texas, settling in Travis County with the combined
2 CONC  assets of the Hudson/Baker and Lancaster inheritances his wife brought into the marriage. With her wealth, they built a successful ranch and farm, so successful that he never returned to practicing law. When his wife died in 1867
2 CONC , he arranged for her two children born to her first husband. to receive equal shares in her estate, but before his death, all land was transferred directly to his natural sons, so her children didn't receive any part of the Lanca
2 CONC ster, Hudson, and Baker inheritance or any part of the Rector Estate. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Lucy married [https://www.geni.com/profile-49794896 Thomas Mason Rector I] in 1831  in Bellefonte, Alabama. Together they had the following children:
2 CONT * [https://www.geni.com/profile-34715964897 Catherine Elizabeth Rector]; 
2 CONT * [https://www.geni.com/profile-34725328546 Benjamin Fieldon Rector]; 
2 CONT * [https://www.geni.com/profile-34715964904 Thompson Mason Rector II]; 
2 CONT * [https://www.geni.com/profile-34715964896 Lucy Jane Rector]; 
2 CONT * [https://www.geni.com/profile-34725328549 Landoria Madaline Rector]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT She died on February 15, 1867, and is buried in the Rector Family Section of The Manor Cemetery in Manor, Travis County, Texas, USA. 
2 CONT ----
2 CONT '''The Lancaster- Hudson -Baker Estates'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Lucy Ann Hudson Lancaster, a wealthy widow, acquired quite a large inheritance from the Hudson/ Baker and Lancaster families, She brought wealth to Judge Thompson Mason Rector when they married and he promised to manage it well fo
2 CONC r her and her two children Ann Drucilla and John Nathaniel Lancaster for their future Estate, It was to be willed to the Lancaster natural born children when she passed away.  The land and assets were to be kept in the Lancaster n
2 CONC ame in a Trust for the children, but Judge Thompson Mason Rector was the probate attorney and managed her and the Lancaster Children's estate. He later married her. 
2 CONT
2 CONT  When she died, he didn't probate the Lancaster Children's Estate, between the time she died, and before he passed away, he had transferred all of his vast properties and business her purchased with his wife Lucy Ann Hudson Rector
2 CONC 's wealth. Lucy's two children from her first marriage didn't inherit the Lancaster, Baker Hudson wealth.  All was transferred over a few years to their Rector brother's names. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Despite not inheriting as they should have, John Nathaniel Lancaster and his sister Ann Drucilla Lancaster  Wildbahn's children, grandchildren, and great-grandchildren were all well-educated, well-known, successful Texans.  
2 CONT ----
2 CONT ----
2 CONT   Both the Hudson's and Baker's lineages are well documented. She married 1) Josiah Lancaster,  son of John Lancaster and Margretta Drucilla Lancaster (LeGrand).  They moved to Madison County, Alabama, in 1823. After his death in 
2 CONC 1834, she married Judge Thomas Mason Rector in 1835 in Jackson County, Alabama. 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''References'''
2 CONT
2 CONT _Listed as second Child born to John and Drucilla Lancaster (Legrand) in Lancaster Family Bible- copy attached - Signed as Surety on Marriage of Sister Judith Baker Lancaster, to George W. Gills, July 22, 1814 Lucy Ann Lancaster (
2 CONC Hudson) is listed in Caleb Baker, Jr's Will as Lucy Hudson (Baker's) heir 
2 CONT
2 CONT A very well-documented family, land deeds, Wills, and Family published  Genealogy Books copies and attributes attached. Many DAR Patriots in this lineage and not a Quaker lineage.
2 CONT
2 CONT _all copies pertaining to this lineage are attached with attributes.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Reference''' '''''' Please note the slave names could change between ownership, from Baker to Husdon,  '''Inherited Slaves also from grandfather's estate''' Names of Children and grandchildren from Will of Caleb Baker (Jr), Pri
2 CONC nce Edward County Records, Will Book 6, p. 60; Marriages from Prince Edward County, Virginia, Marriage Records and Chancery Court Records, Prince Edward County, August 1826 Court; also see Roster and Soldiers, op, cit., “ Virginia
2 CONC  Magazine of History and Biography”, Vol. 18, pg 42 
2 CONT
