1 NOTE {geni:about_me} [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zeng_Guofan '''Zeng Guofan''', Marquis '''Yiyong'''] (Chinese: 曾國藩 pinyin: Zēng Guófān), birth name '''Zeng Zicheng''', courtesy name '''Bohan''', was a Chinese statesman, military ge
2 CONC neral, and Confucian scholar of the late Qing dynasty. He is best known for raising and organizing the Xiang Army to aid the Qing military in suppressing the Taiping Rebellion and restoring the stability of the Qing Empire. Along 
2 CONC with other prominent figures such as Zuo Zongtang and Li Hongzhang, Zeng set the scene for the Tongzhi Restoration, an attempt to arrest the decline of the Qing dynasty. Zeng was known for his strategic perception, administrative 
2 CONC skill and noble personality on Confucianist practice, but also for the ruthlessness of his repression of the rebellion. He exemplified loyalty in an era of chaos, but is also regarded as a precursor to the rise of warlordism.
2 CONT
2 CONT ---------------
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Tsêng Kuo-fan''' 曾國藩 (T. 伯涵, H. 滌生), Nov. 26, 1811-1872, Mar. 12, statesman, general and scholar, the first '''Marquis I-yung''' (毅勇侯), was a native of Hsiang-hsiang, Hunan. He was born in a poor peasant family and in his youth
2 CONC  was much influenced, in his characteristic tendencies and habits of thought, by his grandfather, [https://www.geni.com/people/ZYP/6000000013868986438 Tsêng Yü-p'ing 曾玉屏] (T. 星岡, 1774-1849). His father, [https://www.geni.com/peopl
2 CONC e/ZLS/6000000012827447629 Tsêng Lin-shu 曾麟書] (T. 竹亭, 1790-1857), became a ''hsiu-ts'ai'' in 1832—a year before [https://www.geni.com/people/ZGF/6000000012827488681 Tsêng Kuo-fan] himself obtained the same degree. [https://www.geni
2 CONC .com/people/ZGF/6000000012827488681 Tsêng Kuo-fan] was a ''chin-shih'' of 1838 and in June of the same year became a member of the Hanlin Academy. At the capital, he pursued his studies with great tenacity of purpose and profited 
2 CONC by his contacts with noted contemporary scholars. After routine promotions he was appointed, in 1849, junior vice-president of the Board of Ceremonies. At different times he served as acting vice-president on several other Boards 
2 CONC and thus gained wide knowledge of state affairs. This experience enabled him, in his later memorials to the throne, to make practical proposals and to frame them with great clarity and precision. In 1852 he was sent to conduct the
2 CONC  provincial examination of Kiangsi, but learning, on his way south, of the death of his mother, he was granted leave to return home to observe the customary mourning period. 
2 CONT
2 CONT From 1850 onward the Taiping Rebellion had spread rapidly from Kwangsi to Hunan, Hupeh and down the Yangtze River to Nanking (see under Hung Hsiu-ch'üan). For three years the pursuing imperial troops vainly followed the insurgents
2 CONC  from Kwangsi to the outskirts of Nanking (see under Hsiang Jung). But the militia, organized in the villages by [https://www.geni.com/people/JZY/6000000070668791375 Chiang Chung-yüan] and [https://www.geni.com/people/LZN/60000000
2 CONC 13883521110 Lo Tsê-nan] [qq.v.], proved to be more effective than the regulars—particularly in 1852 in the defense of Changsha (see under Lo Ping-chang). After the Taipings had abandoned the siege of Changsha (November 30, 1852) [
2 CONC https://www.geni.com/people/ZGF/6000000012827488681 Tsêng] was ordered by the emperor to recruit and drill the Hunan militia. When, after much persuasion, he decided on January 29, 1853 to assume this responsibility he swore to hi
2 CONC mself that he would not covet wealth nor fear death. 
2 CONT
2 CONT [https://www.geni.com/people/ZGF/6000000012827488681 Tsêng Kuo-fan]'s first task was to organize the Hunan Army (Hsiang-chün 湘軍), usually referred to as the "Hunan Braves." It comprised, among other troops, [https://www.geni.com/p
2 CONC eople/%E5%BF%A0%E7%AF%80%E5%85%AC-%E6%BE%A4%E5%8D%97-%E7%BE%85/6000000013883521110 Lo Tsê-nan]'s "Hsiang Yung" and [https://www.geni.com/people/JZY/6000000070668791375 Chiang Chung-yüan]'s "Ch'u Yung" (see under [https://www.geni.
2 CONC com/people/LZN/6000000013883521110 Lo] and [https://www.geni.com/people/JZY/6000000070668791375 Chiang]). These constituted [https://www.geni.com/people/ZGF/6000000012827488681 Tsêng]'s land force. With foresight, characteristic o
2 CONC f his later campaigns, he laid careful plans for the training of his troops and initiated methods of discipline and organization which greatly contributed to his ultimate success. He established central training camps at which tho
2 CONC se troops with previous military experience received further instruction, and opened recruiting stations in each district of Hunan where new recruits received initial preparation. He determined to send his troops first against loc
2 CONC al Bandit groups, to give them experience in fighting before taking them outside the province to war against the Taipings. For this he was severely criticized by those generals who were vainly fighting the Taipings, and even by th
2 CONC e Emperor himself, to whom he addressed a long memorial explaining his plan for the campaign. Stubbornly refusing to be moved, either by ridicule or pleas for aid, he kept on with his organization of the poorly disciplined and unt
2 CONC rained militia. The success of his plan depended upon funds given by Hunan officials and gentry. They proved lukewarm in their support until a victory by [https://www.geni.com/people/JZY/6000000070668791375 Chiang Chung-yüan] and 
2 CONC [https://www.geni.com/people/%E5%BF%A0%E7%AF%80%E5%85%AC-%E6%BE%A4%E5%8D%97-%E7%BE%85/6000000013883521110 Lo Tsê-nan] at Heng-shan-hsien so impressed the Court that local officials found it expedient to give him the necessary fund
2 CONC s. [https://www.geni.com/people/ZGF/6000000012827488681 Tsêng] was embarrassed in all his campaigns by lack of support of the officials until he finally accepted an official post himself, which placed him in control of the finance
2 CONC s of the provinces in which his campaigns were waged. Within a few months he acquiesced in the urgent proposal of [https://www.geni.com/people/JZY/6000000070668791375 Chiang Chung-yüan] and [https://www.geni.com/people/Kwok-Sung-t
2 CONC ao/6000000013883562166 Kuo Sung-t'ao] [q.v.] to build gunboats and to train marines under the command of Yang Yüeh-pin (see under [https://www.geni.com/people/PYL/6000000070668105937 P'eng Yü-lin]) and others, in the hope of drivi
2 CONC ng the Taipings off the Yangtze. 
2 CONT
2 CONT After the conquest of Nanking in 1853 the Taipings were pressing two major campaigns: one to North China (see under [https://www.geni.com/people/SGLQ/6000000017063688968 Sêng-ko-lin-ch'in] and Lin Fêng-hsiang), the other westward 
2 CONC to Anhwei, Kiangsi and Hupeh. At that time [https://www.geni.com/people/ZGF/6000000012827488681 Tsêng Kuo-fan], though again besieged by requests for aid, had no force which he could spare for the defense of Hupeh. A large part of
2 CONC  the Hunan Braves already had been sent to the rescue of Kiangsi, and the rest were occupied in quelling local uprisings in Hunan, while the "navy" was still in process of organization. When, however, a few months later the Taipin
2 CONC gs from Hupeh pressed upon his forces in Hunan, [https://www.geni.com/people/ZGF/6000000012827488681 Tsêng] mobilized (February 25, 1854) his new flotilla of 240 boats with 5,000 marines, and a still larger army under the command 
2 CONC of T'a-ch'i-pu [q.v.], to stem their advance. But owing to a storm which rendered many boats unfit, and to the inexperience of his troops in fighting, [https://www.geni.com/people/ZGF/6000000012827488681 Tsêng] was twice defeated 
2 CONC in Hunan—once in Yochow and again at Ching-chiang 靖港. So mortified was he that he attempted to commit suicide. Fortunately T'a-ch'i-pu and [https://www.geni.com/people/PYL/6000000070668105937 P'êng Yü-lin] triumphed over the Taipi
2 CONC ngs at Hsiang-t'an (May 1, 1854), forcing them to retreat to Yochow, which was finally taken on July 25, 1854—a victory which much encouraged [https://www.geni.com/people/ZGF/6000000012827488681 Tsêng]. On January 12, 1852 the Tai
2 CONC pings had taken Wuchang—the first of three occupations—but had lost it to the government forces in February of the same year. They again seized the city on June 26, 1854, and successfully defended it until October 14 when they wer
2 CONC e oten come by [https://www.geni.com/people/ZGF/6000000012827488681 Tsêng]'s forces under [https://www.geni.com/people/LZN/6000000013883521110 Lo] and [https://www.geni.com/people/JZY/6000000070668791375 Chiang]—a severe blow to t
2 CONC heir plan of conquest. On December 2, 1854 [https://www.geni.com/people/ZGF/6000000012827488681 Tsêng] also won (see under [https://www.geni.com/people/PYL/6000000070668105937 P'êng Yü-lin]) a signal victory over the strong defens
2 CONC e which the Taipings made at T'ien-chia-chên. Meanwhile the city of Shanghai, which had been taken by a band of local rebels in 1855, was recovered in February 1855 (see under Chi-êr-hang-a). By the end of May 1855 the northern ex
2 CONC pedition of the Taipings was finally suppressed (see under [https://www.geni.com/people/SGLQ/6000000017063688968 Sêng-ko-lin-ch'in]). 
2 CONT
2 CONT The victorious advance of [https://www.geni.com/people/ZGF/6000000012827488681 Tsêng Kuo-fan]'s forces was stemmed at Kiukiang, however, by the stubborn resistance of the rebel chief, Lin Ch'i-jung 林啟容 (d. 1858), who had fought fo
2 CONC r the Taipings from the beginning of their activities. A part of [https://www.geni.com/people/ZGF/6000000012827488681 Tsêng]'s navy was bottled up in Po-yang Lake; that part which was in the Yangtze was defeated, even [https://www
2 CONC .geni.com/people/ZGF/6000000012827488681 Tsêng]'s flagship being captured by the rebels; and a storm damaged many of the remaining boats. The morale of [https://www.geni.com/people/ZGF/6000000012827488681 Tsêng]'s troops, disheart
2 CONC ened by these reverses was now at a low ebb. After making the required adjustments, he went to Nanchang, capital of Kiangsi, to rehabilitate the imprisoned fleet which was on the west shore of Po-yang Lake. The Taipings, on the ot
2 CONC her hand, hoping to weaken the attack which the government troops were making on Kiukiang, retook Wuchang for the third time on April 3, 1855. Despite this threat, [https://www.geni.com/people/ZGF/6000000012827488681 Tsêng] ordere
2 CONC d T'a-ch'i-pu to keep an assaulting Kiukiang while [https://www.geni.com/people/%E5%BF%A0%E7%AF%80%E5%85%AC-%E6%BE%A4%E5%8D%97-%E7%BE%85/6000000013883521110 Lo Tsê-nan] and [https://www.geni.com/people/HLY/6000000026353094069 Hu L
2 CONC in-i] [q.v.] were sent to attack Wuchang, he himself remaining at Nanchang. The situation became all the more grave when T'a-ch'i-pu and [https://www.geni.com/people/%E5%BF%A0%E7%AF%80%E5%85%AC-%E6%BE%A4%E5%8D%97-%E7%BE%85/6000000
2 CONC 013883521110 Lo Tsê-nan] both died and [https://www.geni.com/people/ZGF/6000000012827488681 Tsêng] himself was harassed by the almost invincible Taiping leader, Shih Ta-k'ai [q.v.]. But thanks to [https://www.geni.com/people/ZGF/6
2 CONC 000000012827488681 Tsêng]'s farsighted planning, his patience and his perseverance against great odds, coupled with his ability to select and inspire able commanders, Wuchang was recovered for the last time, December 19, 1856, by 
2 CONC the forces under [https://www.geni.com/people/HLY/6000000026353094069 Hu Lin-i] and [https://www.geni.com/people/LXB/6000000070670756257 Li Hsü-pin] [q.v.]. Owing to the help of [https://www.geni.com/people/PYL/6000000070668105937
2 CONC  P'eng Yü-lin] and the reinforcements sent to Kiangsi from Hunan by [https://www.geni.com/people/ZGF/6000000012827488681 Tsêng]'s younger brother, [https://www.geni.com/people/ZGQ/6000000012827361516 Tsêng Kuo-ch'üan] [q.v.], [htt
2 CONC ps://www.geni.com/people/ZGF/6000000012827488681 Tsêng]'s difficult position in Nanchang was alleviated. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Though at this time (1856) the Taipings failed in Hupeh and Kiangsi, they succeeded in crushing Hsiang Jung's large army at Nanking. Thereafter their forces were greatly weakened by a series of murders among their leaders (see und
2 CONC er Hung Hsiu-ch'üan), and so they failed to press the advantage their victory offered. [https://www.geni.com/people/ZGF/6000000012827488681 Tsêng Kuo-fan]'s father died on February 27, 1857, making it necessary for him to retire t
2 CONC emporarily for mourning, but his capable generals were able to carry out his plans and recover Kiukiang on May 19, 1858. He was recalled from retirement before the period of mourning elapsed, and resumed his task-the working out o
2 CONC f a careful plan to take Anking as a first step in the final recovery of Nanking. To accomplish these objectives he again declined to go to the relief of other cities still in the hands of the Taipings, although implored to do so.
2 CONC  In pursuance of his plan to retake Anking he encamped at Ch'i-men in southern Anhwei (1860-61). In 1860 he was appointed governor-general of Kiangnan and Kiangsi and Imperial Commissioner for the suppression of the Taipings in So
2 CONC uth China. He thus was given full power to deal with all matters relating to the campaign, including the levy of funds for this purpose. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In the period 1860-61 [https://www.geni.com/people/ZGF/6000000012827488681 Tsêng] faced a difficult situation. The Taipings, who had earlier in 1860 crushed the reorganized imperial force near Nanking (see under Hsiang Jung), agai
2 CONC n became very strong and active under the leadership of Li Hsiu-ch'êng [q.v.]. A large part of Kiangsu and Chekiang were still in the enemy's hands—only Shanghai was never fully occupied by the rebels, their assaults in that area 
2 CONC being repeatedly repulsed (see under [https://www.geni.com/people/Li-Hung-Chang/6000000011626279828 Li Hung-chang]). At the same time (1860) British and French forces were fighting their way to Peking, while the Court rook refuge 
2 CONC in Jehôl. Appeals for help came to [https://www.geni.com/people/ZGF/6000000012827488681 Tsêng] from all sides, though after September 1860 he himself was so harried by the Taipings at Ch'i-men as to be unable, had he wished, to re
2 CONC nder aid to others. His difficulties reached a climax in April 1861, but by this time he was determined to die rather than retreat. The tide finally turned in his favor when [https://www.geni.com/people/ZZT/6000000026244898796 Tso
2 CONC  Tsung-t'ang] [q.v.] and others came to the relief of Ch'i-men. Moreover, Anking was taken (September 5,1861) by his brother, [https://www.geni.com/people/ZGQ/6000000012827361516 Tsêng Kuo-ch'üan], after long and murderous attacks
2 CONC . [https://www.geni.com/people/ZGF/6000000012827488681 Tsêng Kuo-fan] then made that city his base of operations for the conquest of Nanking. Fearing to concentrate too large an army at Nanking lest the Taipings retake districts a
2 CONC lready under government control—as had repeatedly happened in the past—he set up three military areas: one in Kiangsu under [https://www.geni.com/people/Li-Hung-Chang/6000000011626279828 Li Hung-chang] [q.v.], another in Chekiang 
2 CONC under [https://www.geni.com/people/ZZT/6000000026244898796 Tso Tsung-t'ang], and a third in Anhwei under his own command. In all these areas active campaigns were carried out against the Taipings who were gradually encircled as Na
2 CONC nking was being besieged. [https://www.geni.com/people/ZGQ/6000000012827361516 Tsêng Kuo-ch'üan], who had proved himself an indomitable commander, volunteered for the difficult task of taking Nanking, the Taiping capital since Mar
2 CONC ch 19, 1853, where large government armies had several times been crushed, particularly in August 1856, November 1859, and August 1860. Though he was offered the aid of foreigners, he declined their help, and after a long siege an
2 CONC d desperate fighting took Nanking on July 19, 1864 (see under [https://www.geni.com/people/ZGQ/6000000012827361516 Tsêng Kuo-ch'üan]). The last remnants of the Taipings, however, were not cleared away until the beginning of 1866 (
2 CONC see under Pao Ch'ao). The chief credit for the suppression of this long and bloody Rebellion naturally went to [https://www.geni.com/people/ZGF/6000000012827488681 Tsêng Kuo-fan] who was made a Marquis of the first class with the 
2 CONC designation '''I-yung'''—the first civil official to obtain such a rank. 
2 CONT
2 CONT After the Taiping Rebellion ended [https://www.geni.com/people/ZGF/6000000012827488681 Tsêng Kuo-fan] resumed his post as governor-general of Kiangnan and Kiangsi—thus remaining in Nanking for several months. His main objective wa
2 CONC s to restore peace and order and to promote the rehabilitation of learning in South China after a terribly destructive war lasting fifteen years. At his headquarters at Anking he established, early in 1864, an official printing of
2 CONC fice to reprint important works, chiefly classics and histories; and he now invited celebrated scholars, such as Wang Shih-to, Mo Yu-chih [qq.v.] and others, to be the chief editors. He disbanded a majority of the Hunan army, send
2 CONC ing the soldiers home to their farms and employing the officers (many of whom were students) in proof-reading. In 1864 he issued regulations for printing establishments in each of the cities of Nanking, Soochow, Yangchow, Hangchow
2 CONC  and Wuchang. These were known as "the five official printing offices" (五局). At the same time he restored (December 20, 1864) the provincial examinations at Nanking where, owing to the Taiping occupation, they had been for many ye
2 CONC ars discontinued. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In June 1865 [https://www.geni.com/people/ZGF/6000000012827488681 Tsêng Kuo-fan] was ordered, by hurried mandate, to Shantung where [https://www.geni.com/people/SGLQ/6000000017063688968 Prince Sêng-ko-lin-ch'in] had been killed in
2 CONC  battle (May 1865) while fighting the Nien Bandits. [https://www.geni.com/people/ZGF/6000000012827488681 Tsêng], now in supreme command of military affairs in Shantung, Chihli, and Honan, at once reorganized his forces, distributi
2 CONC ng them at four points in order to draw a net about the elusive rebels. After more than a year in the north in an unsatisfactory campaign to exterminate these rebels, and increasingly conscious of the criticism of his enemies, he 
2 CONC recommended [https://www.geni.com/people/Li-Hung-Chang/6000000011626279828 Li Hung-chang] as his successor (December 12, 1866), he himself returning to his former post as governor-general at Nanking. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1863 [https://www.geni.com/people/YUNG-Wing/6000000020471243967 Jung Hung] [q.v.] had recommended to [https://www.geni.com/people/ZGF/6000000012827488681 Tsêng] the establishment of ironworks at Shanghai—works which later becam
2 CONC e the Kiangnan Arsenal—and [https://www.geni.com/people/YUNG-Wing/6000000020471243967 Jung] had purchased the machinery for it from abroad. In 1868 the first steamship was built there by Chinese and brought to Nanking for [https:/
2 CONC /www.geni.com/people/ZGF/6000000012827488681 Tsêng]'s inspection. The opening of these ironworks was one of the most important contributions [https://www.geni.com/people/ZGF/6000000012827488681 Tsêng] made to the future welfare of
2 CONC  China. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1867 he was appointed a Grand Secretary, and in September 1868 was made governor-general of Chihli province. In the latter capacity he cleared up a large number of long-pending legal cases, improved administrative efficiency, a
2 CONC nd set up a plan for a standing army which, however, was not carried out. In 1870 he was ordered to investigate and settle the case of the Tientsin Massacre (see under [https://www.geni.com/people/CH/6000000075503417075 Ch'ung-hou
2 CONC ]). Fully conscious of China's military weakness, he pressed for a policy of justice and conciliation toward the Western powers involved, and so incurred the ill-will of many officials in Peking who desired war. The case was nearl
2 CONC y settled when, aged and ill, he was transferred (1871) to his old post at Nanking, made vacant through the assassination of Ma Hsin-i [q.v.]. He was succeeded in Tientsin by [https://www.geni.com/people/Li-Hung-Chang/600000001162
2 CONC 6279828 Li Hung-chang]. On August 18, 1871 he sent a joint memorial with [https://www.geni.com/people/Li-Hung-Chang/6000000011626279828 Li], recommending the dispatch of young students to study abroad (see under [https://www.geni.
2 CONC com/people/YUNG-Wing/6000000020471243967 Jung Hung]). Their plan was put into effect in 1872, but [https://www.geni.com/people/ZGF/6000000012827488681 Tsêng] died a few months before the students actually set sail. He was given po
2 CONC sthumously the title of Grand Tutor, and was canonized as '''Wên-chêng''' 文正. 
2 CONT
2 CONT [https://www.geni.com/people/ZGF/6000000012827488681 Tsêng] was a man of great foresight, as evidenced not only in his preparation for military campaigns but in many other matters as well. Several times the Shanghai and Kiangsi ge
2 CONC ntry suggested to the Court that foreign troops, who had successfully defended Shanghai against the rebels, be sent inland in an effort to bring the Taiping rebellion to a speedier close. [https://www.geni.com/people/ZGF/600000001
2 CONC 2827488681 Tsêng], whose opinion in the matter was asked by the Emperor, pointed out that though there was justification for using foreign troops at Shanghai and Ningpo, where in reality they were defending their own interests, th
2 CONC e situation in the interior was different. Here, should joint Chinese and foreign troops be victorious, complications would surely arise and the "guest-soldiers" might seize the land and become a danger to the empire. He urged tha
2 CONC t, even in the use of foreign troops at treaty ports, a careful understanding should be reached before any fighting was undertaken. ln addition to being a man of great foresight and indomitable perseverance, he showed an extraordi
2 CONC nary ability to select men of promise, train them for their posts, and to retain their loyalty. He had on his staff more than eighty able men—many of whom, like [https://www.geni.com/people/Li-Hung-Chang/6000000011626279828 Li Hun
2 CONC g-chang] and [https://www.geni.com/people/PYL/6000000070668105937 P'êng Yü-lin], later became famous in history. He learned a great deal from personal experience in drilling soldiers, controlling subordinate officers, and co-ordin
2 CONC ating troops from different parts of the country—and so finally was able to develop far-reaching plans which he carried out regardless of obstacles. Sometimes he is criticized for his loyalty to the Manchu dynasty for conservatism
2 CONC  and obstinacy, and for cruelty in his treatment of the rebels. Yet the times in which he lived called for stern action, and however strict he may have been with others, he was even more strict with himself. He sought daily to imp
2 CONC rove himself by constant examination of his own mistakes and short-comings, as shown vividly in his diary which he kept from January 1, 1839 to March 11, 1872—the day before he died. The same habits of rigid self-examination are s
2 CONC hown in the letters which he wrote to his parents, to his brothers, and to his sons; and in the admonitions he gave to the young to live lives of frugality, diligence, and integrity. 
2 CONT
2 CONT [https://www.geni.com/people/ZGF/6000000012827488681 Tsêng] was an honest and upright official. We are told in the ''nien-p'u'' (see below) of his young daughter, that during the years he lived in Peking, he was always poor; and t
2 CONC hat even when he held high command in the army, he sent home annually to his family not more than ten to twenty taels silver. It was not until he became governor-general of Chihli that he was able to save 20,000 taels from his sal
2 CONC ary. Throughout his life, no matter under what stress of war or governmental activity, he seldom passed a day in which he did not seek consolation or self-improvement by reading selections from the classics, history, or poetry. He
2 CONC  found in the Sung philosophers, rather than in the writings of the School of Han Learning (see under [https://www.geni.com/people/GYW/6000000075406347000 Ku Yen-wu]), the solace and encouragement which the times required. It is t
2 CONC herefore no wonder that he was instrumental in reviving Sung philosophy in his day. Like some of the great Neo-Confucianists, he became master of a lucid, emotive style, interspersed with wise mottoes and sententious sayings conce
2 CONC rning political, social, military, academic, and family affairs. 
2 CONT
2 CONT According to a bibliography of his works, compiled by [https://www.geni.com/people/LSM/6000000013012276453 Liu Shêng-mu] (see under [https://www.geni.com/people/ZYZ/6000000071873147911 Chang Yü-chao]), [https://www.geni.com/people
2 CONC /ZGF/6000000012827488681 Tsêng Kuo-fan] himself compiled or wrote some thirty-seven works. The more important of these are easily accessible in the so-called complete collection known as 曾文正公全集 ''Tsêng Wên-chêng kung ch'üan-chi'',
2 CONC  174 ''chüan'', printed in 1876. This comprises fifteen titles, including his memorials to the throne, in 36 ''chüan''; his essays and verse, each in 3 ''chüan''; his official correspondence in 33 ''chüan''; and two anthologies of
2 CONC  prose and verse. Appended to the collection are his ''nien-p'u'' in 12 ''chüan'', and a record of the outstanding events of his life (大事記 ''Ta-shih chi'')—both compiled by his pupils. In addition, there is his diary, 手書日記 ''Shou-
2 CONC shu jih-chi'', in 40 volumes, printed in 1909; his letters to his family, ''Tsêng Wên-chêng kung chia-shu'' (家書), 10 ''chüan'', printed in 1876; instructions or admonitions to members of his family, 家訓 ''Chia-hsün'', 2 ''chüan'', 
2 CONC also printed in 1876; and a collection of other essays, ''Tsêng Wen-cheng kung chi wai-wên'' 外文, 1 ''chüan'', printed in 1929. His mottoes and sayings on many subjects have been collected from his writings and published under vari
2 CONC ous titles, such as: 曾胡治兵語錄 ''Tsêng-Hu chih-ping yü-lu'' (1911), sayings of [https://www.geni.com/people/ZGF/6000000012827488681 Tsêng] and of [https://www.geni.com/people/HLY/6000000026353094069 Hu Lin-i] on military matters; ''T
2 CONC sêng Wên-chêng kung chia-yen ch'ao'', 嘉言鈔 (1916), a collection of [https://www.geni.com/people/ZGF/6000000012827488681 Tsêng]'s famous sayings; and ''Tsêng Wên-chêng kung hsüeh-an'' (學案 1925) sayings on character-building and meth
2 CONC ods of study. Many other works were compiled under the general editorship or direction, such as the 江蘇減賦全案 ''Kiangsu chien-fu ch'üan-an'' (1866) on the reduction of taxation in the Soochow area (see under [https://www.geni.com/peo
2 CONC ple/FGF/6000000046150245021 Fêng Kuei-fên]); and 江西全省輿圖 ''Kiangsi ch'üan-shêng yü-t'u'' (1868), 14 + 1 ''chüan'', an atlas of Kiangsi province. 
2 CONT
2 CONT [https://www.geni.com/people/ZGF/6000000012827488681 Tsêng Kuo-fan] had four younger brothers: [https://www.geni.com/people/ZGH/6000000012827586454 Tsêng Kuo-huang 曾國潢] (T. 澄侯 1820-1885), [https://www.geni.com/people/ZGH/600000001
2 CONC 2827501848 Tsêng Kuo-hua 曾國華] (T. 溫甫, posthumous name 愍烈 1822-1858), [https://www.geni.com/people/ZGQ/6000000012827361516 Tsêng Kuo-ch'üan], and [https://www.geni.com/people/ZGG/6000000013869716667 Tsêng Kuo-pao 曾國葆] (T. 季洪 name l
2 CONC ater changed to 曾貞榦, T. 恆靖, posthumous name 靖毅, 1828-1863)—all of whom served in the army which fought against the Taiping Rebels. He had four sisters: Tsêng Kuo-lan 曾國蘭 who married Wang P'êng-yüan 王鵬遠 ; Tsêng Kuo-hui 曾國蕙 who marr
2 CONC ied Wang Tai-p'in 王待聘 ; Tsêng Kuo-chih 曾國芝, who married Chu Yüng-ch'un 朱詠春, and a sister who died in infancy. He had two sons: Tsêng Chi-tsê [q.v.], the inheritor of his hereditary rank of Marquis; and Tsêng Chi-hung 曾紀鴻 (T. 栗諴 18
2 CONC 48-1881) who was skilled in mathematics, especially algebra. He had five daughters: Tsêng Chi-ching 曾紀靜 who married Yüan Ping-chên 袁秉楨; Tsêng Chi-yao 曾紀耀 who married Ch'ên Yüan-chi 陳遠濟 ; Tsêng Chi-ch'ên 曾紀琛 who married [https://ww
2 CONC w.geni.com/people/LZS/6000000013883529073 Lo Chao-shêng 羅兆升], son of [https://www.geni.com/people/%E5%BF%A0%E7%AF%80%E5%85%AC-%E6%BE%A4%E5%8D%97-%E7%BE%85/6000000013883521110 Lo Tsê-nan] [q.v.]; Tsêng Chi-ch'un 曾紀純 who married Kuo
2 CONC  Kang-chi (see under [https://www.geni.com/people/Kwok-Sung-tao/6000000013883562166 Kuo Sung-tao]); and Tsêng Chi-fên 曾紀芬 who married Nieh Ch'i-kuei 聶緝槼. Tsêng Chi-fên, born in 1852, edited her own ''nien-p'u'' under the title, 崇德
2 CONC 老人八十自訂年譜 ''Ch'ung-tê lao-jên pa-shih tzu-ting nien-p'u'', with portrait and calligraphy, first edition 1931, revised edition, 1935. 
2 CONT
2 CONT [ 1/411/la; 2/45/lla; 5/5/llb; 8/1/la; 26/4/5a; 29/10/5b; Li Yüan-tu [q.v.], ''T'ien-yüeh shan-kuan wên-ch'ao'' 14/1; [https://www.geni.com/people/Kwok-Sung-tao/6000000013883562166 Kuo Sung-tao], ''Yang-chih shu-wu wên-chi'' 19/la
2 CONC ; Li Shu-ch'ang [q.v.], ''Cho-tsun-yüan ts'ung-kao'' 3/la; Hsüeh Fu-ch'êng [q.v.], ''Yung-an wên-pien'' 4/17; Yü Yüeh [q.v.], ''Ch'un-tsai-t'ang tsa-wên'' 2/lOb; I-hsin [q.v.], ''Chiao-ping Yüeh fei fang-lüeh''; Kuan-wên [q.v.], '
2 CONC 'P'ing-ting Yüeh fei chi-lüeh'', l8 ''chüan'' (1869); Wang K'ai-yün 王闓運, 湘軍志 ''Hsiang-chün chih'', 16 ''chüan'' (1886); Wang Ting-an 王定安, 湘軍記 ''Hsiang-chün chi'', 20 ''chüan'' (1889), 求闕齋弟子記 ''Ch'iu-ch'üeh-chai ti-tzu-chi'', 32 ''
2 CONC chüan'' (1876); 咸豐三年以來兵事月日 ''Hsien-fêng san-nien i-lai ping-shih yueh-jih'', in the 同治上江兩縣志 ''T'ung-chih Shang Chiang liang-hsien chih'', ''chüan'' 18 (1874) ; Li Hsiu-ch'êng, ''Li Hsiu-ch'êng kung-chuang''; McClellan, J. W., ''Th
2 CONC e Story of Shanghai'' (Shanghai, 1889); Yung Wing, ''My Life in China and America'' (New York, 1909); Morse, H. B., ''The International Relations of the Chinese Empire'', Vol. II (London, 1918); Hail, William James, ''Tsêng Kuo-fa
2 CONC n and the Taiping Rebellion'' (1927); Liu Shêng-mu, 直介堂叢刻 ''Chih-chieh-t'ang ts'ung-k'o'' (1929); Chiang Hsing-tê 蔣星德, 曾國藩之生平及事業 ''Tsêng Kuo-fan chih shêng-p'ing chi shih-yeh'' (1936) ; ''Ta-kung pao'' (daily), ''Wên-hsüeh fu-k'an
2 CONC '' (Literary Supplement) no. 253 (November 7, 1932); 文哲季刊 ''Wên-che chi-k'an'', vol. III, No. 4, pp. 691728 (Wuhan University, Wuchang, China, 1934); 師大月刊 ''Shih-ta yüeh-k'an'', no. 28, pp. 149-67 (National Normal University, Peip
2 CONC ing, November, 1936).] 
2 CONT
2 CONT TÊNG SSÛ-YU
