1 NAME 紀澤 劼剛 /湖南湘鄉/
2 GIVN 紀澤 劼剛
2 SURN 湖南湘鄉
2 _MARNM 曾
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE 1839
1 DEAT
2 DATE 1890
1 TITL Marquis
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Tsêng Chi-tsê''' 曾紀澤 (T. 劼剛), Dec. 7, 1839-1890, Mar. 12, diplomat, a native of Hsiang-hsiang, Hunan, was the elder son of [https://www.geni.com/people/ZGF/6000000012827488681 Tsêng Kuo-fan] [q.v.]. When about a
2 CONC  year old he was taken to Peking where his father was in office, and so did not leave the capital for his ancestral home until 1853. Thereafter his father was chiefly occupied in the suppression of the Taiping Rebellion. [https://
2 CONC www.geni.com/people/DJZ/6000000012827521360 Tsêng Chi-tsê] occasionally visited his father's various headquarters, and sometimes accompanied him on tours of inspection, but did not take an active part in military campaigns. Though
2 CONC  he made no effort to qualify for the official examinations, he nevertheless received a liberal education. He familiarized himself with the Classics, history, literature, music and archery, and achieved some skill in painting and 
2 CONC calligraphy. At the same time he had some grasp of Western science and of the English language. One of his trusted European friends was [https://www.geni.com/people/Sir-Halliday-Macartney/6000000047927952068 Samuel Halliday Macart
2 CONC ney] (see under [https://www.geni.com/people/GST/6000000013883562166 Kuo Sung-tao]) who later accompanied him on his mission to Russia. When his father died, in 1872, [https://www.geni.com/people/DJZ/6000000012827521360 Tsêng Chi-
2 CONC tsê] inherited the hereditary rank of ''hou'' 侯 and therefore was given in the West the appellation Marquis. In 1878 he was appointed minister to England and France as successor to [https://www.geni.com/people/GST/6000000013883562
2 CONC 166 Kuo Sung-tao] [q.v.]. He sailed from Shanghai on November 22, 1878; arrived in Paris on January 4, 1879; and presented his letters of credence six days later. He proceeded to London on February 4, and on March 20 presented his
2 CONC  credentials at the Court of St. James. 
2 CONT
2 CONT During his seven years of diplomatic service in Europe the most outstanding achievement of [https://www.geni.com/people/DJZ/6000000012827521360 Tsêng Chi-tsê] was the conclusion with Russia, in February 1881, of the Treaty of St. 
2 CONC Petersburg. Rebellious uprisings among the Mohammedan groups of Chinese Turkestan had already begun in 1862. By 1867 Yakoob Beg (see under [https://www.geni.com/people/ZZT/6000000026244898796 Tso Tsung-t'ang]) emerged as conqueror
2 CONC  and as ruler over Kashgar and Yarkand. In 1867 [https://www.geni.com/people/ZZT/6000000026244898796 Tso Tsung-t'ang] [q.v.] was charged with the task of putting down these Mohammedan rebels. But the distance being great, and the 
2 CONC means of transport limited, he had to proceed slowly. In the meantime Russia took advantage of the chaotic situation to move troops into Kuldja and occupy the territory of Ili (1871), giving assurances, however, to the Chinese gov
2 CONC ernment that the territory would be restored when China was in a position to maintain order in that area. Apparently Russian officials were then of the opinion that China would not be able to resume control. Nevertheless, [https:/
2 CONC /www.geni.com/people/ZZT/6000000026244898796 Tso Tsung-t'ang] slowly but surely pushed forward his military campaign. By 1878 the whole territory was pacified and Russia was informed that China was ready to resume the administrati
2 CONC on of Ili. [https://www.geni.com/people/CH/6000000011325508386 Ch'ung-hou] [q.v.] was appointed ambassador plenipotentiary to Russia, presenting his credentials at St. Petersburg in January 1879. As the Treaty of Livadis, negotiat
2 CONC ed by [https://www.geni.com/people/CH/6000000011325508386 Ch'ung-hou], was regarded in China as a complete failure, and was received there with consternation, it was renounced by the Chinese government on February 19, 1880. On Feb
2 CONC ruary 12 [https://www.geni.com/people/DJZ/6000000012827521360 Tsêng Chi-tsê] was appointed minister to Russia in the hope of being able to conclude a new treaty. Leaving London on July 14, he arrived at St. Petersburg on July 30 a
2 CONC nd presented his credentials at Tsarskoe Selo on August 22. As the people of both nations were indignant, and war seemed imminent, the negotiation of a new treaty was exceedingly difficult. A work, entitled 金軺籌筆 ''Chin-yao ch'ou-p
2 CONC i'' gives a full account of the conferences held from August 4, 1880 to February 23, 1881 between [https://www.geni.com/people/DJZ/6000000012827521360 Tsêng Chi-tsê] and Nicolas de Giers 格爾斯, Russian minister of foreign affairs; B
2 CONC aron de Jomini 熱梅尼, a member of the council of that ministry; and Eugene C. Butzow 布策, the Russian minister to China. This record, with an appendix containing the text of the Treaty of St. Petersburg as annotated by [https://www.g
2 CONC eni.com/people/DJZ/6000000012827521360 Tsêng Chi-tsê], was printed in 1887. The Treaty of Livadia was finally annulled, and the Treaty of St. Petersburg was signed on February 24, 1881. By this new treaty China, gained a large str
2 CONC ip of territory including the Tekkes Valley and the mountain passes between Ili and Kashgaria. Changes were made in regard to trade routes and customs, and additional Russian consulates were authorized in China. In return, China a
2 CONC greed to pay Russia, for the expense of occupation, nine million metallic roubles instead of the five million previously arranged. The treaty was generally regarded as a diplomatic triumph for China. 
2 CONT
2 CONT There was also the question of Annam. In 1874 a treaty of peace and alliance and a commercial treaty were signed between Annam and France, which amounted, in effect, to a transfer of Annam's allegiance from China to France. Annam 
2 CONC continued, however, to send tribute to Peking. Early in 1880 [https://www.geni.com/people/DJZ/6000000012827521360 Tsêng Chi-tsê] was instructed by the Chinese government to inquire about the status of Annam. Repeated assurances ha
2 CONC d been given by France that she had no designs on Tongking but, with a. change of administration, the French policy altered. On August 25, 1883 another treaty was signed by which Annam accepted the status of a French protectorate.
2 CONC  Though warfare broke out here and there between China and France (see under Liu Ming-ch'uan and Fêng Tzǔ-ts'ai), negotiations were intermittently carried on with China until a treaty was signed at Tientsin on June 9, 1885 by [htt
2 CONC ps://www.geni.com/people/LHC/6000000011626279828 Li Hung-chang] [q.v.] and Patenotre (see under Tsêng Kuo-ch'üan). Meanwhile [https://www.geni.com/people/DJZ/6000000012827521360 Tsêng Chi-tsê]'s term as minister to France ended in
2 CONC  the spring of 1884. On July 18, 1885 he and [https://www.geni.com/people/Robert-Gascoyne-Cecil-3rd-Marquess-of-Salisbury/5311102004990062258 Lord Salisbury] signed at London an additional article to the Chefoo Convention (see und
2 CONC er [https://www.geni.com/people/LHC/6000000011626279828 Li Hung-chang]) concerning an increase of the tax levy on the importation of opium. In the same year [https://www.geni.com/people/DJZ/6000000012827521360 Tsêng] was recalled 
2 CONC and [https://www.geni.com/people/LRF/6000000050132431907 Liu Jui-fen] [q.v.] was made his successor. [https://www.geni.com/people/DJZ/6000000012827521360 Tsêng] was then ordered to assist in the newly-formed Board of Admiralty (se
2 CONC e under [https://www.geni.com/people/YH/6000000009796546841 I-huan]) and, while waiting in London for his successor to arrive, began negotiations for the purchase of warships. As England was then taking steps toward the annexation
2 CONC  of Burma, [https://www.geni.com/people/DJZ/6000000012827521360 Tsêng] was simultaneously charged with making overtures in this matter, though a convention was not concluded until later. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Before leaving London [https://www.geni.com/people/DJZ/6000000012827521360 Tsêng Chi-tsê] wrote an article which appeared in the January issue (1887) of the ''Asiatic Quarterly Review'' under the title "China, the Sleep and the Aw
2 CONC akening". In it he described the existing state of China and forecast her future policies. He arrived at Shanghai on October 18, 1886, and reached Peking on December 11. Appointed to serve in the Tsungli Yamen, he was made junior 
2 CONC vice-president of the Board of War, and later (1887) became a senior vice-president of the Board of Revenue. He also served (1885-90) on the Board of Admiralty. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1889 he was charged with the administration of the T'ung-wên Kuan (see under [https://www.geni.com/people/DX/6000000058335104062 Tung Hsün]). His death at the age of fifty-two ''sui'' was a severe loss to China. He was granted 
2 CONC full posthumous honors and was canonized as '''Hui-min''' 惠敏. Being progressive in his ideas, and liberal in his understanding and appreciation of things Western, he met with frequent opposition from his more conservative colleagu
2 CONC es. Some of them even attributed his premature death to his reliance on Western medicine. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The complete works of [https://www.geni.com/people/DJZ/6000000012827521360 Tsêng Chi-tsê], entitled ''Tsêng Hui-min kung ch'uan-chi'' (公全集) were first printed by the Kiangnan Arsenal (see [https://www.geni.com/people/DRC/600000007
2 CONC 1621011871 Ting Jih-ch'ang]) in 1893 and were reprinted lithographically at Shanghai in 1894. The material is distributed under the following titles: ''Tsêng Hui-min kung tsou-i'' (奏議), 6 ''chüan'', comprising his memorials; ''Tsê
2 CONC ng Hui-min kung Wên-chi'' (文集) 5 ''chüan'', consisting of his writings in prose; 歸樸齋詩鈔 ''Kuei-p'u chai shih-ch'ao'', 4 ''chüan'', his poems; and 使西日記 ''Shih Hsi jih-chi'', 2 ''chüan'', the diaries he kept during his terms as a dip
2 CONC lomat in Europe. 
2 CONT
2 CONT [ 1/452/3b; 2/58/29b; 5/15/17b; 19 ''hsin-hsia'' 28b; ''Ch'ing-chi wai-chiao shih-liao'' (see under I-hsin); Boulger, D. C., ''The Life of Sir Halliday Macartney''; Martin, W.A.P., ''A Cycle of Cathay''; Cordier, Henri, ''Histoire
2 CONC  des relations de la Chine avec les puissances occidentales''; see bibl. under [https://www.geni.com/people/ZGF/6000000012827488681 Tsêng Kuo-fan].] 
2 CONT
2 CONT TU LIEN-CHÊ
