1 NAME Robert George /McCrea/
2 GIVN Robert George
2 _MARNM McCrea
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE 8 DEC 1920
2 ADDR
3 CITY Ashburton
3 STAE Canterbury
3 CTRY New Zealand
1 DEAT
2 DATE 24 NOV 2003
2 ADDR
3 CITY Ashburton
3 STAE Canterbury
3 CTRY New Zealand
1 BURI
2 DATE 26 NOV 2003
2 ADDR
3 CITY Ashburton
3 STAE Canterbury
3 CTRY New Zealand
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} EULOGY
2 CONT
2 CONT ROBERT GEORGE (BOB) McCREA
2 CONT
2 CONT Firstly, I wish to thank you all for coming today to share with us in our loss and to celebrate Dad’s life. It’s always hard saying a final goodbye but I think that Dad truly thought that he’s had his turn and that with Mum gone, 
2 CONC it’s now time to move on….and it’s comforting to know that he was able leave us in a comfortable and peaceful manner. Still, I don’t want to dwell on his death but to focus on his life. Bob was a fairly simple and humble man and c
2 CONC ould be a bit brusque at times.   He tended to focus on the ‘half empty rather than half full’ and he had lots of little idiosyncrasies.  But despite any imperfections, my father was a bloody good bloke – on whatever scale you lik
2 CONC e to measure it. Firstly his vital statistics: Robert George McCrea Born:	8th December 1920 to Henry and Elizabeth Daisy McCrea (known as Harry and Daisy)   Married 	Ethel Margaret Owen, 10th July 1948 Two Kids:	Patricia and Peter
2 CONC  Six Grandkids: Sarah, Hayden, & Emma Vessey. Robbie, Jack and Sylvia McCrea Died:	24th November, 2003  And  now the form stats: •	Served in the NZ infantry for four years, with three of these overseas  •	Married for 52 years, 364
2 CONC  days •	Lived at 114 Cass Street for just over 50 years •	Worked at Higgins and Co Printers for  around 39 years •	Life member of both the Ashburton RSA and the MSA Working Mens Club That’s the mark of a solid citizen and although
2 CONC  to some, it may seem to reflect a fairly dull and boring life, it wasn’t…..and I came across a quote from Mark Twain the other day that seems particularly pertinent here: There was never yet an uninteresting life.  Such a thing i
2 CONC s an impossibility.  Inside of the dullest exterior there is a drama, a comedy and a tragedy…..and so it was with Dad.  Beneath Dad’s sometimes rough exterior lay a very sensitive character, and a number of events during his forma
2 CONC tive years, particularly his service on the frontlines during WW2 had a deep impact on him and blunted much of the youthful drive and ambition that he previously possessed.  The war changed his views on societal values and he had 
2 CONC a huge job adjusting back to civilian life.  He fronted in every major battle from North Africa to Italy, including Al Alamein and Monte Cassino,  and many of his good mates were shot around him – all at around 21 years of age.  S
2 CONC o, if he was a bit cynical and conservative about things at times, in my book, he was well and truly entitled to it.  He’s made a huge contribution to his country, his community and his family.    Alright, enough soul searching.  
2 CONC I really want to give you a bit of an overview of Bob’s life and despite my comments above, he still very much liked to live life and to have a good time. Dad was born and grew up in Ashburton, the eldest boy and third child of si
2 CONC x children.  He’s never told me much about his early life but from what I’ve been told, like most people in those days, life was pretty tough.  His father was a drum beating Orangeman from Londonderry who worked when he could as a
2 CONC  labourer around the Mid-Canterbury area, including as a teamster driving a team of horses working the local farmland, constructing the Ashburton sewerage system and building the Selwyn River bridge.  Grandad was a tough old roost
2 CONC er, and both he and Nana were staunch Presbyterians – Grandad was manager of the St Andrews Church and Nana ran the Sunday school – and they expected the kids to toe the line. Their standard Sunday was church in the morning, Sunda
2 CONC y school in the afternoon, church again at night, and then finished off by gospel singing at the Cochranes place on the way home.   Dad was named after his Uncle Robert who was killed in WW1.  Until he was about 12, the family liv
2 CONC ed down Suffolk Street, or Cowshit Alley, as it was known, and during the depression, Grandad was largely unemployed and the family had to line up on a regular basis at the Ashburton hospital to receive handouts of meat.  Grandad 
2 CONC tried to provide for the family by planting large tracts of spuds on vacant sections…..as good Irishmen do.   He had an old hand plough, which he used to harness Dad and brother Noel to and make them drag around tilling the garden
2 CONC s like donkeys.  They also had a cow called Daisy, which was interesting, as Nana was also known as Daisy…I guess it was a different era, but still.  Apparently, Dad cunningly never quite learnt how to milk Daisy (the cow that is)
2 CONC  and Noel still laments the fact that he always had to do the milking.   Dad got a bad case of peritonitis when he was six and almost died but he seemed to recover and apparently the kids spent most of their spare time swimming an
2 CONC d eeling down the Ashburton River.  It must have been time well spent because dad got right into competitive swimming and swam in carnivals all around the district for both Hampstead and Allenton.  He was also a keen member of the
2 CONC  St Andrews Church Physical Culture Club, which I imagine was a 1930’s cross between the New Life Centre, a boot camp and a Les Mills Gym.  He also played hockey, but being the eldest boy, I think that pre war, this pretty much pl
2 CONC ayed second fiddle to helping provide for the family.  He worked for a while delivering the Guardian and then secured an apprenticeship with the paper as a printer’s machinist.   Although times were tough during the depression, it
2 CONC  didn’t seem to stop Dad and his young Ash-Vegan mates being bits of larrikins.  Dad and Noel went halves in an old convertible Dodge car (Noel did the driving but that’s a later story), and  they used to drive to Chertsey to buy 
2 CONC booze, because Ashburton was a prohibition area in those days.  Apparently the car converted to a mobile bar outside local dances and it was particularly useful for deerstalking, where the lads discovered that you didn’t actually 
2 CONC have to get out of the car to shoot the deer but just needed to shoot the 303 bullets through the convertible’s roof.  I’m not quite sure whether they ever actually got any deer but by the sounds of it, the mailboxes were very ner
2 CONC vous on the way home! With war looming, Dad joined the territorials and was camped at Westerfield for a couple of years before heading off by troopship to North Africa, as part of the Canterbury A Company of the 23rd Infantry Batt
2 CONC alion.  Dad never would tell me much about his time in the war…despite my constant badgering for information.  But from talking to others and a bit of reading, I have learnt that he went through some very tough times.  Many of his
2 CONC  best mates were killed and in one battle, his unit suffered a ninety percent casualty rate – not a nice thing to experience at 22.  Dad would only ever slip a few things in about some good times – such as breaking into wine cella
2 CONC rs in Italy and getting totally sloshed, killing local pigs and having big hangi’s with the Maori 28 Battalion (whom they regularly fought alongside), and breaking into the American bases – at great risk of being shot – to steal f
2 CONC ood and drink.  He was also particularly proud of the fact that he had peed into the Po River.  But it seems that the sly old bugger also had an Italian girlfriend over there who wanted to follow him out – which he only revealed i
2 CONC n his last month or two and whom I only saw a picture of yesterday – I must say that I’m glad he passed up on that one. During the war he rose to the rank of Sergeant and during his latter time was posted to the very front-line Ma
2 CONC chine Gun Platoon.  It seems that he was highly respected by his men and after the war, received a glowing commendation from the commanding officer, Emery.  He also received a special mention in Colonel Frank Rennie’s book, Regula
2 CONC r Soldier, who noted that “ I was very fortunate to have Bob McCrea from Ashburton as my Platoon Sergeant” but then he went on to recount an incident that occurred when they were undertaking a heavy firing programme at night in pr
2 CONC eparation for a frontal assault by the main troops.  It seems that as they finished their firing programme, they all scarpered for some pre-arranged cover.  Rennie tripped over a stump as he made the mad dash and was winded - and 
2 CONC when he finally recovered and made it back, he found that Sergeant Bob had already taken over command.  Someone had apparently reported that Rennie had bought it - but even so, Rennie reckoned that it was the quickest transfer of 
2 CONC command ever.  Still, Rennie recounted that it brought a few laughs and they brought out the vermouth.
2 CONT  After the war, dad found it pretty hard to adjust back to civilian life and I gather that he hit the booze pretty hard with his mates for a while.  He got a job for a few months as a linesman at the Ashburton Power Board but soon
2 CONC  left and joined Higgins and Co as  a printers machinist, where he worked until he retired in 1985.  He also got back into hockey and played for Tech Old Boys (and made the Ashburton Rep team) with a bunch of ratbag returned guys 
2 CONC who cleaned up the competition for years in a row.  The hockey boys were to provide the social basis in Dad’s life for many years and the same group later all took up indoor bowls together, although how the bowls actually managed 
2 CONC to cut their way through the almost impenetrable smoke haze of the MSA and RSA halls I could never fathom. Regular Saturday night dances at the Radiant Hall were the big thing in those days and it was there that Dad hooked onto a 
2 CONC hockey babe from Methven.  He must have been keen because pretty soon he was hopping on the mail van to Methven with his bike, courting mum and then biking back to Ashburton,  though I’m told the bike was a bit wobbly on the way b
2 CONC ack. They got married in Methven in 1948, set up house on East Street in Ashburton and Trish was born in 1950.  In 1951, Dad made a big plunge and with the assistance of a State Advances loan, purchased their castle at 114 Cass St
2 CONC reet for the princely sum of $3000.  Here they lived for their next 50 years and although quite a few modifications were made, one major bastion remained – the trusty old coal range, which we heated the kitchen with, cooked on and
2 CONC  heated the water.  I remember during my school years that I used to get a bit embarrassed when mates came around and that we still had a coal range.    But the coal range basically typified the conservatism of dad post-war and hi
2 CONC s resistance to change.  He had his comfort zone and that’s where he wanted to stay.  However, he was lucky with mum that they were a great counter for each other and soon carved out their respective niches in the relationship.   
2 CONC Mum always said that she wouldn’t do without the coal range but secretly, I reckon she would have loved an electric one and that she only said it to support dad. It’s funny how a lot of the lessons that your parents teach you don’
2 CONC t become apparent or appreciated until well beyond your teens.  Although Dad largely turned away from the church after the war, the morals and values stayed strongly with him and he made sure that us kids took these on board and t
2 CONC hat we attended church every week.  Dad taught us heaps about what was really important and instilled strong ethics within us.  He was also pretty strict and I used to get really annoyed that I was not allowed to go to things that
2 CONC  some other kids were – I now fully appreciate what he was trying to do and am starting to fight the same battles with my own kids.    Anyway, back to the story….. Dad was the provider and pedalled off to Higgins and Co every morn
2 CONC ing, back at lunchtime for a hot dinner and then back again after work, sometimes with an extra 3 hours overtime at night – the same bike for 40 years I might add.  Paypackets came on Thursdays and were promptly handed over to mum
2 CONC , less enough for a couple of jugs at the MSA that night with his mates. Most of his spare time at home was spent tending his immaculate and bountiful vegetable garden or playing with us kids.   Dad was heaps of fun in his younger
2 CONC  days but he had defined boundaries.  He was comfortable fooling with us at home and on holiday and always helped out with such things as the school flower show and on the committee of the Hampstead Swimming Club, etc.  But he’d n
2 CONC ever get directly involved with us at school or attend class shows.   Teachers, ministers and policemen were held with arms length respect by Dad in those days. The vege garden was Dad’s pride and joy and there was a large element
2 CONC  of competition with Noel and Hazel, his brother and sister, as to whose was better.  I reckon Dad’s was the best but in any case, three generations of relations and friends have made a feast of gorging on succulently fresh raspbe
2 CONC rries, strawberries, grapes, gooseberries, peas, tomatoes and carrots direct from his garden.  In the early days we also had a big chookhouse which kept us in eggs and prized chickenmeat for big occasions.  It was always an event 
2 CONC when dad lopped off their head with an axe and let them fly like a mad chook over the backyard – Tegal have certainly killed that treat! Dad also used to make me all sorts of toys and pieces of sports equipment from wood – tommy g
2 CONC uns, rifles, cricket bats, swords and shields, go-carts, and so forth - and these were always preferred over the bought plastic ones.  He also had a black leather strap hanging from the kitchen mantelpiece which was forever a fore
2 CONC boding threat…and occasionally carried out. Mum ran the household, looked after all the finances and tended the flower garden.  No mean feat! They both played sport to a representative level and they both enjoyed a good time.  Spo
2 CONC rt drove their social life, firstly with hockey, then indoor bowls and then golf…but it was largely the same crowd throughout and when we were young, there would be great parties where a whole heap of us kids would be put into bed
2 CONC s topping and tailing and telling stories of Aggie Taylor, the crazy woman that lived at the back of the domain…the more stories we told, the crazier she got.    They also loved to play cards – euchre and 500 mainly – and there wa
2 CONC s many a Saturday night around the kitchen table with a few friends, a few flagonsand a thick haze of Capstan Cork and Pall Mall.  Also, because our place was pretty central to the great metropolis, lots of people tended to drop i
2 CONC n – and there was always a meal and/or a beer brought out.  Mum and dad were both very hospitable but, perhaps being a little defensive that our place wasn’t the fanciest around, they were always adamant to guests that ‘ you take 
2 CONC us as you find us’.  Oh, and card nights were always followed by sardines on toast for supper.  Dad was also a great offal lover.  His specialty was brawn made from Uncle Gordons cast off pigs heads – his mates used to ooh and aah
2 CONC  about how good it was but I worked out long ago that if I craved a feed of jelly, I’d stick to a Greggs packet.  Another delicacy was oysters.  Dad and his mates used to order sacks of Bluff oyster and then have opening nights, r
2 CONC esplendent with a jar of DYC vinegar and a few flagons..  They were cheap in those days.  He also home brewed in the old copper in my early years but I think that that died after the abolition of the Ashburton prohibition in the 5
2 CONC 0’s In 1961, we got our first car – a green 1953 Morris Minor – and this is another great story of Dad’s character.  Mum got her licence and started carting us all around the country and Dad intended to follow suit.  Prior to gett
2 CONC ing his licence, he would sometimes take me out on a Saturday morning to do a couple of jobs….. this is 1960’s Ashburton afterall!   A few Saturdays down the track and already booked in to take his licence, Dad and I ground our wa
2 CONC y down Cass Street and into the drive.  However, unbeknown to us, Aunty Shirl had parked the Mark II Zephyr, complete with tow bar, just inside the gate.  The poor Morrie didn’t stand a chance and had quite a dent in it’s grill.  
2 CONC I hope that no-one’s here from State Insurance because Mum and Dad lodged an insurance claim, except I think that they forgot who was driving because the claim said that mum was the driver and consequently, the repair bill was dul
2 CONC y paid by State.  The upshot was, that Dad was so upset about his distortion of the truth that he never ever drove again. We never had much money but Dad always made sure we had an annual holiday and a few weekends away during the
2 CONC  year – the holidays weren’t fancy but they were always great fun.  Perhaps the most memorable were train trips to the North Island to visit our cousins at Mount Maunganui and Tauranga.  Sometimes we just looked after the Locks fa
2 CONC rm while they were away and that was great too.  In the summer, we’d often bike down for tea at the river, complete with the obligatory flagon of Greggs cordial.     Dad’s best mate was Tommy Thomson and we always had Xmas dinner 
2 CONC with them, as well as  regular Saturday night teas, picnics and many weekends away.   As we got older, Dad and mum got more into golf and together ran the summer golf competition at Tinwald Gold Club for many years.  They also sta
2 CONC rted attending the annual RSA golf tournament and travelled all over New Zealand to these events.  Dad retired at 65 and it was good that they had these outside interest because the extra time spent sitting around the coal range a
2 CONC fter retirement sometimes put a bit of a strain on things, to say the least - but I guess that that’s not too uncommon at that stage of life. Dad gave up golf about four years ago, soon after mum had a fall and couldn’t play any m
2 CONC ore.  The old Cass Street garden was also starting to get too much and after many heated arguments, Trish and I finally managed to convince – or rather force – them into moving to their current flat adjacent to Coldstream House.  
2 CONC Ironically, although it was Dad who had resisted moving for many years, in the end, it was him who realised the need to move and it was Mum, who was by then suffering from Alzheimers, that stubbornly dug her heels in. When mum die
2 CONC d a couple of years ago, dad seemed to lose purpose in life and he appeared to slow down and age in front of us.  Without mum to manage his affairs, he became reliant on Trish to organise his life and in many respects, he was like
2 CONC  a lost little boy.  As perhaps you do in later life, he became more reflective, particularly on his war years – though he would still divulge little of his experiences - and the RSA became a much stronger focus in his life. Parti
2 CONC cularly because I’ve not been around all that much, I am especially gratetful to Trish and Ian for their continuous support for Dad, and also to the RSA, staff of Coldstream House, the Ashburton Hospital and to the many friends an
2 CONC d relations who have helped and supported Dad and mum.  I was also gladdened to see that Dad and Noel had largely dropped their competitive brotherly pride over the last couple of years and have actually been there for each other,
2 CONC  though a little less of Uncle Noels home brewed gin may have been advisable.. Finally, I’d like to end with a quote from a friend of mine who made the following observation about dad a few years ago – and I think it about sums hi
2 CONC m up: BOB IS ONE OF THOSE BLOKES WHO, IF HE SAYS SOMETHING’S GOOD, YOU KNOW IT’S BLOODY GOOD
