1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ==Biography==
2 CONT '''Judge Leroy Gilbert Denman''' was born on October 31, 1855, in Guadalupe County, Texas, United States. His parents were [https://www.geni.com/profile-111705784 Morgan Gilbert Denman] and [https://www.geni.com/profile-126333366 
2 CONC Henrietta Denman]. He was a Texas State Supreme Court Justice. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Leroy married [https://www.geni.com/profile-116918511 Susanna Emily Carpenter] in 1882 in Texas, United States. Together they had the following children:
2 CONT # [https://www.geni.com/profile-34749782244 Roy Emily Browne (Denman)]; 
2 CONT # [https://www.geni.com/profile-34743509778 Flossie Floyd Terrell (Denman)]; 
2 CONT # [https://www.geni.com/profile-34743509779 Leroy Gilbert Denman, II]; 
2 CONT # [https://www.geni.com/profile-117041303 Gilbert Morgan Denman]; 
2 CONT # [https://www.geni.com/profile-34743509781 Bebe Denman]; 
2 CONT # [https://www.geni.com/profile-34743509782 Susanna Dorothy Denman]; 
2 CONT # [https://www.geni.com/profile-34743509780 Clare Shirley Denman]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT He died on September 14, 1916, in San Antonio, Bexar County, Texas, United States and was buried in Mission Burial Park South, San Antonio, Bexar County, Texas, United States of America. 
2 CONT ----
2 CONT ==Biography==
2 CONT '''Judge Leroy Gilbert Denman''' was born on October 31, 1855, in Guadalupe County, Texas, United States. His parents were [https://www.geni.com/profile-111705784 Morgan Gilbert Denman] and [https://www.geni.com/profile-126333366 
2 CONC Henrietta Denman]. He was a Texas State Supreme Court Justice. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Leroy married [https://www.geni.com/profile-116918511 Sue Emily Carpenter] in 1882. Together they had the following children:
2 CONT
2 CONT He died on September 14, 1914, in San Antonio, Bexar County, Texas, United States and was buried after September 14, 1916, in Mission Burial Park South, San Antonio, Bexar County, Texas, United States of America. 
2 CONT ----
2 CONT ---- '''The Denman Estate Park''' Judge Denman's Legacy lives on[https://tclf.org/denman-estate-park] was one of his private family estates. Located northwest of downtown, this approximately twenty-acre city park was last owned by
2 CONC  his son, a local attorney, and philanthropist, Gilbert Denman, Jr.  The City of San Antonio purchased 12.5 acres from Denman’s estate in 2007, Judge Denman also owned a large Ranch in Guadalupe County for weekend retreats. 
2 CONT ----
2 CONT Judge Leroy Denman was an Associate Justice of the Supreme Court of Texas and founded the prestigious law firm of Denman, Franklin, and Denman in 1899.  He was educated principally by his father and subsequently taught school for 
2 CONC four years before entering the law school of the University of Virginia, from which he graduated in 1880. He practiced law at New Braunfels for two years before he moved to San Antonio, where he had an active practice in both civi
2 CONC l and criminal cases throughout Southwest Texas.
2 CONT ----
2 CONT *  President of San Antonio Loan & Trust Company
2 CONT * Chairman of the board of directors of the San Antonio National Bank
2 CONT *  Farm & Ranch owner near his birthplace in Guadalupe County, Texas [https://atlas.thc.state.tx.us/Details/5187001412 ] '''El Capote Ranch-'''  Historical Marker  Atlas Number 5187001412 still owned by heirs. 
2 CONT * Denman Hall, Hollin's University, 1906 named in his honor for his generous donations. 
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT ---- In 1894 he was appointed associate justice of the state Supreme Court by Governor James S. Hogg. Denman was re-elected to the position and remained on the bench until May 1899, when he resigned and returned to his private pra
2 CONC ctice in San Antonio. 
2 CONT ----
2 CONT Among his important opinions [https://tshaonline.org/handbook/online/articles/fde40]
2 CONT *  Joske v. Irvin, in which the Supreme Court of Texas refused to follow the scintilla-of-evidence rule previously announced by the United States Supreme Court
2 CONT
2 CONT * Royal Insurance Company v. McIntyre, in which the court defined for Texas the measure by which total losses in fires would be determined
2 CONT
2 CONT * Crawford v. MacDonald, in which the court defined the difference between direct and collateral attacks on judgments. Denman was not noted for his forensic power, but he was a fluent speaker. He handed down 146 opinions-opinions 
2 CONC marked by "brevity, simplicity of expression, and accuracy of a legal conclusion
2 CONT ----
2 CONT [https://tarltonapps.law.utexas.edu/justices/profile/view/25]
2 CONT
2 CONT "Denman Family History From the Earliest Authentic Records down to Present Day" by Harris, Harriet Newell [https://archive.org/details/denmanfamilyhis00harrgoog/page/n16]
2 CONT
2 CONT * Reference: His grandson, Clare's son,  Todd Burney's Estate - a few Items up for auction, one happens to be Judge Leroy Denman's desk [https://www.austinauction.com/calendar/may-estates-auction?page=2] Family heirlooms often not
2 CONC  appreciated by future generations. The same happens to many of our portraits and photographs if we don't share them. 
2 CONT  [https://www.ancestry.com/family-tree/person/tree/111701831/person/430126881619 Ancestry Genealogy] - [https://www.geni.com/projects/SmartCopy/18783 SmartCopy]: ''Oct 5 2019, 3:21:31 UTC''
2 CONT ** Reference: [https://www.ancestry.com/family-tree/person/tree/111701831/person/430126881619/facts Ancestry Genealogy] - [http://www.geni.com/projects/SmartCopy/18783 SmartCopy]: ''Oct 8 2019, 9:02:58 UTC''
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Leroy Gilbert Denman, lawyer and Texas Supreme Court justice, was born in Guadalupe County, Texas, on October 31, 1855. He was educated principally by his father and subsequently taught school for four years before entering the la
2 CONC w school of the University of Virginia, from which he graduated in 1880. He practiced law at New Braunfels for two years before he moved to San Antonio, where he had an active practice in both civil and criminal cases throughout S
2 CONC outhwest Texas until 1894, when he was appointed associate justice of the state Supreme Court by Governor James S. Hogg. Denman was reelected to the position and remained on the bench until May 1899, when he resigned and returned 
2 CONC to his practice in San Antonio. Among his important opinions was that in Joske v. Irvin, in which the Supreme Court of Texas refused to follow the scintilla-of-evidence rule previously announced by the United States Supreme Court;
2 CONC  that in Royal Insurance Company v. McIntyre, in which the court defined for Texas the measure by which total losses in fires would be determined; and that in Crawford v. MacDonald, in which the court defined the difference betwee
2 CONC n direct and collateral attacks on judgments. Denman was not noted for his forensic power, but he was a fluent speaker. He handed down 146 opinions-opinions marked by "brevity, simplicity of expression, and accuracy of legal concl
2 CONC usion." He was married in 1881 to Sue E. Carpenter. He died on September 14, 1916.
2 CONT
