1 OCCU Homemaker
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ==Biography==
2 CONT Obit-Gonzales Inquirer Dec. 13, 1917
2 CONT
2 CONT Laid to Rest
2 CONT
2 CONT Simple but impressive funeral services were held at the W. B. Green residence Wednesday afternoon in honor of Mrs. Zella A. S. Green, who passed away Tuesday night.
2 CONT
2 CONT Numerous friends and relatives of the family gathered to pay a last tribute to her memory. Her pastor, Rev. J. W. Rowland, assisted by Rev. Chas. H. Dobbs conducted the services.
2 CONT
2 CONT A wealth of beautiful floral offerings empowered the casket, tributes from the many among young and old who had known and loved Mrs. Green, beautiful expressions of sympathy for the bereaved loved ones. Among the special tributes 
2 CONC were handsome emblems from the Eastern Star, the Rebekah Lodge, the W. C. T. U. the Methodist Missionary, and Parsonage societies, to all which Mrs. Green belonged. Another pretty offering composed of white geraniums was a tribute
2 CONC  from the fourth grade of the City Schools of which Mrs. Green's young grandson, William Green, is a member.
2 CONT
2 CONT This morning before 9 o'clock the funeral party left for the long drive to Old a Rancho where the remains were interred in the old family burying ground beside those of loved ones gone before. Rev. J. W. Rowland and Rev. Chas. H. 
2 CONC Dobbs accompanied the remains, together with friends and relatives of the family.
2 CONT
2 CONT While the bright presence of the loved mother and grandmother will be sadly missed, her sons, Judge W. B. Green, J. N. Green of Nixon, and only daughter, Miss Rosa, have the precious heritage of a noble Christian mother's example 
2 CONC to treasure for the remainder of their lives, and to the younger members of the household, she will also ever be a tender and fragrant memory, inspiring them to nobler and better living.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Obit above provided by Cindy S Munson (FAG Member #47210136)
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Zelpha Ann Green (Nixon)''' was born on March 1, 1843, in Randolph County, North Carolina, United States. Her parents were [https://www.geni.com/profile-79564215 Zachariah Nixon] and [https://www.geni.com/profile-34717925897 Ga
2 CONC briella Nixon]. She was a Homemaker, born a Quaker who joined the Methodist Church when she moved to Texas with her brother's. 
2 CONT
2 CONT She was raised in The Friends Society as a Quaker but left the religion when she migrated to Texas in the Autumn of 1852 with her older brother [https://www.geni.com/people/Captain-Robert-Nixon/6000000075592453259?through=60000000
2 CONC 07645907506]and his wife[https://www.geni.com/people/Laura-Nixon/6000000012840011209]. She joined the Methodist Church and became a member of the Eastern Star.  She was highly active in church activities and often visited her brot
2 CONC her and his wife.  
2 CONT
2 CONT Zelpha married [https://www.geni.com/profile-34743050387 James Knox Polk Green, MD] on May 29, 1867, in Luling, Caldwell, Texas, USA. Together, they had the following children: James Knox divorced his first wife before Sept. 1881;
2 CONC  he remarried soon after, and she never remarried. 
2 CONT # [https://www.geni.com/profile-34743050391 William Burton Green Atty, Sr];
2 CONT # [https://www.geni.com/profile-34743050396 Rosannah M Green];
2 CONT # [https://www.geni.com/profile-34743050392 Knox Polk Green];
2 CONT # [https://www.geni.com/profile-34743050393 Henry B Green];
2 CONT # [https://www.geni.com/profile-34743050394 James M. Green];
2 CONT # [https://www.geni.com/profile-34743050401 Alta N Green];
2 CONT # [https://www.geni.com/profile-34743050400 Edgar F. Green];
2 CONT # [https://www.geni.com/profile-34743050398 Jessie Lou Green]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT They divorced on 19 Sep 1881, and Dr. Green soon thereafter married a widow in town, Eliza Elizabeth Thomas Cone (Mangum). This fact wasn't mentioned in the Nixon genealogy nor in any other historical records. Research uncovered i
2 CONC t on her Death certificate, stating she was divorced. The separate census reports and marriage certificate for Dr. Green. All the Nixon women were independent-minded and could care for themselves quite well. Her father, Zachariah 
2 CONC Nixon, treated her with the same respect he gave his sons and taught her to manage her household as a man would, so she did quite well without him, even in her weakened condition. Nixon women weren't well-suited for many men durin
2 CONC g this time in history. 
2 CONT
2 CONT She died on November 27, 1917, in Gonzales, Gonzales County, Texas, United States, and was buried in The Old Rancho Cemetery in Nixon, Gonzales County, Texas. Buried Old Rancho Cemetery[https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/83863357
2 CONC /zella-a_s_-green]
2 CONT ----
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT '''References''' The Obit above provided by Cindy S Munson (FAG Member #47210136)
2 CONT * "Early Nixon's of Texas" by Pat Ireland Nixon, MD, Genealogies by Dr. and Mrs. Pat Ireland Nixon, Jr., Published by Carl Hertzog, El Paso, Texas 1956 [https://babel.hathitrust.org/cgi/pt?id=wu.89082553272&view=1up&seq=11]
2 CONT * Find-A-Grave Buried Old Rancho Cemetery[https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/83863357/zella-a_s_-green]
