1 NOTE {geni:about_me} 
2 CONT ==Biography==
2 CONT '''Dr. Phineas Nixon, II''' was born on April 4, 1763, in Little River, Pasquotank County, North Carolina, Colonial America. His parents were [https://www.geni.com/profile-78563056 Dr. Phineas Nixon, I] and [https://www.geni.com/p
2 CONC rofile-79562005 Mary Nixon]. He was a Medical Doctor.
2 CONT
2 CONT Phineas married [https://www.geni.com/profile-79563906 Millicent Nixon] on September 16, 1787 they were approved to be married. Our family records have the marriage date as October 16, 1787, as The Wedding Date at Back Creek Meeti
2 CONC ng of The Society of Friends, Randolph, North Carolina, United States, Guilford, North Carolina, United States. Together they had the following children:
2 CONT # [https://www.geni.com/profile-34742212590 Jesse Henley Nixon]; 
2 CONT # [https://www.geni.com/profile-79564134 Mary Cosand]; 
2 CONT # [https://www.geni.com/profile-79564153 Thomas Nixon, MD]; 
2 CONT # [https://www.geni.com/profile-79564168 Anna Henley Winslow]; 
2 CONT # [https://www.geni.com/profile-34742212592 Dr. Phineas Nixon, III]; 
2 CONT # [https://www.geni.com/profile-79564215 Zachariah Nixon]; 
2 CONT # [https://www.geni.com/profile-34683063429 Barnabas Nixon, MD]; 
2 CONT # [https://www.geni.com/profile-79564248 Gabriel H Nixon]; 
2 CONT # [https://www.geni.com/profile-79564272 Stephen Nixon, MD]; 
2 CONT # [https://www.geni.com/profile-79564303 John Nixon]; 
2 CONT # [https://www.geni.com/profile-79564332 Henley Nixon]; 
2 CONT # [https://www.geni.com/profile-79564343 Millicent Jones]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT He died on November 23, 1837,  Back Creek Meeting Society of Friend, Pasquotank County, North Carolina, United States. 
2 CONT ----
2 CONT Dr. Phineas Nixon was a well respected active member of the Back Creek Society of Friends, He served on the Education Board Committee. All Society Committee Meeting notes were positive about him throughout his life.  He was a hard
2 CONC -working Physician and Surgeon during the days when his wife would drive him to his house calla which would be miles apart at times, late at night, he would sleep as she drove the wagon. She was his nurse and his surgical assist a
2 CONC  legacy passed down generations to Robert Thomas Nixon's son, James Wesley Nixon and his wife, it stopped with them, but the family story is still told. 
2 CONT ----
2 CONT '''References'''*
2 CONT
2 CONT *"Early Nixon's of Texas" by Pat Ireland Nixon, MD, Genealogies by Dr. and Mrs. Pat Ireland Nixon, Jr., Published by Carl Hertzog, El Paso, Texas 1956 [https://babel.hathitrust.org/cgi/pt?id=wu.89082553272&view=1up&seq=11] pg, 114
2 CONC , 115.
2 CONT
2 CONT * Obituary [https://www.newspapers.com/clip/36227036/the_weekly_standard/]
2 CONT
2 CONT * Views on Slavery [https://archive.org/stream/towardfreedomfor00hilt/towardfreedomfor00hilt_djvu.txt] Important note, The Nixons did not believe in Slavery, they were the first American immigrants to free their slaves, when slave
2 CONC s were listed on Census Reports they were living at the Nixon's and working for them for wages. The Nixon Family protected them. 
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
