1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ==Biography==
2 CONT '''Reverend David Rasberry Bruton''' was born on December 23, 1829, in Montgomery County, North Carolina, USA, Montgomery County, North Carolina, United States. His parents were [https://www.geni.com/profile-34743111351 James Ozie
2 CONC r Bruton] and [https://www.geni.com/profile-34645636260 Diana Bruton]. He was a Methodist Minister, College Professor. 
2 CONT
2 CONT David married [https://www.geni.com/profile-34743111349 Virginia V. Bruton]. Together they had the following children:
2 CONT * [https://www.geni.com/profile-34760038494 James Valentine Bruton]; 
2 CONT * [https://www.geni.com/profile-34760038493 Lily Mae Burt]; 
2 CONT * [https://www.geni.com/profile-34760038496 David M Bruton]; 
2 CONT * [https://www.geni.com/profile-34760038495 Helen Walton Gaither]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT David married [https://www.geni.com/profile-34717928405 Margaret Gaziell Bruton] on October 9, 1856, in North Carolina, United States. Together they had the following children:
2 CONT * [https://www.geni.com/profile-34717928434 Annie Raspberry Bruton]; 
2 CONT * [https://www.geni.com/profile-34717928435 Sarah Ella Bruton]; 
2 CONT * [https://www.geni.com/profile-34717928436 Col John Fletcher Bruton]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT He died on March 27, 1894, in Wilson County, North Carolina, United States and was buried in Maplewood Cemetery, Wilson, Wilson County, North Carolina, USA, Wilson, Wilson County, North Carolina, United States. 
2 CONT ----
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT ---- Rev. David Bruton and his wife Margaret Nixon were both notable in private education, after graduation from the Bingham Academy, they moved to Wilson during the fall of 1881 as teachers in the Wilson Graded School. Although h
2 CONC e was elevated to the rank of superintendent in June 1883, he soon afterward entered the University of North Carolina Law School and then, in the latter part of 1884, secured his license. He also became a Law Professor at the  Und
2 CONC ergraduate Bingham Private College. He was also a Methodist Minister. 
2 CONT
2 CONT University of North Carolina Law School., a law degree.  
2 CONT ----
2 CONT Education, Law Degree, Bingham Academy.[https://www.ncpedia.org/biography/bruton-john-fletcher] 
2 CONT * Reference: [https://www.ancestry.com/family-tree/person/tree/111701831/person/430130365209 Ancestry Genealogy] - [https://www.geni.com/projects/SmartCopy/18783 SmartCopy]: ''Mar 22 2018, 13:50:25 UTC''
2 CONT ** Reference: [https://www.ancestry.com/family-tree/person/tree/111701831/person/432022835693/facts Ancestry Genealogy] - [http://www.geni.com/projects/SmartCopy/18783 SmartCopy]: ''Sep 30 2019, 12:59:22 UTC''
2 CONT ----
2 CONT MEMOIR OF REV. DAVID R. BRUTON, from the Journal of the North Carolina Annual Conference of the Methodist Episcopal Church, South:
2 CONT ----
2 CONT David Rasbury Bruton, youngest son of James and Diana Bruton, was born in Montgomery County, N. C., December 23, 1829, and died at Wilson, N. C., March 27, 1894.
2 CONT ----
2 CONT Early in life he gave his heart to God and connected himself with the Methodist Episcopal Church, South. Soon afterward he gave himself to the work of the Methodist ministry.
2 CONT ----
2 CONT In 1857, after his graduation at Trinity (then Normal) College, he applied for admission on trial into the North Carolina Conference, holding its session in the town of Goldsboro. At this Conference, he was appointed to the Presid
2 CONC ency of the Raleigh Female Seminary. His ministerial life was a long and honorable one, as can be seen from the following list of appointments served by him : 1857-’59, Raleigh Female Seminary. 1859-’61, Wentworth Circuit. 1861-’6
2 CONC 3, Madison Circuit. 1864- 65, Thomasville and Lexington Circuit. 1865- ’67, Thomasville Station. 1867-’69. President of Thomasville Female College. 1869- ’70, Davidson Circuit. 1870- ’74. Concord Station. 1874-’78, Salisbury Distr
2 CONC ict. 1878-’82, Greensboro Station. 1882- ’84, Warrenton District. 1884- ’86, Reidsville Station. 1886-’88, supernumerary. 1888- ’89, Nashville Circuit. 1889- ’90, Bethel Circuit. 1890- ’94, superannuated.
2 CONT ----
2 CONT In 1858 he was married to Margaret G. Nixon, who died in 1865, leaving two children, who are now living — Col. John F. Bruton, of Wilson, N. C., and Miss Ella S. Bruton, of Kentucky Wesleyan Female Institute.
2 CONT ----
2 CONT In 1869 he was married to Virginia Mauney who died in 1889. The fruit of this union was four children, three of whom survive him — Mrs. E. W. Burt, Helen, and David Bruton.
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1858 he was married to Margaret G. Nixon, who died in 1865,  leaving two children, who are now living — Col. John F. Bruton, of  Wilson, N. C., and Miss Ella S. Bruton, of Wesleyan Female Institute. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1869 he was married to Virginia Mauney who died in 1889. The fruit of this union was four children, three of whom survive him — Mrs. E. W. Burt, Helen and David Bruton. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Brother Bruton’s ministry was a long and honorable one, covering  more than one-third of a century. As circuit rider, stationed pastor,  presiding elder and college president he ran almost the whole gamut  of ministerial experienc
2 CONC e. This fact shows the worth and influence  of the man in his day and generation. He was, in fact, one of our  most useful and influential men. In his prime he did much to impress  his character upon the important plans and enterp
2 CONC rises of North  Carolina Methodism. His mind was acute, his thought clear and  striking — he was a man of true culture. This culture was manifested  in his general bearing and preaching power. It can be truthfully  said that as a 
2 CONC preacher he was above the ordinary. His preaching  was characterized by strength rather than brilliancy, ’^et at times  he was both brilliant and strong. A minister of the North Carolina  Conference who is not given to the utteran
2 CONC ce of fulsome compliment  remarked to the reader, in substance, that some of the sermons  preached by Brother Bruton the preceding summer in Catawba County  were powerful in the extreme. He was especially pointed and power-  ful i
2 CONC n the denunciation of sin. Bro. Bruton’s most popular work was  done, perhaps, when he was pastor at Concord Station and Presiding  Elder of the Salisbury District; yet his best work, I have no doubt,  was done in the country, awa
2 CONC y from the noisy towns, where he met  large congregations hungry for the bread of life; where he could  inhale the sweet breath of the hills and hear the voice of Nature  echoed in the song of bird and moan of forest. Here he seem
2 CONC ed to  get nearer to God, and preached with melting tenderness and apos-  tolic zeal. His preaching was truly evangelical, and through the  Holy Spirit brought many wanderers into the fold. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Perhaps the richest tribute is paid to his character when it is said  that he was a man of heart. We have not many of whom this can  be said. The learned author of the “Tongue of Fire” has said with  beauty and force, “The heart c
2 CONC ommands the man. Give me a heart  and you give me a man.” According to this, Brother Bruton was a  man — a man such as we need and such as God loves. He had a  heart tender and true, pure and Christ-like. This tender part of his  
2 CONC nature made him a loving husband and father, and riveted to him the  deepest affections of his family. It made him a welcomed one in the  social circle. It enlisted the affections of the poor and unfortunate. 
2 CONT
2 CONT It made him the special friend of young men. No young preacher  had any fear in preaching to a congregation in which Brother Bruton  sat. The benignant sympathetic look, the hearty amen, bade every  timid fear begone. There are in
2 CONC  North Carolina to-day numbers of men,  prominent in church and State, who, to a great extent, owe what  they are to the interest Brother Bruton showed in them when they  were young men. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Brother Bruton never altogether ceased from preaching. He did  much effective work in the superannuated relation. It is a cheering  thought that on the Sabbath preceding his death — that bleak, wintry  Sabbath, when nature seemed 
2 CONC disorganized, distracted — this man of  God stood in the pulpit at Fremont and told the “old, old story of  Jesus and His love.” 
2 CONT
2 CONT As he grew older his spiritual experience grew richer. He enjoyed  with increasing relish the presence of his Master. Though worn  down with disease and harassed by misfortune his spirit never mur-  mured and his faith never weake
2 CONC ned. In one of the last letters  written to his son. Col. J. F. Bruton, he said, “I have placed my  hand in His, and although everything appears dark I am trusting  implicitly.” No one could doubt that Brother Bruton enjoyed his  
2 CONC religion. He returned from Fremont March 26. Before retiring on  Monday night his conversation was about Jesus. He was ripening  for Heaven. Though the wheat had been beaten and torn in many  a storm, yet the golden color deepened
2 CONC  in the harvest days, and it  was a rich sheaf that the angels bore away in the twilight of that  Tuesday morning. On the following Wednesday afternoon we laid  his body down in quiet Maplewood Cemetery. Many friends followed  his
2 CONC  body to the grave. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Another one of the “Old Guard” of the Conference has passed  away. Only a few are left. David Rasbury Bruton, with unstained  shield and unrusted sword, has answered his Captain’s call. What a  rich legacy descends to every younge
2 CONC r soldier in the memory of the  old soldier and hero! “Blessed are the dead that die in the Lord.”  “Thus, though oft depressed and lonely.  All my fears are laid aside;  If I but remember only  Such as these have lived and died.’
2 CONC ' 
2 CONT
2 CONT His wife was Margaret Nixon
2 CONT
