1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ==Biography==
2 CONT '''Colonel John Fletcher Bruton''' was born on May 29, 1861, in Wentworth, Rockingham County, North Carolina, United States. His parents were [https://www.geni.com/profile-34717928431 David Rasberry Bruton] and [https://www.geni.c
2 CONC om/profile-34717928405 Margaret Gaziell Bruton]. He was an Attorney, Chairman of Board of Trustees Duke University for 22 years.
2 CONT
2 CONT John married [https://www.geni.com/profile-34743104629 Hattie Tartt Barnes] on November 15, 1887, in Wilson, North Carolina, USA. Together they had the following children:
2 CONT # [https://www.geni.com/profile-34743104630 John Barnes Bruton]; 
2 CONT # [https://www.geni.com/profile-34743077915 Howard Barnes Bruton]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT He died on March 27, 1946, in Wilson, North Carolina, United States, and was buried in Wilson, Wilson County, North Carolina, United States of America. [https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/96639370]
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.ncpedia.org/biography/bruton-john-fletcher
2 CONT
2 CONT John Fletcher Bruton, lawyer, educator, banker, civic leader, and churchman, was born at Wentworth in Rockingham County, a son of the Reverend David Rasberry Bruton and his wife, Margaret Nixon, both notable in private education. 
2 CONC After graduation from the Bingham Academy, Bruton moved to Wilson in the autumn of 1881 as a teacher in the Wilson Graded School. Although elevated to the rank of superintendent in June 1883, he soon afterward entered The Universi
2 CONC ty of North Carolina Law School and then, in the latter part of 1884, secured his license.
2 CONT
2 CONT Returning to Wilson, he practiced for nearly two years in partnership with John Exum Woodard before establishing an independent practice, which he maintained for the remainder of his long career. Although he was never a candidate 
2 CONC for political office, many honors resulted from the public confidence in his dedication to principle. From 1894 to 1897 he served as mayor of Wilson. He became vice-president and attorney of the First National Bank in 1897 (servin
2 CONC g as its president from 1904 to 1932), and in the same year he was one of the organizers of the North Carolina Bankers Association, of which he was later a president. Bruton was a director of the Federal Reserve Bank in Richmond f
2 CONC or ten years and was subsequently a member of the Federal Advisory Council to the Federal Reserve Board. He was a director and attorney for the Wilson Home and Loan Association for forty-five years and for a considerable time the 
2 CONC president of the Wilson Trust and Savings Bank. He was a director and vice-president of the North Carolina Home Insurance Company and also served on the board of directors of the Carolina Telephone and Telegraph Company. For three
2 CONC  years he was chairman of the Wilson County Board of Education.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1889, Bruton organized and was elected captain of Company F, Second Regiment of the North Carolina State Guard. Three years later he was commissioned colonel of the regiment, but the pressure of business commitments forced his 
2 CONC resignation in 1900. He expressed a continuing interest in education by taking an active part in the founding of the Kinsey Female Seminary in 1897 (known as Atlantic Christian College since 1902). In 1900 he was elected a trustee
2 CONC  of Trinity College, becoming chairman of the board in 1912. He continued as chairman of the board of trustees of Duke University from 1927 until his death. For more than fifty years he served on the board of the Wilson Methodist 
2 CONC Church and also taught the Bible class that still bears his name.
2 CONT
2 CONT On 15 Nov. 1887, Bruton was united in marriage by his father to Hattie Tartt Barnes (12 June 1863–10 Nov. 1954), daughter of John Thomas Barnes and his wife, Elizabeth Obedience Tartt. Bruton and his wife died in Wilson and were b
2 CONC uried in Maplewood Cemetery, survived by two sons: John Barnes (28 Oct. 1888–27 Jan. 1960), who married Gladys Smith; and Howard Barnes (1 Nov. 1893–11 July 1971), who married Almira Woodard.
2 CONT
2 CONT There is a portrait of Bruton in the Wilson County Courthouse.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT Lawyer, educator, banker, civic leader, Board of Directors for Duke University for 22 years. Col. Bruton was a successful attorney who would become a bank president, vice-president of the North Carolina Home Insurance Co., a direc
2 CONC tor of Carolina Telephone & Telegraph Co, and mayor of Wilson (1894-96).
2 CONT * Reference:
2 CONT * Military: In 1889, Bruton organized and was elected captain of Company F, Second Regiment of the North Carolina State Guard. Three years later he was commissioned colonel of the regiment, but the pressure of business commitments
2 CONC  forced his resignation in 1900. 
2 CONT
