1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Robert Fulton Nixon''' [https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/81589154/robert-fulton-nixon] Lawyer
2 CONT
2 CONT Son of Steven D. Nixon and Carry Hysaw/Hisaw Nixon
2 CONT
2 CONT Obit-Gonzales Inquirer June 10, 1912
2 CONT
2 CONT Remains of Robert Fulton Nixon Laid to Rest
2 CONT
2 CONT The remains of Robert Fulton Nixon who passed away at his residence in this city Monday morning were reverently consigned to their last resting place on Tuesday afternoon.
2 CONT
2 CONT Funeral services were conducted at the family residence at 5:30 o'clock by Rev. Chas. H. Dobbs, pastor of the Presbyterian church, in the presence of a large gathering of sorrowing friends and relatives assembled to pay the last t
2 CONC ribute of respect to all that was mortal of him whom they had known and loved in life.
2 CONT
2 CONT After the services were concluded the I.O.O.F. Lodge of which the deceased was a member took charge of the remains and the active pallbearers bore the casket to the hearse, the members of the order in full regalia drawn up in a do
2 CONC uble file on either side. The long procession then formed and headed by the members of the lodge marching two and two, proceed to the Odd Fellows cemetery where the remains were interred with the impressive burial service of the o
2 CONC rder.
2 CONT
2 CONT A wealth of exquisite lovely floral offerings, eloquent tokens of the esteem and love of many friends and dear ones, completely covered the newly made mound in fragrant benediction. So numerous were the beautiful offerings that th
2 CONC ey also covered the adjoining grave where sleep the remains of the loved wife whom he has joined after a separation of less than a year. Two especially handsome offerings, the one in white and red blossoms, the other in white and 
2 CONC green in the form of three links emblematic of the I.O.O.F and Rebekah orders were sent by two lodges of which Mr. Nixon was a prominent member. There were also, a number of other handsome emblems and wreaths from friends and rela
2 CONC tives in San Antonio and other places at a distance.
2 CONT
2 CONT That one in the full prime of life should be thus early called away from its activities is beyond our understanding and is not for us to question for we must remember that "He doe all things well."
2 CONT
2 CONT Mr. Nixon will be sadly missed not only by those who were related to him but by a large circle of friends to whom he was ever true and faithful and who will fondly cherish the memory of his bright genial companionship.
2 CONT
2 CONT Obit-Gonzales Inquirer June 12, 1912
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert Fulton Nixon passed away this morning at 11 o'clock at his residence in this city after a lingering illness of some eight weeks duration.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mr. Nixon had not been in good health since Christmas, his condition becoming more serious as spring advanced. In April he left for Mineral Wells hoping that the change and the treatment there would be beneficial. He, however, gre
2 CONC w worse, and, for a time, was so ill that it was deemed unsafe for him to make the journey home. After slight improvement, he was brought home several weeks ago since which time he gradually grew worse until the end came at the ab
2 CONC ove-named hour.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mr. Nixon was born in Guadalupe County, September 13, 1871, and was 40 years, 8 months, and 27 days old when the summons came to lay down life's burden. He was the son of Mr. and Mrs. Stephen D. Nixon, who with their other childre
2 CONC n have preceded him to the Great Beyond.
2 CONT
2 CONT He received his education at A. & M. College, the University of Virginia, and the University of Texas, in which institution he received a degree from the Law department.
2 CONT
2 CONT He begun the practice of law in this city in 1898, served as county attorney from 1900 to 1908, for the last four years, was associated with W. P. Midkiff in the practice of his profession.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1901 he married Miss Marie Bernadine Pfeuffer, daughter of Col. Daniel Pfeuffer of New Braunfels, with whom he was permitted to spend ten happy years f wedded life, the beloved wife being called away July 8, 1911, less than a y
2 CONC ear ago, leaving, besides a heart-broken husband, two little motherless sons, Robert Fulton Jr. and Daniel Steven Decatur, aged about 8 and 3 years respectively, who, by the sad providence of this morning are now bereft of both pa
2 CONC rents.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mr. Nixon was a prominent Odd Fellow as well as a member of the Woodmen of the World.
2 CONT
2 CONT Besides the two children above referred to he leaves a number of relatives in this section of the state to whom the Inquirer extends sincere sympathy.
2 CONT
2 CONT Funeral services will be conducted at the family residence at 5 o'clock Tuesday afternoon and interment will be made in the Odd Fellows Cemetery under the auspices of that order.
2 CONT
2 CONT The following have been appointed as pallbearers: Active-Messrs. John M. Rainey, W. P. Midkiff, W. W. Johnson, J. S. White, D. U. Ramsay, N. D. Cone; honorary-Messrs. T. F. Harwood, R. S. Dilworth, J. W. Rainbolt, W. H. Blanton, W
2 CONC . T. Miller, J. C. Romberg, W. D. C. Jones, H. Duncan, W. W. Glass, Jon. S. Conway, N. P. Reid, W. M. Atkinson, C. A. Burchard, W. B. Green, C. K. Walter, W. M. Fly, S. Joseph R. H. Walker, C. R. Hoskins, J. L. DuBose Sr., Geo. P.
2 CONC  McDonald, W. B. Houston, and T. E. Stephenson.
2 CONT
2 CONT Gonzales Inquirer Aug. 23, 1912 pg 3
2 CONT
2 CONT Will of Robert F. Nixon, Deceased Probated
2 CONT
2 CONT Among other businesses coming before the court in session this week, the last will and testament of the late Robert F. Nixon were presented for probation on Thursday.
2 CONT
2 CONT Owing to the fact that county Judge W. B. Green is related to the beneficiaries named in the will be was disqualified to act as judge in this case, in view of which, by the appointment of Governor Colquitt, Mr. J. C. Romberg, acte
2 CONC d as a special judge in the case.
2 CONT
2 CONT The will was duly probated and Dr. J. W. Nixon and Mr. W. P. Midkiff, who was associated with the late Mr. Nixon in the practice of law, were appointed executors without bond as provided in the will. Dr. J. W. Nixon who was appoin
2 CONC ted temporary administrator of the estate filed his report which was approved by the court.
2 CONT ==Biography==
2 CONT '''Robert Fulton Nixon, Atty. I''' was born on September 13, 1871, in Seguin, Guadalupe County, Texas, United States. His parents were [https://www.geni.com/profile-34717928407 Stephen Decatur Nixon] and [https://www.geni.com/prof
2 CONC ile-34717928503 Susan Caroline Nixon (Hisaw)]. He was a Prominent Attorney,  Business Owner. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert married [https://www.geni.com/profile-34744643359 Marie Bernadine Nixon (Pfeuffer)] on February 11, 1902. Together they had the following children:
2 CONT [https://www.geni.com/profile-34744643360 Robert Fulton Nixon, II]; [https://www.geni.com/profile-34744643361 Daniel Decatur Nixon]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT He died on June 10, 1912, in Gonzales, Gonzales, Texas, United States and was buried in Odd Fellows Cemetery. 
