1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Genealogy] - [http://www.geni.com/projects/SmartCopy/18783 SmartCopy]: ''Sep 21 2019, 3:33:13 UTC'' The Macon Beacon Macon, Ms. 18 October 1912, page 5
2 CONT
2 CONT                                                     Death of Mr. Stanton S. Field Mr. Stanton Slaughter Field, for a long time a prominent citizen of Noxubee, having been in the mercantile business in Shuqualak under the firm name
2 CONC  of Constantine & Field, but who has lived in Mobile for several years, died at his home in that city Monday morning, October 14th, at five o'clock, after an illness of only a few days. It was not known until Sunday evening that h
2 CONC is condition was at all serious, when his heart's action became bad, and it failed to respond to the medicine. He was conscious until the last and passed peacefully away as quietly as if he was falling to sleep.
2 CONT
2 CONT He had been a strict member of the Episcopal church from his early youth, and services of that church were held at his late residence before the remains were taken to the home of his son, Mr. C. S. Field in this county. The interm
2 CONC ent took place at Woodlawn Cemetery, with Masonic honors, Rev. Mr. Williams of Shuqualak officiating. Mr. Field was born in Virginia in 1832 and had reached his eightieth year. He moved to Noxubee county when about eighteen years 
2 CONC of age. He was a student at the University of Alabama at Tuscaloosa and entered the army from that state. He was married three times, first to Miss Weir, who lived only a brief time. His second wife was Miss Labuzan of Mobile, of 
2 CONC this union three children were born, Mr. Charles S. Field of Noxubee, Miss Liley Field of Mobile, and Mr. James Field now deceased. His last wife was Miss Eugenia Ferris, daughter of the late Eugene Ferris.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mr. Field was a gentleman of a special charm of manner, cultured, generous, and a type of the old-time hospitable southerner.
2 CONT * Reference: [https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/76472526/stanton-slaughter-field Find A Grave Memorial] - [http://www.geni.com/projects/SmartCopy/18783 SmartCopy]: ''Nov 7 2020, 22:07:32 UTC''
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Biography==
2 CONT '''Stanton Slaughter Field''' was born on April 1, 1835, in Culpeper, Virginia. His parents were [https://www.geni.com/profile-126964477 Henry Hill Field] and [https://www.geni.com/profile-34737737378 Dianna Field (Slaughter)]. He
2 CONC  was a Merchant.
2 CONT
2 CONT Stanton married [https://www.geni.com/profile-34742574624 Ellen M Labuzan] on July 19, 1860 in Mobile, Alabama, USA. Together they had the following children:
2 CONT # [https://www.geni.com/profile-34742574625 Charles Stanton Field, Jr]; 
2 CONT # [https://www.geni.com/profile-34742574627 Liley E. Field]; 
2 CONT # [https://www.geni.com/profile-34742574626 James Field]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Stanton married [https://www.geni.com/profile-34742574622 Liley E. Field] on December 17, 1856, in Noxubee County, Mississippi, USA. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Stanton married [https://www.geni.com/profile-34742574623 Eugenia Ferris] on October 18, 1871, in Noxubee County, Mississippi, USA. 
2 CONT
2 CONT He died on October 14, 1912, in Mobile, Mobile, Alabama, USA, and was buried after, in Shuqualak, Noxubee County, Mississippi, United States of America. 
2 CONT ----
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
