2 CONT
2 CONT At least one of the sons was born in Eufaula, Barbour County, Alabama - Stanton Slaughter Field was born in Virginia - Find-a-grave has Virginia, as well as his Military record. His father, Henry Hill Field owned a Plantation in C
2 CONC ulpeper, VA, and returned after the death of his wife with Stanton Slaughter Field. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Stanton Slaughter Field's father, Henry Hill Field, moved with his first wife, Diana Field (Slaughter) to Eufaula, Alabama, until her death, February 19, 1838, but she must've returned to Culpeper, Virginia for his birth He then m
2 CONC oved back to Culpeper County, Virginia for several years. He sold his home in Eufaula he shared with his first wife in 1842, but in the deed, Mr. Field Reserved Ten Square Feet of the lot, which contained the graves of his first w
2 CONC ife and child. He moved back with Mary Russell Field (Colvin) and family by 1848, where they lived until 1855 when they moved to Lockhart, Caldwell County, Texas. William Hill Field was a very prosperous Merchant and large Plantat
2 CONC ion owner in Texas until his death, his wife oversaw the business with managers until the oldest son, William Edward Field stepped into his merchant business when he was a teenager and continued to run this business until his reti
2 CONC rement. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Culpeper County, Virginia Records, Will Book P, pg. 253, Division of Slaves of James Colvin, Deceased, Among his Children. MARY Russell Field (Colvin), among his children, named in Will.
2 CONT
2 CONT Data from DAR Nt. no 443238, Miss Russell Lucille Colvin, a descendant of Daniel Colvin (H.J. Eckenrode, Virginia Rev.Soldiers, Richmond, 1912, pg. 80,107. Elizabeth Hansbrough was the daughter of James and Lettice Hansbrough (Sum
2 CONC ner) granddaughter of Peter Hansbrough of ”Coal Hill”, St. Elizabeth Hansbrough was the daughter of James and Lettice Hansbrough (Sumner) granddaughter of Peter Hansbrough of ”Coal Hill”, St. Mark’s Parish, Culpeper County, Virgin
2 CONC ia. 
2 CONT ----
2 CONT His mother was the daughter of Stanton Slaughter, High Sheriff of Culpeper County, Virginia, and his wife, Mrs. Slaughter (Pickett). His parents moved from Culpeper, Virginia to Eufaula, Alabama, his father returned to Culpeper Co
2 CONC unty, Virginia after September 14, 1829, when his mother passed away.
2 CONT
2 CONT The only information our family has about his brother, Henry Hill Field, Jr., was killed in the Civil War in 1866.
2 CONT
