1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ==Biography==
2 CONT '''Frances Field (Hill)''' was born on August 5, 1775 in Culpeper Co., VA, Culpeper County, Culpeper County, Virginia, United States. Her parents were [https://www.geni.com/profile-50752220 Col. Henry Hill] and [https://www.geni.c
2 CONC om/profile-50752223 Ann Hill (Powell)]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Frances married [https://www.geni.com/profile-34718939273 Henry Field of Culpeper County] on September 22, 1796 in Culpeper, Virginia. Together they had the following children:
2 CONT # [https://www.geni.com/profile-34742471713 Captain John Field]; 
2 CONT # [https://www.geni.com/profile-34742471714 Ann Powell Kelly (Field)]; 
2 CONT # [https://www.geni.com/profile-34742471718 Diana A Field]; 
2 CONT # [https://www.geni.com/profile-34742471716 Elizabeth Jane Field]; 
2 CONT # [https://www.geni.com/profile-126964477 Henry Hill Field]; 
2 CONT # [https://www.geni.com/profile-34742471717 William Hill Field]; 
2 CONT # [https://www.geni.com/profile-34742471721 Albert Francis Field]; 
2 CONT # [https://www.geni.com/profile-34742471720 Robert M. Field]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''References'''
2 CONT ---
2 CONT ''Lucie Field's trip to Virginia - documentation, and notes [http://www.luciefield.net/vafieldcem2.html Gravesites] The first '''tombstones they found were Frances Hill Field circa 1780-''' circa 1859 daughter of Col Henry Hill- w
2 CONC ife of Henry Field (1798-1850) the grandson of Capt Abraham Field (1695-1774) and the son of the famous Col. John Field (1726-1774) who was killed leading his regiment at the Battle of Point Pleasant, in what is now West Virginia,
2 CONC  during Braddock's War; October 10, 1774. Capt. Abraham was also the father of Keene Field (abt. 1774-1815) our first immigrant ancestor in Indiana for the Fields in that State. Keene Field was the brother of Pvt. Joseph Field (-1
2 CONC 807) and Pvt. Rueben Field (-1822) of the Lewis and Clark Expedition. At least the Field lineage had some ties with the Lewis & Clark Expedition, not related, so far, just participated. Lucie Field and her family also found the to
2 CONC mbstones of Diana Field (1840-1858) and Anna P. Kelly (Field) (1797-1856), daughters of Henry and Frances Field. Anna P was the wife of Col. John Payne Kelly (1789-1871) - another grave was of Frances "Fannie" Field Kelly Berry, w
2 CONC ife of John S. Berry. The graves of Frances Hill Field and Diana Field were side by side under a Juniper tree, the stones were in good condition. There were many depressions in the ground near the tombstones indicating other grave
2 CONC s. The land was at the crest of a hill that sloped east toward the Rappahannock River.  
2 CONT
2 CONT 1.      Nugent, Nell Marion. Cavaliers and Pioneers: Abstracts of Virginia Land Patents and Grants, Vol. 3, 1695-1732. Richmond: Virginia State Library, 1979. Page 256.
2 CONT
2 CONT 2.      Sparacio, Sam and Ruth Sparacio (comp), Virginia County Court Records, Deed Abstracts of Culpeper County, Virginia, 1790-1791 (Deed Book Q pg. 1-325), (The Antient Press, 1993), pg. 113. 
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT The miniature painting in a daguerreotype case of[https://www.geni.com/photo/view/6000000045461975961?album_type=photos_of_me&photo_id=6000000207462799867  Frances Field (HIll)] is one of 9 originals made for each of her children.
2 CONC  Only one has been located in Texas, 8 more should be available somewhere, in our ancestors closets, boxes or desks, if they survived. We hope they're not in resale or antique shops. The search is on for our ancestors portraits, d
2 CONC aguerreotype photos, 1849 and later , tin-type and ambro-type  photos (mid 1860's) CDV Cards mid 1860's - Check  and gold lockets they were popular for carrying a love ones photos as well. 
2 CONT Field Genealogy- Hill Genealogy " Lancaster From Virginia to Texas" by Mary Nixon Rogers pg 215, 216 7.Albert Frances Field 
2 CONT
2 CONT 1. John Field, Jr. was the son of Col. John Field and Anna Rogers Field (Clark)  _*Change_ was proven to be "not" to be the sister of Gen. George Rogers Clark and William Clark of "The Lewis and Clark Expedition") 
2 CONT
2 CONT Col. John Field was killed at the Battle of Point Pleasant, October 10, 1774 (DAR National Number 455884, Mary Nixon Rogers). Diana Field was the daughter of Henry Field, Jr. Patriot of Culpeper Co, Virginia; and a member of The H
2 CONC ouse of Burgesses. Attachments in Media.  
