1 NOTE {geni:about_me} https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/178000245/captain_john-field
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Biography==
2 CONT '''John Field, (CSA)''' was born on March 31, 1799, in Culpeper, Culpeper, Virginia, United States. His parents were [https://www.geni.com/profile-34718939273 Henry Field, of Culpeper County] and [https://www.geni.com/profile-3469
2 CONC 2402028 Frances Field (Hill)]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT John married [https://www.geni.com/profile-34766578746 Alice Kathryn Field (O'Bannon)] on April 29, 1823, in Shelby, Kentucky, USA, Shelby County, KY, United States. Together they had the following children:
2 CONT * [https://www.geni.com/profile-34766579432 James O'Bannon Field]; 
2 CONT * [https://www.geni.com/profile-34766579433 William Field]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT After the death of his first wife, he married his wife's cousin. 
2 CONT
2 CONT John married [https://www.geni.com/profile-34742471939 Mary Elizabeth Field] on April 24, 1827, in Shelby, Kentucky, USA. Together they had the following children:
2 CONT * [https://www.geni.com/profile-34742471945 Parmenas O'Bannon Field]; 
2 CONT * [https://www.geni.com/profile-34742471942 Robert Oliver Field]; 
2 CONT * [https://www.geni.com/profile-34742471946 Mildred Jane Field]; 
2 CONT * [https://www.geni.com/profile-34742471944 Diana A Field]; 
2 CONT * [https://www.geni.com/profile-34742471949 Albert Gallatin Field]; 
2 CONT * [https://www.geni.com/profile-34742471947 John M. Field]; 
2 CONT * [https://www.geni.com/profile-34742471943 Frances C. Field]; 
2 CONT * [https://www.geni.com/profile-34742471952 Stanton Slaughter Field Of Texas]; 
2 CONT * [https://www.geni.com/profile-34742471953 Thomas McGilton Field]; 
2 CONT * [https://www.geni.com/profile-34742471954 Pattie Irvin Field]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT He died on May 31, 1873, in Mission Valley, Victoria, Texas, United States, and was buried in the Power's Family Cemetery in Thomaston, DeWitt County, Texas, United States of America.[https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/178000245]
2 CONC
2 CONT ----
2 CONT Captain John Field moved to Kentucky in 1821 and was living in Warsaw, KY prior to the Mexican War. He served two terms in the Kentucky Legislature, the first term in Henry County, Gallatin, and Carroll Counties the latter. In 184
2 CONC 2 while serving a man named Mr. Morrow, who was a counterfeiter burned Captain Field’s home in retribution.
2 CONT
2 CONT Capt. John Field was head of a notable family from Kentucky, born in Culpeper County, VA He arrived in Mission Valley Victoria, Texas in 1852, bringing other family members. He brought his wife and eight children with him Kentucky
2 CONC . It was not long after the arrival of the Field family that John A. Emison, in 1853, married the eldest daughter, Miss Frances "Fannie" Emison (Field) Capt. John Field built a lovely home for his large family and was a successful
2 CONC  Rancher along with his son’s. 
2 CONT
2 CONT He was editor of a paper called the Warsaw Herald and was the first editor in the United States to hoist the flag for the annexation of Texas. Capt. The field was a surveyor for the city of Victoria in 1854 and was elected Sheriff
2 CONC  in 1857. His wife died in Mission Valley, Victoria, Texas Aug 16, 1857, his children lived with him until his death on May 31, 1873. His children took over his Ranch, which was adjoining his son-in-law's Col. John Ash Emison prop
2 CONC erty and daughter, Francis Emison Field.  
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Military'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Captain Field commanded a company of Kentucky volunteers under Zachary Taylor at Buena Vista in the Mexican American War. He enlisted in Marshall’s regiment Field’s Shanshorn’s and Bear’s Companies were ordered by General Taylor o
2 CONC n the mountains, brought on the Battle of Buena Vista. Captain John Field’s horse was shot out from under him in that battle, he was thirteen years old, his father sent him with a worker from the plantation to Alexandria to exchan
2 CONC ge two loads of wheat for salt, but he and the man were pressed into service until the war was over. Then when the Civil War broke out in his advanced age, he buckled on his armor and fought to protect Texas, serving in the Home G
2 CONC uards until the close of the Civil War. 
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT ----
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT * Surveyor for the city of Mission Valley, Victoria County, Texas  
2 CONT * Sheriff of the County
2 CONT * two terms in the Legislature of Kentucky
2 CONT * Mexican American War Rank of Lieutenant enlistment 
2 CONT * Civil War- Texas Calvary- Rank Captain- Capt. Edgar's Co., State Troops (Alamo City Guards); Capt. Gould's Co., State Troops (Clarksville Light Infantry); Capt. Graham's Co., Mounted Coast Guards, State Troops; Capt. Hampton's C
2 CONC o., State Troops (Victoria Blues); Capt. Killough's Co., Home Guards (Wheelock Home Guards) 
2 CONT ----
