1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ==Biography==
2 CONT '''Diana A Field''' was born on February 9, 1801, in Culpeper, Culpeper, Virginia, USA. Her parents were [https://www.geni.com/profile-34718939273 Henry Field, of Culpeper County] and [https://www.geni.com/profile-34692402028 Fran
2 CONC ces Field (Hill)]. Never married.
2 CONT
2 CONT She died on December 28, 1858, in Culpeper, Culpeper, Virginia, USA. She is buried beside her mother under the Juniper tree. 
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT ----
2 CONT ----
2 CONT '''References'''
2 CONT ---
2 CONT Lucie Field's trip to Virginia - documentation, and notes [http://www.luciefield.net/vafieldcem2.html Gravesites] The first tombstones they found were Frances Hill Field circa 1780- circa 1859 daughter of Col Henry Hill- wife of H
2 CONC enry Field (1798-1850) the grandson of Capt Abraham Field (1695-1774) and the son of the famous Col. John Field (1726-1774) who was killed leading his regiment at the Battle of Point Pleasant, in what is now West Virginia, during 
2 CONC Braddock's War; October 10, 1774. Capt. Abraham was also the father of Keene Field (abt. 1774-1815) our first immigrant ancestor in Indiana for the Fields in that State. Keene Field was the brother of Pvt. Joseph Field (-1807) and
2 CONC  Pvt. Rueben Field (-1822) of the Lewis and Clark Expedition. At least the Field lineage had some ties with the Lewis & Clark Expedition, not related, so far, just participated.  Lucie Field and her family also found the tombstone
2 CONC s of Diana Field (1840-1858) and  Anna P. Kelly (Field) (1797-1856), daughters of Henry and Frances Field.  Anna P was the wife of Col. John Payne Kelly (1789-1871) - another grave was of Frances Fannie Field Kelly Berry, wife of 
2 CONC John S. Berry. 
2 CONT ----
2 CONT ---- '''The graves of Frances Hill Field and Diana Field were side by side under a Juniper tree, the stones were in good condition. There were many depressions in the ground near the tombstones indicating other graves. The land wa
2 CONC s at the crest of a hill that sloped east toward the Rappahannock River.''' 
2 CONT
2 CONT 1.      Nugent, Nell Marion. Cavaliers and Pioneers: Abstracts of Virginia Land Patents and Grants, Vol. 3, 1695-1732. Richmond: Virginia State Library, 1979. Page 256.
2 CONT
2 CONT 2.      Sparacio, Sam and Ruth Sparacio (comp), Virginia County Court Records, Deed Abstracts of Culpeper County, Virginia, 1790-1791 (Deed Book Q pg. 1-325), (The Antient Press, 1993), pg. 113. 
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT Field Genealogy- Hill Genealogy " Lancaster From Virginia to Texas" by Mary Nixon Rogers pg 215, 216 7.Albert Frances Field 
2 CONT
2 CONT 1. John Field, Jr. was the son of Col. John Field and Anna Rogers Field (Clark)  _*Change_ was proven to be "not" to be the sister of Gen. George Rogers Clark and William Clark of "The Lewis and Clark Expedition") 
2 CONT
2 CONT Col. John Field was killed at the Battle of Point Pleasant, October 10, 1774 (DAR National Number 455884, Mary Nixon Rogers). Diana Field was the daughter of Henry Field, Jr. Patriot of Culpeper Co, Virginia; and a member of The H
2 CONC ouse of Burgesses. Attachments in Media.  
