1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ==Biography==
2 CONT '''Lucy Hudson''' was born between 1770 and 1772 in Buffalo Creek, Prince Edward County, Virginia, United States. Her parents were [https://www.geni.com/profile-34682558139 Caleb Baker, II] and [https://www.geni.com/profile-346825
2 CONC 58041 Catherine Campbell Baker]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Lucy married [https://www.geni.com/profile-34688527390 Captain John Hudson] on June 5, 1790 in Prince Edward County, Virginia, United States. Together they had the following children: 
2 CONT # [https://www.geni.com/profile-128673397 Elizabeth D Flood];  
2 CONT # [https://www.geni.com/profile-34715964894 Caleb Baker Hudson]; 
2 CONT # [https://www.geni.com/profile-34715964889 John Hudson]; 
2 CONT # [https://www.geni.com/profile-34715964893 Nathaniel Hudson]; 
2 CONT # [https://www.geni.com/profile-34715964803 Lucy Ann Lancaster (Hudson)]; 
2 CONT # [https://www.geni.com/profile-34715964890 William Hudson].  All children of Lucy Hudson (Baker) were named in her father's probated Will 
2 CONT
2 CONT She died before November 1822 and buried in Prince Edward County, Virginia, United States. 
2 CONT ----
2 CONT '''Lucy Hudson (Baker) ''' was born in Buffalo Creek, Prince Edward County, VA to Caleb Baker, Jr and wife Catherine Campbell Baker (Hognett). She married Captain John Hudson on June 3, 1790. 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Children'''
2 CONT
2 CONT 1. Elizabeth D. Flood (Hudson) Born circa 1791, Prince Edward County, Virginia; died before July 19, 1825; married John Flood, December 20, 1809, Prince Edward County, VA. Two daughter’s born of this marriage: Sarah Y. Flood, Lucy
2 CONC  B. Flood. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 2. Caleb Baker Hudson, born circa 1793, Prince Edward County, Virginia; married Ann Martha. Children born of this marriage 1) Mary Ann Harris (Hudson), born March 11, 1817, married Carter O. Harris, Sept. 17, 1835, Jackson County,
2 CONC  Alabama 2) John Hudson died young from accidental shooting about age 20-21’ buried in Holland Cemetery, Maynard’s Cove, 8mi Nth of Scottsboro, Ala.
2 CONT
2 CONT 3. John Hudson, Jr., born circa 1795 married 1) Sarah H._____? 2) a widow, Mrs Elizabeth B.  Martin ( Davis), he died soon after second marriage. Then, Elizabeth Hudson, the widow married Caleb Hognett Baker of Knoxville, TN  ( Co
2 CONC usin of her late husband, John Hudson’s Cousin) 
2 CONT
2 CONT 4. Nathaniel Hudson, born circa 1798 was living in Jackson County, Alabama by October 1824, served in the Commission Appointed to Select New Site For County Seat of Jackson County, Alabama in *1859. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 5. Lucy Ann Lancaster (Hudson) born June 3, 1803, Prince Edward County, Virginia; died Feb. 15, 1867 Manor, TX; Buried at the Rector Cemetery, one Mi NW of Manor. Married 1) Josiah Lancaster Early 1820’s in TN, 2) Married Thompson
2 CONC  Mason Rector, ATTY, Judge, 1831 in Jackson County, AL 
2 CONT
2 CONT 6. William Hudson, born circa 1804 in Prince Edward County, VA, was still a minor in 1825.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''References''' Lancaster From Virginia To Texas complied by Mary Nixon Rogers
2 CONT
2 CONT June 3, 1790, Marriage Bond, Prince Edward County,  lineage. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Caleb Baker, Jr Will, Probated in Prince Edward County, it mentions his daughter Lucy Hudson’s children. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Lucy Hudson (Baker)  great-grandfather was James Baker the Gunsmith and grandfather, Caleb Baker, Sr.  Note: * Jackson County, Alabama in 1859, Civil War about to heat up, to think many branches of this lineage settled in Alabama 
2 CONC and Georgia. These States were just about wiped out, many records were burned.)  The younger son’s of The Hudson lineage migrated farther  South to seek their fortunes. My lineage did migrate to Texas before the onset of the War. 
2 CONC This is how many of the records were saved for this lineage, but we do have a few gaps. We preserved our Family Bible, I thank my Aunt Mary Nixon Rogers an Uncle Jake Rogers for their dedication and work that is assisting us today
2 CONC  to unravel the Hudson lineage.
2 CONT
2 CONT Baker Genealogy 
2 CONT
2 CONT About Robert Baker, the Gunsmith
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert Baker, Sr. was granted land and a gun bearing mill for manufacturing of firearms, the Pennsylvania rifle, by the King of England. Robert Baker was the first man in recorded history to design and manufacture the PA rifle. At
2 CONC  his death his son Caleb kept up the tradition and later used the rifle to great effect in the American Revolution. The rifle was later known as the "Hog Rifle" and the "Kentucky Rifle" in Daniel Boone's day.
2 CONT
2 CONT They were commissioned by the King of England to make firearms for the Colonies. Later the Bakers would join the Colonies against England in the Revolutionary War.
2 CONT
2 CONT Three of Robert's sons, Samuel, Robert and Douglas married sisters, Elizabeth, Mary and Jane Thompson.
2 CONT
2 CONT Married: Abt 1720 in Ashe County,North Carolina 1
2 CONT
2 CONT Children
2 CONT
2 CONT Bolin (Andrew W. Sr. And Mary Agnes Bolling) BAKER b: 4 MAY 1738 in Ashe County, North Carolina
2 CONT
2 CONT Capt. John Renta (Andrew W. Sr. And Mary Agnes Bolling) BAKER b: 17 OCT 1735 in Pine Mountain, Wilkes County, North Carolina
2 CONT
2 CONT Abednego (Andrew W. Sr. And Mary Agnes Bolling) BAKER b: 1741 in Culpepper County, Virginia
2 CONT
2 CONT Leonard (Andrew W. Sr. And Mary Agnes Bolling) BAKER b: 1745 in Culpeper County,Virginia
2 CONT
2 CONT James (Andrew W. Sr. And Mary Agnes Bolling) BAKER b: 1753 in Ash County,North Carolina
2 CONT
2 CONT Cuthbert (Andrew W. Sr. And Mary Agnes Bolling) BAKER b: 1746 in Lunenburg,Virginia
2 CONT
2 CONT Richard (Andrew W. Sr. And Mary Agnes Bolling) BAKER b: 1753 in Lunenburg,Virginia
2 CONT
2 CONT Andrew W. Jr. (Andrew W. Sr. And Mary Agnes Bolling) BAKER b: 1749 in Grayson County,Virginia
2 CONT
2 CONT William (Andrew W. Sr. And Mary Agnes Bolling) BAKER b: 1750 in Virginia
2 CONT
2 CONT Morris (Andrew W. Sr. And Mary Agnes Bolling) BAKER b: 1750 in Wilkes County,North Carolina
2 CONT
2 CONT Joseph (Andrew W. Sr. And Mary Agnes Bolling) BAKER b: 1751 in Orange County,North Carolina
2 CONT
2 CONT Capt. George F. (Andrew W. Sr. And Mary Agnes Bolling) BAKER b: 14 OCT 1759 in Guilford County,North Carolina
2 CONT
2 CONT Ellendor "Nellie" (Andrew W. Sr. And Mary Agnes Bolling) BAKER b: 24 DEC 1765 in Wilkes County,North Carolina
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert and his brother Samuel grew up in or around Boston, MA. His father and probably his grandfather were gunsmiths by trade. Both he and Sam were keenly interested in the gun making trade which they probably learned from their 
2 CONC father and possible other gunsmiths in the area. Robert’s occupation as an adult was listed as Rifle maker.
2 CONT
2 CONT As young men the two brothers moved to PA and set up a gunmaking business in Lancaster Co . It is believed that he and his brother Sam were the first gunmakers in Lancaster Co, PA.and that they obtained a grant from the King of En
2 CONC gland to manufacture guns for the colonies
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert met a young lady named Susan Parker /Packer. Susan lived in an older community near the coast. (The town was established in 1682 as Philadelphia) Susan like her parents and neighbors were of the Quakers Religion. After a sh
2 CONC ort courtship, they got married in 1685. Robert was 25 and Susan was 21 years old. They had seven children over the next fourteen years. The boys were named Robert Jr., Douglas, Caleb, Andrew and Samuel and the girls Mary and May.
2 CONC  Then in 1713, thirteen years later another daughter, Elizabeth was born.
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert and Samuel had developed a reputation for quality in the gun manufacturing business. It was about this time,early 1700′s, that some reseachers report that Robert Baker was ordered to go to England to make guns for King Will
2 CONC iam, and to teach their gunsmiths their tecnique of cutting rifling in gun barrels.(England was involved in two wars with France known as King Williams War and Queen Anne’s War.) He probably took his family with him to England. Af
2 CONC ter a stay of eight to ten years, he returned to America with a grant to make firearms for the Colonies. (Later the Bakers would join the Colonies against England in the Revolutionary War.)
2 CONT
2 CONT When Robert returned to the Colonies, he moved his family and settled in Conestoga Township, Chester County, PA. He bought 500 acres of land on the Susquehanna River from Col. John French in 1717. This land was located one mile fr
2 CONC om the junction of Pequea Creek and the Susquehanna River. Two of his sons Samuel and Caleb, were interested in gun manufacturing and joined with their father and uncle in the business.
2 CONT
2 CONT On August 15, 1719, Robert Baker had Jacob Taylor, Surveyor, with permission from William Penn, and Lancaster Co layout a site for the erection of a gun boring mill at the mouth of Pequea Creek. They developed and produced the Ame
2 CONC rican Long Rifle later known as the” Kentucky Rifle”. This rifle was developed from Jaeger rifle which was a short stocky flintlock rifle designed for hunting by the well-to-do in the fields of Europe. The American frontier hunter
2 CONC  needed a practical rifle to put food on the table and to protect their families. They elongated the barrel and reduced the caliber. The longer barrels increased the accuracy and gave greater muzzle velocity. The smaller bore gave
2 CONC  more bullets from a pound of lead. The typical American Rifle was a slender full stock flintlock with a 40-inch barrel and of about 50 calibre. The guns barrels were rifled for accuracy, as were the earlier European rifles.
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert, his sons Samuel and Caleb (who were about 25 and 29 years old) and a cousin, John, joined together to run the mill. Records indicate that they were in operation for the next nine years. (Pequea creek originates in the hill
2 CONC s about 20 miles east of the town of Lancaster, runs southwest and empties in the Susquehanna River.)
2 CONT
2 CONT In February 1721, iron ore deposits were located on the West bank of the Sesquehanna River. It is believed that this was one of the sources of iron to make the Baker gun barrels. A Philip Syng claimed 200 acres of this land in 172
2 CONC 2. Robert Baker and James McClean filed a complaint before Francis Worley Esq., a JP for Chester County. A warrant was filed to have him punished if he tried to survey this land in question. This stopped his claim.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Baker family grew up in a neighborhood of German Immigrants, known as Pennsylvania Dutch, who settled in that area in the early 1700′s. It is noted that most of Robert Baker’s neighbors seem to have German names. Caleb Baker a
2 CONC lways signed his name Beaker and possibly spoke with a German accent.
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert died in September, 1728. He left no will and his son Caleb was appointed an administrator of his estate, dated September 13, 1728. Along with the original papers at the Register’s Office at the Court House in Lancaster Coun
2 CONC ty is the Administrators Bond and inventory signed by Joseph Higginbotham and Tobias Hendricks and witnessed by Douglas Baker. Among the creditors were the names of Caleb Baker, Robert Baker Jr. and Douglas Baker. Caleb being part
2 CONC  owner of the business, took over and operated the mill until1741, when he sold out to Jacob Godin.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1741-1742 Caleb, his immediate family and one or more brothers (Douglas and Robert Jr.) moved from Lancaster Co., Pa. and bought land and settled on what was then called “The Backwoods” in Amelia Co., Virginia on Buffalo Creek.
2 CONT
2 CONT We have a copy of Caleb’s will dated 24 Nov 1750 with codicil dated 6 Feb 1754 filed in Prince Edward Co. VA. (He was 64 years old.) He named his wife Martha and sons Samuel and Henry as Executors of his will. To his sons, he left
2 CONC  463 acres to Samuel, 400 acres to Henry, 200 acres to Abraham, 307 acres to Caleb—all on Spring creek. He designated 305 acres on Buffalo Creek to be sold to help legacies. He left his four daughters 20 pounds each, except Easter
2 CONC  got only one shilling.
2 CONT
2 CONT We have a copy of Martha’s will dated May 8, 1759, filed in Prince Edward Co.,VA. She left her son Caleb the following: two negros, three horses and a large bay mare, two cows with calfs, all meat, and all the working tools for us
2 CONC e on the plantation. To her son Samuel she left a brown mare, a colt, and a side-saddle. The executors of her will were Samuel and Caleb. She signed the will with her mark.
2 CONT
2 CONT We also have a copy of Douglas Baker’s will dated Sept 16, 1765 and filed in Prince Edward Co.,VA ( He was 77 years old). He left his daughter, Jean, one Negro boy and one mare and the balance of the estate to his wife, Jane. The 
2 CONC will was witnessed by Samuel and Andrew Baker and George Shilliday. He signed the will with his mark.
2 CONT * This family was originally of  The Friends Society Religion, and they were Gunsmiths, Susan Packer's family were Quaker. We don't have records past Susan Packer for the Meeting notes. 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''References'''
2 CONT
2 CONT * Probated Will in Media, documents and photos for viewing. Caleb Baker named Lucy Hudson (Baker)'s children in his will as she was already deceased.. 
2 CONT
