1 OCCU Professor of Art, Sculptor, Southwestern University, at Georgetown.
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ==Biography==
2 CONT '''Robert Lee Lancaster, II Ph.D.''' was born on September 3, 1924 in San Marcos, Hays County, Texas, USA. His parents were [https://www.geni.com/profile-34764097630 Robert Lee Lancaster, I] and [https://www.geni.com/profile-34764
2 CONC 098700 Louise Lancaster]. He was a Professor of Art, Sculptor, Southwestern University, at Georgetown.. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert married [https://www.geni.com/profile-34764098786 Wanda Beatrice Lancaster] on January 24, 1948 in San Marcos, Texas, USA. Together, they adopted the following children:
2 CONT [https://www.geni.com/profile-34764098788 Laura Lancaster]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT He died on May 1, 1974, in Georgetown, Williamson County, Texas, USA, from a shotgun wound to the base of the skull, reported as self-inflicted, and was buried in San Marcos Cemetery, San Marcos, Hays County, Texas, USA. 
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT [https://marineraiderassociation.org/lancaster-robert-l-jr/ Marine Raider Robert Lee Lancaster, Jr.]
2 CONT LANCASTER, ROBERT L., JR.
2 CONT May 1, 1974
2 CONT
2 CONT Funeral services for Robert L. Lancaster, 49, well-known sculptor, painter, educator, designer, consultant in art and architecture, and chairman of the art department in the School of Fine Arts at Southwestern University, who died
2 CONC  at his home on May 1 [1974], were held in Lois Perkins Chapel at Southwestern University at 2:30 p.m. on Thursday, May 2.
2 CONT
2 CONT Officiating at the service were The Rev. Schnaufer, Vicar of Grace Episcopal Church in Georgetown, and The Rev. Konrad Kelley of Austin. Davis Funeral Home in Georgetown was in charge. Graveside service was held in San Marcos at 4
2 CONC :45 p.m. at the City Cemetery.
2 CONT
2 CONT Pallbearers were Dr. Jeff Campbell, Claude Kennard and Gus Farmer, all of Southwestern University; Rodney Montgomery of Georgetown; Kenneth McGee, Waco; and Mel Fowler, Austin.
2 CONT
2 CONT Memorials may be made to the Southwestern University Art Department Scholarship Fund or to the Stonehaven Center Memorial Fund in Georgetown.
2 CONT
2 CONT Survivors include his wife, the former Wanda Hayes, and a daughter, Laura Lancaster, both of Georgetown; his mother, Mrs. Louise Lancaster of San Marcos, San Antonio and Dallas; and a sister, Miss Jane Lancaster of Dallas.
2 CONT
2 CONT In paying tribute to Lancaster’s memory, Dr. Durwood Fleming, president of Southwestern University, said: “He was an unusually gifted person who excited his students in the appreciation and performance of creative art. He was a va
2 CONC lued friend and colleague and a fine, Christian gentleman.”
2 CONT
2 CONT A memorial service was held in Lois Perkins Chapel at Southwestern University at 6:30 p.m. on Wednesday, May 1, with the Rev. Farley Snell, University Chaplain, and Dr. Durwood Fleming, University President, participating.
2 CONT
2 CONT Joining the faculty of the School of Fine Arts at Southwestern University in 1959, Lancaster formerly served on the faculties of San Angelo College, West Virginia University, University of New Mexico, and Arkansas State Teachers C
2 CONC ollege.
2 CONT
2 CONT A graduate of the University of Texas at Austin, and the University of New Mexico with the Bachelor of Fine Arts and the Master of Arts degrees, Lancaster’s major areas of emphasis were sculpture and painting.
2 CONT
2 CONT Lancaster’s works have been shown in exhibitions in Texas, Arkansas, Ohio, New Mexico, Arizona, West Virginia, Michigan, and are also represented in many cities in private collections throughout the United States. He was the recip
2 CONC ient of prizes and awards in area, regional, and national exhibitions, and was presented in 50 one-and two-man shows. Lancaster was a frequent judge for exhibitions and filled numerous speaking engagements.
2 CONT
2 CONT Lancaster’s commissions and installations, included mosaics in San Angelo and at San Angelo College; a limestone carving for the San Antonio Housing Authority, the Gold Age Center; a limestone carving in Fredericksburg and one for
2 CONC  the Alma Thomas Fine Arts Center at Southwestern University; a carved wood entrance screen for a residence in Midland; statuary for a garden in Waco; hammered copper figure for the Landrum Mall at Southwestern University; and num
2 CONC erous other commissions.
2 CONT
2 CONT Named one of the eight artists whose works were included in a two-week presentation at the Stagecoach Gallery in Houston, Lancaster’s six sculptures exhibited included limestone, cedar, and mesquite.
2 CONT
2 CONT Lancaster participated in the International Sculpture Symposium in Long Beach, California (1965), and the International Congress on Architecture, Religion and the Visual Arts in New York (1967).
2 CONT
2 CONT A native of San Marcos, Lancaster served for three years (1943-1946) with the United States Marine Corps, with 28 months in the South Pacific Theater. He was awarded the Letter of Commendation and the Purple Heart in the service o
2 CONC f his country.
2 CONT
2 CONT Active in various areas, Lancaster was listed in WHO’S WHO IN THE SOUTH AND SOUTHWEST WITH NOTABLES IN MEXICO and held membership in the National Trust for Historic Preservation, Texas Fine Arts Association, Texas Society of Sculp
2 CONC tors, Grace Episcopal Church, American Association of University Professors, as well as art and education associations on the local, state and national levels. He was recently named a member of the Brotherhood of St. Andrews.
2 CONT « FAIRCHILD, JOHN R. JOYCE, JAMES C. »
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT ----
2 CONT '''References'''
2 CONT Death Certificate [https://www.geni.com/documents/view?doc_id=6000000152754594831]
2 CONT
2 CONT [https://www.southwestern.edu/giving/ways-to-give/endowed-funds/ The Bob Lancaster Award- SW University Endowment Fund]
2 CONT
2 CONT Established in 2019 by Laura Lancaster Faulk ’78 and Wanda Lancaster to provide a scholarship award to the outstanding graduating majors in studio art in spring semesters.
2 CONT
2 CONT Note: 
2 CONT Lancaster MTHFR 677T Dual Mutation Lancaster lineage carries the mutation. 
2 CONT
